window.PAPER_DATA={"today": 7, "meta": {"1": {"date": "Tuesday 6 October 2026", "short": "Tue 6 Oct", "longread": null}, "2": {"date": "Wednesday 7 October 2026", "short": "Wed 7 Oct", "longread": 2}, "3": {"date": "Thursday 8 October 2026", "short": "Thu 8 Oct", "longread": 3}, "4": {"date": "Friday 9 October 2026", "short": "Fri 9 Oct", "longread": 4}, "5": {"date": "Saturday 10 October 2026", "short": "Sat 10 Oct", "longread": 5}, "6": {"date": "Sunday 11 October 2026", "short": "Sun 11 Oct", "longread": 6}, "7": {"date": "Monday 12 October 2026", "short": "Mon 12 Oct", "longread": 7}}, "editions": {"1": {"title": "Morning paper, Tuesday 6 October 2026", "fact": "Five stories on the front page, three short beats below", "intro": "A sample of the new format. Facts are from sources I read in full; anything resting on one outlet or on a vendor's own numbers is marked. Nothing here is a stock tip.", "groups": [{"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "IRDAI wants to cap what insurers pay distributors", "kicker": "Your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "IRDAI's paper \"Recalibrating Economics of Insurance Distribution\" proposes a company-level expense-of-management cap of 12.5% of gross direct premium for life insurers, and for general insurers a cut from 30% to 20%, phased over five years. The case rests on sampled data: distributor remuneration for a group of corporate agents rose 125% (₹9,580 cr to ₹21,600 cr, FY23 to FY25) while new business premium rose 28%. General insurance broker commissions rose 173% against 37% growth in premium routed. Banks tied to several insurers averaged a 33% payout, up to 72%, against 13% for single-tie-up banks. Group credit life payouts reached up to 45% in FY25, from about 5%."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Bank and corporate-agent channels, and broker commissions, are the economics Probus sits on. If the expense cap lands, payout structures get renegotiated, and aggregator take rates are in the frame. It is a consultation paper, so nothing is final."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/banking/irdai-issues-consultation-paper-on-distribution-reforms-article-14036664.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol, 23 Sep</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Banks' deposit growth is flattered by a window that has closed", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "RBI's concessional swap window for FCNR(B) deposits closed on 31 August, and banks mobilised nearly $133 bn through it. HDFC Bank alone took $11.5 bn, about 8.6% of the inflows. Yes Bank shows how large the effect can be: reported deposit growth of 19.5% year on year is 6.5% once FCNR(B) is removed, and loan growth of 23.8% is 3.3% on the same basis. System credit grew 18.1% and deposits 17.3% as of 15 September (RBI data, via Business Standard). Axis Securities warns of margin pressure from the higher cost of funds."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Headline growth at banks in Q2 results season overstates the underlying trend, and the cost of those deposits shows up later in margins. Read adjusted numbers, not reported ones."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/deposit-growth-gets-a-leg-up-for-banks-in-q2-due-to-fcnr-b-mobilisation-126100400354_1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Business Standard, 4 Oct</a> / <a href=\"https://www.financialexpress.com/business/industry-bank-q2-updates-hdfc-bank-small-finance-banks-bajaj-finance-lead-credit-growth-4354049/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Financial Express, 5 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Foreign investors sold ₹35,861 cr of equities in September; domestic funds are absorbing it", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "NSDL data puts September FPI equity flow at -₹35,861 cr. By trade date it is closer to -₹45,093 cr, because ₹9,232 cr of 30 September trades were booked in October. FPIs are down ₹2,69,534 cr in 2026 so far. On 5 October, one aggregator reports FIIs net sold ₹4,699 cr while DIIs bought ₹5,182 cr: a seven-session FII sell streak against an eleven-session DII buy streak, and Nifty down 2.53% over five sessions. The 5 October figures come from that single aggregator and I could not cross-check them."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the flow behind the Nifty's drop, and a watch item before Wednesday's RBI decision. It is a description of flows, not a signal to trade."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://paisesamjho.com/fpi-selling-september-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Paisa Samjho, citing PTI/Times of India</a> / <a href=\"https://flashfinance.news/market-intelligence/fii-dii-flows/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Flash Finance (single source for the 5 Oct detail)</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Google and OpenAI now price new models at $2 per million input tokens", "kicker": "Your side projects"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Prices below are per million tokens, from the vendors' own posts and tables. Benchmark claims in those tables are vendor-reported and not independently tested."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The price of a prototype that calls a top model just fell by a factor of five against GPT-6 Astra. Cached input at $0.10 on GPT-6.1 Sol makes long-context agents cheap to test."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.eweek.com/news/google-gemini-4-argon-gpt-6-astra/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">eWeek on Gemini 4 Argon</a> / <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/claude-opus-5-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic, Opus 5.5</a> / <a href=\"https://community.openai.com/t/gpt-6-1-sol-in-the-api-a-meaningful-step-up-in-cost-performance/1402388/7\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAI Developer Community, GPT-6.1 Sol</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Price per 1M tokens, input / output (vendor-stated)AttributeGemini 4 Argon1GPT-6.1 Sol2Claude Opus 5.53GPT-6 Astra4Input / output$2 / $10 (introductory, $4 / $20 later)$2 / $10, cached input $0.10$4 / $20, cache reads $0.20$10 / $50"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Gemini 4 Argon is rolling out in stages and leads 14 of 19 rows in Google's benchmark table. Opus 5.5 posts 66.4% on Terminal-Bench 4.0 against 57.9% for GPT-6 Astra, in Anthropic's table."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Gemini 4 Argon launch graphic with a large number 4 on a blue gradient", "caption": "Gemini 4 Argon launch graphic. Credit: Google, via eWeek.", "img": "photos/d1-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Indian tech funding is up 7% on fewer, bigger rounds", "kicker": "Startups"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Tracxn counts $10.3 bn raised in the first nine months of 2026, up 7%, across 1,134 rounds, down 38%. Seed funding fell 37% to $698 M, first-time funded companies fell 30% to 338, and Series A and later rounds fell 23% to 409. There were 18 rounds of $100 M or more. AI infrastructure was the largest theme at $1.2 bn, and enterprise infrastructure rose 436% to $1.6 bn. Six new unicorns had raised about $101 M on average before their unicorn round, and the median path from Series A to unicorn was 4.9 years."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If you build, the seed market is the hard one: money is concentrating in later stages and in infrastructure, not in first cheques."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://cxotoday.com/others/tracxn-9m-2026-report-india-tech-funding-rises-7-to-10-3b-despite-38-drop-in-deal-volume/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Tracxn 9M 2026 via CXOToday, 24 Sep</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Below the fold: Finance goldmine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Peak XV cut its Honasa stake from 14.80% to 11.50% in one day", "kicker": "Smart money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "On 30 September Peak XV sold 1,07,55,495 shares of Honasa Consumer, taking its holding from 14.80% to 11.50%. Sequoia Capital Global Growth Fund III sold 25,02,289 shares (3.44% to 2.68%), and Redwood Trust sold 75,549. The stock was up 8.30% over the day per ScanX, so the market absorbed the supply. Single source for the share counts."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A venture investor exiting after the IPO lock-in is a supply event for consumer-brand listings. Watchlist only."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://scanx.trade/stock-market-news/companies/sequoia-capital-sells-25-lakh-shares-honasa-consumer/52748968\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ScanX, from SEBI Reg 29(2) filing dated 2 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Steel capex is the visible catalyst", "kicker": "Capacity expansion"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Tata Steel is adding 5 mtpa at NINL in Kalinganagar, about ₹40,000 cr. Shyam Metalics signed an MoU to invest ₹50,000 cr in a 9 mtpa plant in Chandrapur, Maharashtra. An MoU is intent, not a commissioned plant."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Large announced capex is a lead indicator for banks with infrastructure books and for steel-linked suppliers."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://money.rediff.com/amp/news/market/tata-steel-expands-ninl-capacity-to-5-mtpa-in-kalinganagar/55633120261005\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Rediff/PTI on Tata Steel NINL</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/shyam-metalics-energy-to-invest-50000-cr-in-9-mtpa-steel-plant-in-maharashtra/article71547684.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">BusinessLine on Shyam Metalics</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "RBI decides on Wednesday; the poll leans to a hike", "kicker": "Policy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "In a Reuters poll, 35 of 61 economists expect a 25 basis point increase to 5.50%. That leaves 26 expecting something else. The decision is on 7 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It sets the tone for the bank and NBFC spotlight below."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.indiatoday.in/business/story/rbi-mpc-october-2026-repo-rate-5-50-hike-experts-expect-3009950-2026-10-05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">India Today, 5 Oct (Reuters poll)</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Sector spotlight, days 1 and 2: banks and NBFCs, and insurance", "kicker": "Rotates every two days"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Banks and NBFCs: HDFC Bank's gross advances rose 16% to ₹32,135 bn, its best sequential loan growth in nine quarters, with deposits up 18.8%; Anup Bagchi becomes MD and CEO on 27 October. Bajaj Finance is raising ₹17,500 cr (₹11,700 cr QIP plus ₹5,800 cr promoter warrants) and AUM grew 26.5% to ₹5,848 bn. AU Small Finance Bank deposits grew 28.6% and advances 27.9%. Insurance: life new business premium rose 33.07% in August to ₹41,198 cr, and 20.4% over April to August to ₹1.97 lakh cr. Retail-weighted premium rose about 13.9%, which is the number closer to underlying retail demand."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Both sit under the same two stories above: deposit costs and distribution rules."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.financialexpress.com/business/industry-bank-q2-updates-hdfc-bank-small-finance-banks-bajaj-finance-lead-credit-growth-4354049/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Financial Express, 5 Oct</a> / <a href=\"https://www.capitalmarket.com/markets/news/insurance-news/life-insurance-companies-record-33-07-surge-in-new-business-premium-in-august/1731533\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Capital Market, 8 Sep</a>"}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Contrarian positioningNothing evidence-backed today. I would rather say that than stretch a view."}]}, {"heading": "Below the fold: Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Simple Energy's $180 M round is most of India's weekly funding", "kicker": "Funding landscape"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Indian startups raised $233.6 M across 16 deals that week. Simple Energy's $180 M Series C is 77% of it. The other 15 deals add up to about $53.6 M, which is the better read of the typical week."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Weekly totals mislead when one deal dominates. The rest of the market raised about $53.6 M."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/buzz/from-simple-energy-to-arivihan-indian-startups-raised-over-233-mn-this-week/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42, 28 Sep to 2 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "A 19-year-old raised $11 M to sell a $3,499 personal-AI computer", "kicker": "New business model"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Ghost's founder Zain Javaid raised an $11 M seed led by a16z for Core, a $3,499 computer with an Nvidia RTX Pro 4000 SFF Blackwell GPU. The price and funding are from TechCrunch's report; I have not seen sales figures."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A hardware answer to a software question: local AI for people who do not want their data in someone's cloud. A useful reference for what investors will back at seed."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/05/at-19-ghost-founder-raises-11-million-to-build-a-3499-computer-for-your-personal-ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch, 5 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Four young people in dark Ghost hoodies sitting on a sofa", "caption": "The Ghost team. Image credit: Ghost, via TechCrunch.", "img": "photos/d1-g2.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Etched is fielding funding offers at a $40 B valuation", "kicker": "Ecosystem move"}, {"t": "p", "html": "TechCrunch reports, citing sources, that Etched has received funding offers at a valuation of $40 B. Nothing is signed."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> AI chip startups are being valued like platforms. Sources say so, and it is not a closed round."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/05/etched-fields-funding-offers-at-40b-valuation-sources-say/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch, 5 Oct</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Below the fold: AI race", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Developers say cached-input pricing is the real news on GPT-6.1 Sol", "kicker": "Signal from Hacker News"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The top comment says cached input pricing is the real news; codecentral puts the release on 29 September and OpenAI's forum thread carries the $0.10 cached input price, half the earlier $0.20. Others in the thread argue Opus 5.5 is better at hard coding. That is opinion, not a test result."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It echoes the front-page point: for agent builders, cache cost matters more than headline price."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://news.ycombinator.com/item?id=49896586\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hacker News thread, 362 points</a> / <a href=\"https://codecentral.io/gpt-6-1-sol-vs-gpt-6-sol/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Codecentral on the release</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Product Hunt's week: agent infrastructure and model routing", "kicker": "Launches"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The leaderboard for the week of 5 October has Spira Maxima, FastRouter.ai, which routes across 200+ LLMs, and Opengeni, agent infrastructure, near the top."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Routing across models is becoming a product category, which fits falling prices."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.producthunt.com/leaderboard/weekly/2026/41\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Product Hunt weekly leaderboard</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Anthropic will run Claude inference in India", "kicker": "Ecosystem"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Per the Times of India, Anthropic is launching in-country Claude inference in India via AWS."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> In-country inference matters for regulated Indian customers, including insurance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/anthropic-launches-in-country-claude-ai-inference-in-india-via-aws/articleshow/134677000.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Times of India, 5 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Reported, not confirmedSeveral outlets report, citing a WSJ story, that OpenAI cancelled an October GPT-6.1 Astra release over safety concerns. I found no primary confirmation, only secondary sites, so I am not treating it as fact."}]}]}, "2": {"title": "Morning paper, Wednesday 7 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 2 of the trial. Built on spec v2.", "intro": "RBI decides on rates at 10 AM today, the insurance commission fight is getting louder, and two open-weight frontier models are due this month.", "groups": [{"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "RBI decides today, and the street expects its first hike since February 2023", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Economists expect the MPC under Governor Sanjay Malhotra to raise the repo rate by 25 basis points, from 5.25% to 5.50%. The rate has sat at 5.25% for nearly ten months after 125 bp of cuts in 2025. A hike is widely priced in. Less certain is whether the RBI also changes its stance. NDTV Profit lists rising inflation, higher crude, a weak rupee and a global shift to higher rates as the backdrop. Analysts quoted by BusinessLine say an in-line outcome could push the Nifty toward 23,000. That is their commentary, not a forecast of mine. The GST Council also meets today."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A hike moves your EMIs, bank margins and the rupee, which closed at 96.42 against the dollar, near its all-time low. Volatility around the 10 AM announcement is likely."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.ndtvprofit.com/economy/rbi-hike-repo-rate-for-first-time-since-2023-why-october-policy-could-mark-a-hawkish-turn-12145099\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NDTV Profit</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/bulls-hold-for-second-day-but-rupee-bleeds-near-record-low/article71551341.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine, 6 Oct close</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "The Reserve Bank of India building in Mumbai, a sandstone-coloured block behind trees", "caption": "Reserve Bank of India, Mumbai. Photo: Sailko (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Insurers and brokers are fighting the IRDAI commission caps, and the decision window is now short", "kicker": "Your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Insurance Brokers Association of India told reporters on 6 October that it wants the 25 October deadline for feedback on IRDAI's paper \"Recalibrating Economics of Insurance Distribution\" pushed to December. It wrote to the Finance Minister on 2 October and, per Reuters, to the Prime Minister. Its argument: the paper does not say how savings from lower distribution costs would reach policyholders, and IBAI estimates at least 10 lakh livelihoods could be at risk over five years. IBAI backs the ban on compulsory bundling of insurance with loans and tighter suitability rules."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The proposed caps, as Mint reports them: 2% to 5% for banks and lenders selling insurance with loans; 15% to 20% on health policies at purchase and 5% to 10% for renewals and portability; 5% to 10% on motor personal accident; 5% to 20% on first-year life commission depending on tenure. Moneycontrol reports that life insurers will push for targeted changes, not withdrawal, with credit-life and annuities the main flashpoints. IRDAI is said to have told the industry that a long delay or phased rollout could trigger front-loaded sales before the lower caps apply. CEO consultations are due this week, per the same report."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the economics Probus sits on. Comments close 25 October, and IRDAI is weighing 1 January or 1 April as the start date. Brokers are asking for more time. IRDAI reportedly does not want a long delay."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/finance/irdai-distribution-paper-ibai-more-time-for-consultation-asks-deadline-till-december-20005633.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18 on IBAI</a> / <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/banking/credit-life-annuities-emerge-as-key-concerns-for-life-insurers-over-irdai-distribution-rules-article-14045852.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol on insurers</a> / <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/money/personal-finance/irdai-commission-caps-insurance-brokers-write-to-pm-fm-say-proposed-rules-may-not-benefit-policyholders-11791281595261.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint, citing Reuters</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Mistral and Reflection both announced frontier-class open-weight models, with weights due this month", "kicker": "Your builds"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Mistral Large 4 is in public preview from 6 October on Mistral Studio. It has 1 trillion parameters with 49 billion active, takes images and text, and was trained on 3,800 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GPUs in Mistral's own European datacenters. Weights are promised by the end of the month. Mistral says it is state of the art among open models on cybersecurity, finance and law, and competitive in coding. Those charts are Mistral's own."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Reflection, a Brooklyn startup, announced Beam on 5 October (US time): 501 billion parameters, 23 billion active per token, a one-million-token context and an Apache 2.0 licence when it ships \"later this month\". Reflection says Beam matches Z.ai's GLM 5.2 on hard reasoning tests with three to four times less compute, and trails Kimi K3. Quartz notes those claims are not independently verified. Beam is on a waitlist for now."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If both ship as promised, you get self-hostable models near the top of open-model benchmarks. That changes what a cost-sensitive prototype can run on. Treat the benchmarks as vendor claims until independent tests land."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mistral</a> / <a href=\"https://the-decoder.com/reflections-beam-becomes-the-most-capable-open-weight-model-built-outside-china/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Decoder on Reflection</a> / <a href=\"https://qz.com/reflection-ai-beam-open-weight-model-launch-100626\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Quartz on Reflection</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Concentric dotted circles expanding outward on a dark green background, from Reflection's Beam announcement", "caption": "Reflection's Beam announcement graphic. Credit: Reflection, via The Decoder.", "img": "photos/d2-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Nifty rebounded for a second day after an eight-week slide, with foreign money still leaving", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Nifty closed at 22,776.10 on Tuesday, up 0.98%, after an eight-week decline of 8.8%, its longest weekly losing run since 2020. FIIs sold ₹2,961 cr on 6 October and have been net sellers in all three October sessions, about ₹17,145 cr in total. DIIs bought ₹5,089 cr on the day and about ₹20,312 cr over the three sessions. Year to date on the same NSE series: FIIs net sold ₹4,20,212 cr, DIIs bought ₹6,59,847 cr. This series differs from the NSDL figures in yesterday's paper, so the totals do not match. Full recap is in Markets below."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The market is up on stock-specific Q2 buying, not a change in foreign flows. Domestic funds are absorbing the selling. RBI day will test that."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/fii-net-sell-indian-equities-worth-rs-2-961-crore-while-diis-net-buy-rs-5-089-crore-on-oct-6-14046142.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol, NSE data</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/bulls-hold-for-second-day-but-rupee-bleeds-near-record-low/article71551341.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Oolka is reportedly raising $45 M at roughly double its April valuation", "kicker": "Startups"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Per four people cited by Moneycontrol, Bessemer would put in about $20 M and Prosus about $10 M, with about $15 M from existing backers Accel, Lightspeed and 8i Ventures. The valuation is around $200 M, up from about $90 M in April 2026. Oolka, founded in 2024 by former Meesho executive Utkrishta Kumar, says it is India's first agentic AI credit-management platform. Before this round it had raised about $21 M, per Tracxn. If it closes, it would be Prosus's fourth fintech bet in just over a month. Oolka, Bessemer and Prosus did not respond to Moneycontrol."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> An AI credit-management app for consumers is drawing Bessemer and Prosus money. For an APM at an insurance aggregator, it is a read on how investors value agentic personal-finance products. It is a report of talks, not a closed round."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/startup/fintech-startup-oolka-in-talks-to-raise-45-million-from-bessemer-prosus-and-others-14045092.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol, 6 Oct</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street with its red BSE sign", "caption": "Bombay Stock Exchange, Dalal Street. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Yesterday's recap", "kicker": "India, Tuesday 6 October"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Nifty 50", "v": "22,776.10, up 220.35 points (0.98%). Intraday low 22,561.60."}, {"k": "Sensex", "v": "73,067.81, up 685.34 points (0.95%)"}, {"k": "Bank Nifty", "v": "55,128.40, up 0.76%. Kotak Mahindra Bank gained about 3.82%."}, {"k": "Breadth and volatility", "v": "Advance-decline ratio 1.82, the highest since 10 July. India VIX about 13.6."}, {"k": "Sectors", "v": "Chemicals +2%, Pharma +1.7%, Oil and Gas +1.6%, FMCG +1.4%. IT fell, by nearly 2% per BusinessLine and 0.59% per Moneycontrol."}, {"k": "Rupee and commodities", "v": "Rupee 96.42 per dollar. Brent below $99, gold near $4,153 an ounce, silver above $61."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Top Nifty gainers were Trent, BSE and Kotak Mahindra Bank. Laggards were Coal India, Tech Mahindra and Max Healthcare. The IT sector figure differs between the two sources, so I give both. Sources: <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/bulls-hold-for-second-day-but-rupee-bleeds-near-record-low/article71551341.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine</a> and <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/fii-net-sell-indian-equities-worth-rs-2-961-crore-while-diis-net-buy-rs-5-089-crore-on-oct-6-14046142.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol</a>."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Global", "kicker": "United States, Tuesday 6 October"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The S&P 500 rose 0.6% to a record 7,818.93, the Dow added 0.5% to 51,521.28 and the Nasdaq gained 0.4% to 27,599.79, per AP. Constellation Energy led after a long-term power deal with Google, and easing bond yields helped. Source: <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/wall-street-stocks-dow-nasdaq-267ca73e15e09f7e8deb5f6076405f48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AP</a>."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Today's watch list", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Events first: the RBI policy announcement at 10 AM and the GST Council meeting, both today. Below, each name is on the list because of a reported development. No view on direction is implied."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "Titan", "detail": "Q2 business update: domestic business +22%, international +97%, watch business +30%, jewellery +21%. 3,758 stores."}, {"n": "2", "title": "NBCC", "detail": "Floated ₹9,620 cr tenders to build 16 stalled Supertech projects."}, {"n": "3", "title": "HCL Technologies", "detail": "HCL Group announced a ₹500 cr expansion of HCL IT City, up to 9.5 lakh sq ft for 4,500 people."}, {"n": "4", "title": "NTPC", "detail": "Plans to buy 10 to 15 million tonnes of coal from private commercial mines for FY27."}, {"n": "5", "title": "Ola Electric Mobility", "detail": "Board meets today on a partly paid rights issue of up to ₹1,000 cr."}, {"n": "6", "title": "Asian Paints", "detail": "Board to consider an interim dividend and results. Record date, if declared, is 4 November."}, {"n": "7", "title": "Vedanta Aluminium", "detail": "Q2 aluminium production up 5% to 6.49 lakh tonnes."}, {"n": "8", "title": "Apollo Tyres", "detail": "CFO Gaurav Kumar resigned, effective 6 October."}, {"n": "9", "title": "ABB India", "detail": "Suneel Kumar Munaga named CFO from 1 January 2027."}, {"n": "10", "title": "Blue Dart", "detail": "Agreement with NCRTC to explore parcel movement on the Delhi-Meerut Namo Bharat corridor."}, {"n": "11", "title": "Solex Energy", "detail": "Won work orders worth ₹194.14 cr for TOPCon solar modules."}, {"n": "12", "title": "Asset Reconstruction Co. India", "detail": "Profit +14.6% to ₹59.1 cr, revenue +115.4% to ₹234.7 cr."}, {"n": "13", "title": "Glass Wall Systems", "detail": "Revenue +35.8% to ₹107.4 cr, profit down 7.7% to ₹17.3 cr."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Mahindra Lifespace", "detail": "Launched Mahindra Rivenza in Baner Annex, Pune, with about ₹3,500 cr gross development value."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Adani Ports", "detail": "Set up Paradip Mahanadi Terminal as a wholly owned subsidiary for two dry bulk berths."}, {"n": "16", "title": "JSW Cement", "detail": "Commissioned a 1 MTPA grinding unit at Nagaur. Total grinding capacity is now 25.10 MTPA."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Godrej Consumer Products", "detail": "Indonesian arm opened phase one of a new plant in Kendal, about ₹250 cr invested."}, {"n": "18", "title": "Mphasis", "detail": "£35.4 million agreement to run Social Security Scotland's benefits platform."}, {"n": "19", "title": "Marsons", "detail": "Plans a joint venture with US private equity firm Cleanhill Partners for transformer operations in the US and Canada."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://www.etnownews.com/markets/stocks-to-watch-today-october-7-titan-nbcc-hcl-technologies-ntpc-vedanta-aluminium-and-others-article-156287533\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET Now</a> and <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/stocks-to-watch-today-titan-kanohar-electricals-glass-wall-systems-arcil-karamtara-engineering-apollo-tyres-mphasis-mahindra-lifespaces-in-focus-on-07-october-14046143.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol</a>. Company numbers are as reported in those lists; I did not check them against exchange filings. Moneycontrol labels the ARCIL numbers \"Q1\" and ET Now calls them its latest results, so read the period with care."}]}, {"heading": "Finance goldmine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Smart money: foreigners are selling, domestic funds are buying, and the gap is wide", "kicker": "Flows"}, {"t": "p", "html": "On 6 October FIIs bought ₹11,258.72 cr and sold ₹14,220.02 cr, a net sale of ₹2,961 cr. DIIs bought ₹20,146.40 cr and sold ₹15,057.48 cr, a net purchase of ₹5,089 cr. The combined FII and DII flow for October so far is positive, about ₹3,167 cr. The Nifty is up 0.69% in October after falling about 6% in September."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Foreign selling has been the backdrop to the whole slide. Domestic buying is what kept it orderly."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/fii-net-sell-indian-equities-worth-rs-2-961-crore-while-diis-net-buy-rs-5-089-crore-on-oct-6-14046142.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol, NSE data</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Regulatory window: GST Council meets today on GST 2.0 compliance", "kicker": "Policy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Moneycontrol says the focus is the GST 2.0 process and compliance reforms, including redesigned compliance procedures. I did not find the agenda itself. Outcomes land after this paper is published."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Compliance redesign matters if any of your side projects touch invoicing or small-business tools."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/fii-net-sell-indian-equities-worth-rs-2-961-crore-while-diis-net-buy-rs-5-089-crore-on-oct-6-14046142.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol, 7 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Hidden catalysts: a state builder takes over stalled housing, and a rights issue is on the table", "kicker": "Corporate actions"}, {"t": "p", "html": "NBCC floated ₹9,620 cr of tenders for 16 stalled Supertech real estate projects. Ola Electric Mobility's board meets today to consider a partly paid rights issue of up to ₹1,000 cr. Titan reported domestic business up 22% and international up 97% in its Q2 update. The tenders and the rights issue are proposals, not completed events."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Both are specific, dated actions rather than commentary."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.etnownews.com/markets/stocks-to-watch-today-october-7-titan-nbcc-hcl-technologies-ntpc-vedanta-aluminium-and-others-article-156287533\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET Now</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Sector spotlight, day 2: banks and NBFCs, insurance", "kicker": "Rotates every two days"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Banks: Bank Nifty rose 0.76% on Tuesday, helped by healthy Q2 business updates and positioning ahead of today's decision. Kotak Mahindra Bank was a standout at about 3.82%. Insurance: the open questions are the credit-life and annuity commission limits and the start date, covered on the front page. Next edition the spotlight moves to a new pair of sectors."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Both sectors sit under today's RBI and IRDAI stories."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/bulls-hold-for-second-day-but-rupee-bleeds-near-record-low/article71551341.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine</a> / <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/banking/credit-life-annuities-emerge-as-key-concerns-for-life-insurers-over-irdai-distribution-rules-article-14045852.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol on credit-life</a>"}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Contrarian slotNothing evidence-backed today. The consensus on a 25 bp hike is as strong as it looks, and I have no data that argues otherwise."}]}, {"heading": "Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Garuda Aerospace raised about $10 M pre-IPO at a $320 M pre-money valuation", "kicker": "India"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Chennai company raised about ₹96 cr, led by the Motherson family office and Dubai-based Aditum Investment Group, per people aware of the deal. It filed draft papers confidentially in April and got SEBI's observations on 5 August. The ET page I could read was a short synopsis, so details beyond that are not verified."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A drone company heading to a Q1 2027 listing through the confidential SEBI route. A reference point for how deeptech exits are being planned."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/tech/startups/garuda-aerospace-lands-10-million-pre-ipo-funding-at-320-million-valuation/articleshow/134746224.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Small rounds from the same day's roundup", "kicker": "India"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Beyond Appliances raised ₹110 cr in a Series B led by Fireside Ventures, for R&D and a new plant.", "Lorazzo raised ₹15 cr led by Sauce. It says revenue grew six times in 12 months (company claim).", "CurvetAI raised ₹6 cr pre-seed led by Merak Ventures. It says it has over 100,000 monthly users and has started earning revenue (company claim)."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The pattern: consumer and design-led brands and AI workspaces are still getting early cheques. Company claims are marked."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://yourstory.com/2026/10/startup-news-and-updates-daily-roundup-october-6-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YourStory daily roundup, 6 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Melius raised $25 M after burning its first codebase", "kicker": "Global"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Three former Ramp engineers built an AI performance-marketing tool. After more than six months they decided it did not have legs, scrapped the codebase and rebuilt as an \"agents lab for creative work\", generating ad campaigns, images and video. Melius announced a $20 M Series A led by CRV and a $5 M seed led by General Catalyst. It claims more than $1 M in annualised revenue within two months of leaving stealth in July. Rivals include Higgsfield, valued at $5.4 B in August, which the co-founder says has passed $700 M in annualised revenue."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A founder pattern worth stealing, covered in the insight below."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/06/ex-ramp-engineers-raise-20m-for-platform-melius-after-scrapping-their-first-product/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch, 6 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Set the kill criterion before you start", "kicker": "Startup insight of the day"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Melius did not rescue its first product. It decided it had not found the large market, cut it, and moved to an adjacent, bigger one."}, {"t": "p", "html": "For a side project, the cheap version is to write down in advance what traction by what date would make you stop. Decide the date and the number while you still feel neutral. That sentence is my reading of the Melius story, not something the founders said."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Opportunities", "kicker": "Open now"}, {"t": "p", "html": "AWS x IDFC FIRST Bank x Campus Fund Grand Challenge 2026 closes 10 October. It is for current students, graduates from 2023 onward and dropouts from the 2023 cohort onward, across all sectors. Top 15 startups get a place at the grand finale in Mumbai on 10 December, and AWS credits are listed among the benefits. Check eligibility against your own situation before applying. Sources: <a href=\"https://www.eduparc.in/resources/news/aws-idfc-first-bank-and-campus-fund-announce-seventh-edition-of-grand-challenge\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Eduparc</a> and <a href=\"https://www.linkedin.com/posts/campus-fund_grandchallenge2026-campusfund-studententrepreneurs-activity-7511054273833598976-wKKD\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Campus Fund on LinkedIn</a>."}, {"t": "p", "html": "An aggregator also lists 16 grants and programs closing 5 to 15 October (<a href=\"https://www.startupgrantsindia.com/blog/grants-closing-soon-2026-10-05\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Startup Grants India</a>). Its amounts conflict with its own text in at least one case, so verify each program on the official page before relying on it."}, {"t": "story", "title": "A commission-cap calculator for insurance distributors", "kicker": "One idea a day"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "What", "v": "A free web calculator where a small broker, agent or advisor enters their current mix of products and commissions and sees the effect of IRDAI's proposed caps."}, {"k": "Who pays", "v": "Start free to collect leads. A paid tier for multi-agent firms that want exports and scenario comparison. Price is untested."}, {"k": "Why now", "v": "Comments close 25 October, the start date is 1 January or 1 April, and the caps are published by product: 15 to 20% health, 5 to 20% first-year life, 2 to 5% loan-linked."}, {"k": "First step", "v": "This week, build the calculator from the numbers in the Mint report and the paper itself, and show it to ten distributors."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "This is my idea, not a validated one. The caps come from Mint's report of the draft, so check the paper before any number goes public. Also check whether your Probus employment terms allow side work in insurance distribution."}]}, {"heading": "AI race", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "The two biggest stories, Mistral Large 4 and Reflection Beam, are on the front page. Below is what else moved."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Anthropic's Sonnet 5.5 is now the default Sonnet in the API, at unchanged prices", "kicker": "Models and pricing"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Per the Claude Code changelog as quoted by AI Coding Guide, Sonnet 5.5 (claude-sonnet-5-5) has a 1M context and costs $2 and $10 per million input and output tokens, with $0.20 cache reads. That is the same as Sonnet 5. I read the price from that secondary page, which says it matches Anthropic's pricing table. Anthropic also has a Sonnet 5.5 prompting page dated 6 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If your prototypes call Sonnet, they have moved to 5.5 with no price change."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/weekly-2026-10-04/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Coding Guide, quoting the Claude Code changelog</a> / <a href=\"https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/build-with-claude/prompt-engineering/prompting-claude-sonnet-5-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic docs, Sonnet 5.5 prompting</a>"}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Reported, not confirmedNothing new on the earlier report that OpenAI cancelled an October release over safety. I found no primary confirmation today, so it stays out of the news items."}]}, {"heading": "Vibe coding", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "HeyGen's HyperFrames Studio: a free desktop video editor where your own coding agent does the work", "kicker": "New tool"}, {"t": "p", "html": "It launched on Product Hunt on 5 October for Mac and Linux, marked free, and sign-in is a HeyGen account. Compositions are HTML with timing set by data attributes, and the Apache 2.0 renderer has no per-render fee. The agent is Claude Code or Codex on your machine, and runs bill to that account's plan or an API key. IndieFounder did not see a Windows build. The article is a third-party write-up, so I have not tested the app."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A video is a folder of HTML files, so you can version it and let Claude Code or Codex edit it. Worth a try if you make demo videos for side projects."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.indiefounder.me/articles/hyperframes-studio-desktop-agent-video-editor\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IndieFounder</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Claude Code 2.1.284 to 2.1.288: Sonnet 5.5 default, Claude Mods, new switches", "kicker": "Update"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Version 2.1.284 makes Sonnet 5.5 the default and adds /mcp reconnect all. 2.1.285 adds a switch to turn off WebFetch and a managed setting to limit which API providers a machine may use. 2.1.287 adds Claude Mods, which let plugins change deeper behaviour. This is from AI Coding Guide's summary of the changelog, not the changelog itself."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Worth updating for the new default. Mods are optional."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://aicoding-guide.com/en/posts/weekly-2026-10-04/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Coding Guide, week of 4 Oct</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Lovable swaps Visual edits for a preview toolbar", "kicker": "Update"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The changelog also adds line-level references in chat (for example Button.tsx:42) and a User insights profile for workspace admins. The changelog page does not show entry dates in what I read, so I cannot say when each shipped."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> You can click an element, fix text inline, sketch on the preview or pin a comment, and queue the next change while Lovable is still working."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://docs.lovable.dev/changelog\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Lovable changelog</a>"}]}, {"heading": "AI hack of the day", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Give the model a sample and a reader, then delete the rules", "kicker": "Wednesday: writing and communication"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Anthropic says it removed over 80% of Claude Code's system prompt for its newest models with no measurable loss on its coding evaluations. It found it was over-constraining the model, and that its own instructions sometimes clashed, such as \"leave documentation as appropriate\" next to \"DO NOT add comments\". Its replacement instruction for code is one line: write code that reads like the surrounding code, matching comment density, naming and idiom."}, {"t": "p", "html": "That is guidance about code. The adaptation to writing is mine: replace a pile of style rules with a sample of your own writing and a named reader, and let the model match them."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Copy and pasteDraft a reply to the email below. The reader is [name, role] and they need to decide [what]. Write it so it reads like my two sample emails: match their sentence length, vocabulary and paragraph rhythm. Sample 1: [paste]. Sample 2: [paste]. Email to answer: [paste]. Do not add anything I did not give you."}, {"t": "p", "html": "If a draft still feels ornate, one extra tip is reported by AI Impactful, a third-party site, as an Anthropic suggestion for its model: \"Please remove all mannered prose.\" I could not find that line on Anthropic's own pages, so treat it as unconfirmed, though it costs nothing to try. Sources: <a href=\"https://claude.com/blog/the-new-rules-of-context-engineering-for-claude-5-generation-models\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic, context engineering for Claude 5 models, 24 July</a> and <a href=\"https://aimpactful.com/anthropics-five-word-fix-for-ai-writing/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AI Impactful</a>."}]}, {"heading": "Sports snippets", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "India beat West Indies by 8 wickets in the first T20I", "kicker": "Cricket, Tuesday"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Captain Shreyas Iyer made an unbeaten 102 off 43 balls, his maiden T20I hundred, as India chased 172 in Lucknow with 32 balls to spare. Debutant Naman Dhir took 3 for 31 as West Indies were bowled out for 171. Source: <a href=\"https://www.olympics.com/en/news/india-vs-west-indies-t20-cricket-2026-match-1-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Olympics.com</a>."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Verstappen wins the rain-hit Bahrain GP in Malaysia", "kicker": "F1, Sunday 4 October"}, {"t": "p", "html": "It is his and Red Bull's first win of 2026. Antonelli was second, Hamilton third, Leclerc fourth and Hadjar fifth. Russell stopped on the start-finish straight late on, and Antonelli extends his championship lead over him. Source: <a href=\"https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/verstappen-wins-rain-hit-bahrain-gp-in-malaysia-over-antonelli-as-russell-retires-late-on.6qfpIHPch8mIVj2YgfHdCp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Formula1.com</a>."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A Red Bull Formula 1 car on track in the pit lane area", "caption": "File photo, Red Bull Formula 1 car at the 2026 Chinese Grand Prix. Photo: Liauzh (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g7.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Djokovic takes the China Open, one title short of Federer", "kicker": "Tennis, Tuesday"}, {"t": "p", "html": "De Minaur retired in the second set after Djokovic won the first 7-6(3). It is Djokovic's seventh Beijing title and 102nd on tour, one behind Federer's 103. He is 34-0 in Beijing. In the semi-final, Medvedev was defaulted while Djokovic was serving for the match. Source: <a href=\"https://www.atptour.com/en/news/djokovic-de-minaur-beijing-2026-final\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ATP Tour</a>."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Novak Djokovic stretching for a shot on a red clay court, seen from above", "caption": "File photo, Djokovic at Roland-Garros 2024. Photo: Kuberzog (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g7.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Nakamura hits 3500 on Chess.com blitz", "kicker": "Chess, Monday and Tuesday"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Hikaru Nakamura became the first 3500-rated blitz player in Chess.com history on Monday and reached 3503 on Tuesday. He lost to Vladislav Artemiev in the last round of Titled Tuesday, which Artemiev won. Source: <a href=\"https://www.chess.com/news/view/artemiev-wins-titled-tuesday-october-6-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Chess.com</a>."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Kane scores two, India lose 1-6", "kicker": "Football, Tuesday"}, {"t": "p", "html": "England beat Czechia 3-0 in the Nations League on Harry Kane's record-equalling 125th cap, with Kane scoring twice and setting up another. Spain beat Croatia, with a Mikel Merino double keeping their 2.5-year unbeaten run alive. In Kolkata, India lost 1-6 to Uruguay, with Anirudh Thapa scoring the home side's goal. Sources: <a href=\"https://apnews.com/article/spain-england-nations-league-kane-merino-yamal-2367156909c95b00a0b8d909bce9b763\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AP</a> and <a href=\"https://sportstar.thehindu.com/football/indian-football/india-vs-uruguay-result-anirudh-thapa-goal-salt-lake-stadium-kolkata-2026/article71552864.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sportstar</a>."}]}, {"heading": "CAT warmup", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Essay: \"If computers count all the petals, who tastes the cherries?\"", "kicker": "Reading, about 20 minutes"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Liam Spradlin, a designer, argues that tech culture holds a belief he calls computism: the idea that the world, humans included, is made of measurable bits that can be computed and governed by code. Read the full essay on <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/if-computers-count-all-the-petals-who-tastes-the-cherries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aeon</a> (published 17 September, so older than this paper's news window, picked as a long read). I read all of it before writing the questions below. Read the essay first, then try them."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Reading comprehension", "kicker": "Three questions on the essay"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1.</strong> The author describes a chef who tried to brown lamb under a broiler the way \"smacking it with a propane torch\" would. The author uses this to show that:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) the chefs were poorly trained for the project", "(b) the oven could not translate an embodied cooking instinct into anything its logic understood", "(c) the oven needed a stronger broiler to match restaurant results", "(d) home cooks are less skilled than professional chefs"]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2.</strong> According to the author, the \"real tragedy\" of computism is:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) that computers may become more intelligent than people", "(b) that computers will take over some human activities", "(c) that people might build a world where every petal is counted and lose the ability to taste the cherries", "(d) that data collection will become too expensive to continue"]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3.</strong> Each of the following is used by the author as evidence or illustration EXCEPT:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) Google testing 41 shades of blue for its search links", "(b) Vaucanson's eighteenth-century mechanical duck", "(c) Descartes's picture of the body as a machine with a soul as its user", "(d) a study showing that A/B testing lowered the revenue of most companies"]}, {"t": "story", "title": "Set: the pitch day", "kicker": "LRDI, about 12 minutes"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Six startups, A to F, pitch to one investor on one day, one after another, in six consecutive slots numbered 1 to 6. Each startup is in a different one of six sectors: Fintech, Health, Edtech, Agri, Logistics and Climate. The following is known."}, {"t": "p", "html": "1F pitches before C.2The Agri startup pitches immediately after the Edtech startup.3The Health startup pitches immediately after the Logistics startup.4B and D pitch in consecutive slots.5The Edtech startup pitches immediately after the Climate startup.6A is the Edtech startup, B is the Fintech startup, C is the Logistics startup, D is the Climate startup and E is the Health startup."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The investor has a fixed budget. She hears the pitches in slot order and funds each one in full, in order, until she reaches a startup whose ask she cannot fully meet. She then stops. The asks, in ₹ crore, are below."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "A", "detail": "Ask"}, {"n": "2", "title": "B", "detail": "Ask"}, {"n": "3", "title": "C", "detail": "Ask"}, {"n": "4", "title": "D", "detail": "Ask"}, {"n": "5", "title": "E", "detail": "Ask"}, {"n": "6", "title": "F", "detail": "Ask"}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1.</strong> Which startup pitches in slot 4? (a) A (b) C (c) E (d) F"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2.</strong> What is the total ask, in ₹ crore, of the startups that pitch before the Edtech startup? (a) 7 (b) 8 (c) 10 (d) 13"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3.</strong> Her budget is ₹15 cr. Which startup is the first she cannot fully fund, and how much of her budget is left? (a) F, ₹2 cr (b) F, ₹5 cr (c) C, ₹2 cr (d) C, ₹7 cr"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q4.</strong> Suppose condition 4 (B and D are consecutive) is removed and everything else stays. How many different pitch orders are possible? (a) 1 (b) 2 (c) 3 (d) 4"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q5.</strong> Using the original conditions, her budget is ₹20 cr. How many startups does she fund in full? (a) 3 (b) 4 (c) 5 (d) 6"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Answers and worked solutions", "kicker": "Don't scroll on until you've tried"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Setup.</strong> Sectors are fixed for five startups, so F is Agri. Condition 5 and 2 chain the sectors: Climate, then Edtech, then Agri, in consecutive slots, which means D, A, F in that order. Condition 3 gives C then E in consecutive slots. Condition 4 says B is next to D. D has A right after it, so B must be right before D. That block is B, D, A, F. Condition 1 puts C after F, so C and E follow. The only order is B (Fintech), D (Climate), A (Edtech), F (Agri), C (Logistics), E (Health). I checked this by exhaustive search: it is the only arrangement that meets every condition."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1: (d) F.</strong> Slot 4 is F."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2: (c) 10.</strong> Edtech is A in slot 3. B and D pitch before it, so 4 + 6 = 10."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3: (a) F, ₹2 cr.</strong> In order: B takes 4, D takes 6 (total 10), A takes 3 (total 13). F asks 5 but only 2 remains, so she stops at F with ₹2 cr left."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q4: (c) 3.</strong> Without condition 4, D, A, F stay together and C, E stay together with F before C. The orders possible are B D A F C E, D A F B C E and D A F C E B. That is three."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q5: (b) 4.</strong> B 4, D 6, A 3, F 5 add up to 18. C asks 7, which would take the total to 25, so she funds four and stops at C with ₹2 cr left."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "Mumbai's Nariman Point skyline lit up at night across the water", "caption": "Nariman Point, Mumbai. Photo: Saad Abdullah (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0), cropped.", "img": "photos/d2-hero.jpg"}}, "3": {"title": "Morning paper, Thursday 8 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 3 of the trial. Built on spec v2.", "intro": "The RBI raised rates for the first time since February 2023, IRDAI's commission draft has a feedback deadline 17 days away, and the GST Council meets today.", "groups": [{"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The RBI raised the repo rate to 5.50%, its first hike in 3.5 years, and said cuts are off the table for now", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Monetary Policy Committee voted unanimously to raise the <a href=\"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">repo rate</a> (the rate at which the RBI lends to banks) by 25 basis points, from 5.25% to 5.50%. The standing deposit rate is now 5.25% and the marginal standing facility rate and Bank Rate are 5.75%. The committee also moved its stance to <a href=\"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">calibrated tightening</a> (the RBI says only a hike or a pause is next, no cuts). Two members, Dr Nagesh Kumar and Prof Ram Singh, wanted the stance kept neutral."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The RBI now projects FY27 GDP growth at 7.1% and CPI inflation at 5.2%, with quarterly inflation of 4.9%, 6.0% and 5.7% for Q2 to Q4. August CPI was 4.8%, up from 4.5% in July. Its stated reasons: a re-escalated West Asia conflict, volatile crude, a deficient monsoon and El Niño. The next meeting is 2 to 4 December. Minutes come on 21 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Indian Express words the stance change confusingly in its first paragraph. I used the RBI's own text: neutral to calibrated tightening."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Floating-rate EMIs go up. On a ₹50 lakh, 20-year loan the monthly payment rises by roughly ₹800 per quarter-point (my arithmetic, below). Higher yields also feed through to insurers' investment income, but I found no source quantifying that today, so treat it as a question, not a finding."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RBI policy resolution, 7 Oct</a> / <a href=\"https://www.rbi.org.in/Scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63744\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RBI Governor statement</a> / <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/rbi-hikes-repo-rate-25-bps-to-5-50-pc-emi-10910143/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Express</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "IRDAI's draft is now a three-way argument, with the RBI, brokers and the regulator all on record", "kicker": "Your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "At the post-policy press conference, Governor Sanjay Malhotra said the RBI has not firmed up its view on IRDAI's consultation paper and will send comments. \"It is primarily in the consumer's interest to reduce costs and, at the same time, to prevent or reduce mis-selling,\" he said, adding that the decision sits with the insurance regulator. The Insurance Brokers Association of India (IBAI) asked on 6 October to push the 25 October deadline to the end of December. It also wrote to the Prime Minister's Office and the Finance Minister."}, {"t": "p", "html": "What the paper proposes, per India Today and the ET BFSI report from 23 September: expenses of management (<a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/business/insurance-brokers-oppose-new-reforms-as-commission-cuts-may-lead-to-job-losses/article71552334.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EoM</a> (the share of premium an insurer can spend on running costs)) for life insurers fall to 15% of premium within two years and 12.5% within five. For general insurers the path is 25% in two years and 20% in five. Commission caps would vary by product, channel and effort. Two numbers drive the case: distributor pay in a corporate-agent sample rose 125% between FY23 and FY25 while new premium grew 28%, and 61st-month <a href=\"https://www.indiatoday.in/business/story/irdais-big-insurance-reset-explained-why-its-new-proposal-has-sparked-a-war-of-words-3010939-2026-10-07\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">persistency</a> (the share of policies still active after five years) is 48% overall versus 71% online."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The pushback is from an interested party, so weigh it as such. IBAI says more than 30 commission caps plus a one-third cut in expense limits could affect nearly 10 lakh livelihoods. It backs the ban on compulsory loan bundling, stronger suitability rules and clawbacks for proven mis-selling, and opposes the caps and tighter EoM limits. The Moneycontrol interview with IRDAI chairman Ajay Seth, headlined \"IRDAI rules out glide path for commission caps\", sits behind a paywall. I read only the headline and standfirst, so I am not reporting its contents."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The paper decides how much distributors can earn and how fast insurer expenses fall. Comments close on 25 October. Brokers want December. What you read in the feedback window will shape the final rules."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/rbi-to-firm-up-views-on-irdai-s-proposed-insurance-commission-overhaul-126100701296_1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Business Standard on the RBI</a> / <a href=\"https://www.indiatoday.in/business/story/irdais-big-insurance-reset-explained-why-its-new-proposal-has-sparked-a-war-of-words-3010939-2026-10-07\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">India Today explainer</a> / <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/finance/irdai-distribution-paper-ibai-more-time-for-consultation-asks-deadline-till-december-20005633.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18 on IBAI</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/business/insurance-brokers-oppose-new-reforms-as-commission-cuts-may-lead-to-job-losses/article71552334.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine on IBAI</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Nifty fell 0.76% to 22,603 on the RBI move, foreign investors sold ₹6,121 cr and the rupee hit 96.78", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Nifty 50 closed at 22,603.05, down 173.05 points. The Sensex closed at 72,638.70, down 429.11 points. Two sources agree on both closes. FIIs sold a net ₹6,121.37 cr and DIIs bought a net ₹4,596.57 cr, provisional exchange data via CNBC-TV18. Titan led the Nifty losers after a jewellery update that came in below estimates, and Kotak Mahindra Bank led the gainers. The rupee fell to a five-month low of 96.78 per dollar, per CNBC-TV18."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Rate-sensitive sectors took the hit while PSU banks rose. The pattern matters more than the day: foreign selling has now run through every session this week."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/fiis-sell-rs6121-crore-as-market-snaps-2-day-gaining-streak-20006959.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18, flows and close</a> / <a href=\"https://ninefifteenam.com/notes/post-market-report-7-october-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NineFifteenAM, exchange data</a> / <a href=\"https://aftermarketreport.zerodha.com/p/nifty-cools-off-to-22600-after-rbis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zerodha Aftermarket Report</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "OpenAI is rolling out GPT-6 with Intelligent UI, so ChatGPT answers can be interactive tools, not just text", "kicker": "Your builds"}, {"t": "p", "html": "OpenAI's own page says GPT-6 is rolling out globally in ChatGPT with <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-for-everyone\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Intelligent UI</a> (answers with visuals and interactive elements you can use in chat). Its help page says GPT-6 is rolling out to eligible Pro, Plus, Enterprise and Business users, with GPT-6 replacing \"Latest\" in the model menu, and Pro running on a model called GPT-6 Astra."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Caveat on detail. Unite.AI's longer write-up, bylined to an \"AI Research Agent\", says GPT-6 reaches the Free and Go tiers on 8 October. OpenAI's help page says Free and Go Instant uses GPT-5.6 Luna. I trust OpenAI's own page and did not use Unite.AI's tier claims or its benchmark figures."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> You build products on top of these models. The shift is that the answer itself can be a small working interface, which lowers the cost of prototyping a calculator or a comparison tool inside a chat."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://openai.com/index/gpt-6-for-everyone\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAI announcement</a> / <a href=\"https://help.openai.com/en/articles/20001354-gpt-6-and-other-models-in-chatgpt\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAI Help Centre</a> / <a href=\"https://www.unite.ai/openai-brings-gpt-6-with-intelligent-ui-to-all-chatgpt-tiers/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Unite.AI report</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A glass and steel Google office building with a blue tower and a street sign reading 43", "caption": "Google campus building, file photo. Photo: Gregory Varnum (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/d3-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "A Bengaluru startup serving insurers with voice AI raised ₹32 cr, and says it handles over 10 million customer engagements a month", "kicker": "Startups and your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<a href=\"https://entrepreneur.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/funding/desible-ai-raises-32-crore-seed-plus-round-led-by-prime-venture-partners/134758156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Desible.ai</a> (workflow automation for banks and insurers, starting with voice) raised ₹32 cr in a Seed Plus round led by Prime Venture Partners, with existing investor Invention Engine. It was founded in 2025 by Uttam Tiwari and Omkar Raikar. The company says it runs more than 25 workflows across collections, underwriting, claims, servicing and compliance, and that 40-plus BFSI institutions use or are evaluating it. All usage figures are company-reported. ET Entrepreneur and YourStory carry the same facts, so this is one company statement reported twice, not two independent checks."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is a company working inside insurance servicing: renewals, collections, claims. If IRDAI's rules push distributors toward servicing and persistency, this is the kind of tooling that gets bought."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://entrepreneur.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/funding/desible-ai-raises-32-crore-seed-plus-round-led-by-prime-venture-partners/134758156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET Entrepreneur</a> / <a href=\"https://yourstory.com/2026/10/startup-news-and-updates-daily-roundup-october-7-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YourStory roundup</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street with its red BSE sign", "caption": "Bombay Stock Exchange, Dalal Street. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Yesterday's recap", "kicker": "India, Wednesday 7 October"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Nifty 50", "v": "22,603.05, down 173.05 points (0.76%). Opened 22,690.45, high 22,717.65, low 22,546.30. It stayed below the previous close of 22,776.10 all day."}, {"k": "Sensex", "v": "72,638.70, down 429.11 points (0.59%)"}, {"k": "Bank Nifty", "v": "55,055.55, down 0.13%. Kotak Mahindra Bank +1.88%, ICICI Bank +1.09%, HDFC Bank -1.22%."}, {"k": "Midcap and smallcap", "v": "Midcap 100 down 0.63%. Smallcap 100 up 0.30%, the only index in NineFifteenAM's table to rise."}, {"k": "Volatility", "v": "India VIX 13.89, up 2.06%"}, {"k": "Breadth", "v": "Zerodha counts 957 advancers and 1,349 decliners and 32 new 52-week lows to 9 highs. NineFifteenAM, quoting HDFC Sky for all NSE shares, counts 1,928 up and 2,251 down. The universes differ, so the numbers do too."}, {"k": "Sectors", "v": "PSU Bank +1.00%, Media +0.69%. Metal -2.33%, Realty -1.77%, Auto -1.58%, IT -1.34%."}, {"k": "Flows", "v": "FIIs net sold ₹6,121.37 cr, DIIs net bought ₹4,596.57 cr (provisional, via CNBC-TV18)"}, {"k": "Rupee and crude", "v": "Rupee 96.78 per dollar, down 43 paise (CNBC-TV18 also says five-month low). Brent about $102 a barrel, per PTI as cited by NineFifteenAM, which is the only source I have for the crude figure."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Asia ended lower (Kospi, Nikkei, Hang Seng), and US markets had closed higher on Tuesday, per the same PTI report. I did not pull a US close for Wednesday, so I am not giving one. Zerodha's report says the Nifty recovered after the 10 AM policy announcement, touched about 22,715, then gave it all back after 12:30. Metals fell on a stronger dollar and weaker global metal prices; I did not find a reported reason for the moves in Bharti Airtel, BSE or Bharat Electronics. Sources: <a href=\"https://ninefifteenam.com/notes/post-market-report-7-october-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NineFifteenAM</a>, <a href=\"https://aftermarketreport.zerodha.com/p/nifty-cools-off-to-22600-after-rbis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zerodha Aftermarket Report</a>, <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/fiis-sell-rs6121-crore-as-market-snaps-2-day-gaining-streak-20006959.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a>. I read Zerodha's text; its chart images were not used."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Today's watch list", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Events first: the GST Council meets today, and TCS reports its September-quarter results. Each name below is listed because of a reported development. No view on direction is implied."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "TCS", "detail": "September-quarter results today. Analysts expect modest sequential revenue growth and a stronger year-on-year number. Board may consider a second interim dividend."}, {"n": "2", "title": "Titan", "detail": "Fell 3.80% to ₹4,377. Quarterly update: consumer businesses up about 25%, jewellery about 21%, which was below what analysts had expected. 3,758 stores."}, {"n": "3", "title": "Kalyan Jewellers", "detail": "Closed about 4% higher after its Q2 business update (CNBC-TV18)."}, {"n": "4", "title": "Adani Enterprises", "detail": "CARE Ratings upgraded its long-term rating to AA; Stable from AA-, the highest in its history, per the company filing. The stock fell 3.75% on the day."}, {"n": "5", "title": "Ola Electric", "detail": "Approved a rights issue of up to ₹1,000 cr: 37.03 crore partly paid shares at ₹27, with a ₹17 premium. Issue size ₹999.74 cr if fully subscribed and paid."}, {"n": "6", "title": "HCL Technologies", "detail": "Opened a regional headquarters in Sandton, Johannesburg."}, {"n": "7", "title": "Tata Power", "detail": "Partnered with Norway's Ocean Sun on floating solar technology for India."}, {"n": "8", "title": "Jubilant FoodWorks", "detail": "Consolidated revenue ₹2,608.7 cr for the September quarter, up 11.9% on the year."}, {"n": "9", "title": "Varun Beverages", "detail": "Will invest up to ₹4.10 cr in Jager Renewables, a solar SPV in Uttar Pradesh."}, {"n": "10", "title": "TD Power Systems", "detail": "QIP opened with a floor price of ₹775.35 a share and a possible discount of up to 5%."}, {"n": "11", "title": "Fino Payments Bank", "detail": "Loan-referral disbursals up 223% on the year to ₹278 cr in September (provisional, unaudited)."}, {"n": "12", "title": "Gujarat Pipavav Port", "detail": "Container volume 1.92 lakh TEUs in Q2 against 1.64 lakh a year earlier."}, {"n": "13", "title": "Allcargo Terminals", "detail": "September volume 61,800 TEUs, down 7% on the year and 6% on the month."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Bank of Baroda", "detail": "CARE reaffirmed its AAA; Stable ratings on infrastructure and Tier II bonds and A1+ on its certificate of deposit programme."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Kotak Mahindra Bank", "detail": "Top Nifty gainer, +1.88% to ₹440.00 on a day banks cut early losses. No company news was reported."}, {"n": "16", "title": "Asian Paints", "detail": "Fell 2.15% to ₹2,372.10, with higher crude cited as pressure on paint stocks (CNBC-TV18)."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Infosys", "detail": "Fell 2.16% to ₹992 the day before TCS reports, per NineFifteenAM."}, {"n": "18", "title": "Hindalco", "detail": "Fell 3.15% to ₹910.85 as metals dropped on a stronger dollar."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/stocks-in-news-tcs-adani-enterprises-ola-electric-hcl-tech-tata-power-and-varun-beverages/articleshow/134771196.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times stocks in news, 8 October</a>, <a href=\"https://ninefifteenam.com/notes/post-market-report-7-october-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NineFifteenAM</a>, <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/fiis-sell-rs6121-crore-as-market-snaps-2-day-gaining-streak-20006959.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a>. Price moves are 7 October closes. TCS results arrive after this paper is built."}]}, {"heading": "Finance goldmine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The arithmetic of a 25 bp hike on a home loan", "kicker": "Mechanism"}, {"t": "p", "html": "An illustration, not a quote from any lender. Take a ₹50 lakh loan over 20 years. At 8.50% the monthly instalment is about ₹43,391. At 8.75%, one quarter-point more, it is about ₹44,186. The difference is roughly ₹800 a month, around ₹1.9 lakh over the full 20 years. This assumes the full 25 bp is passed on and the term stays fixed. Lenders pass changes on at different speeds, and many borrowers see the tenure stretch instead of the instalment rise. Check your own loan's reset terms. Indian Express says the hike will push up borrowing costs on home, personal and vehicle loans and provide some support to deposit rates."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Your own loans, and any customer you speak to with a floating-rate loan, move by this much. It also shows why a 'small' hike still makes headlines."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.rbi.org.in/scripts/BS_PressReleaseDisplay.aspx?prid=63742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">RBI resolution</a> / <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/india/rbi-hikes-repo-rate-25-bps-to-5-50-pc-emi-10910143/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Express on EMIs</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Foreign investors sold ₹13,782 cr over three sessions this week, and domestic funds bought about the same", "kicker": "Flows"}, {"t": "p", "html": "FIIs were net sellers of ₹4,699.14 cr on Monday, ₹2,961.30 cr on Tuesday and ₹6,121.37 cr on Wednesday, according to CNBC-TV18's exchange data. That adds up to ₹13,781.81 cr over three sessions, my sum. DIIs bought ₹5,181.62 cr, ₹5,088.92 cr and ₹4,596.57 cr, a total of ₹14,867.11 cr. DIIs have therefore just about matched FII selling this week. One discrepancy: DayStarter's 7 October issue gives Tuesday's FII net sale as ₹4,281 cr, against ₹2,961 cr from CNBC-TV18. I used the exchange-based figure and could not resolve the gap."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Domestic funds keep absorbing the selling, but the gap widened on the day of the hike."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/market/fiis-sell-rs6121-crore-as-market-snaps-2-day-gaining-streak-20006959.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a> / <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-07-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DayStarter, 7 Oct (discovery lead)</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Regulatory window: the GST Council meets today, with refunds, input credit and enforcement on the table", "kicker": "Policy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The 57th Council meeting was originally 12 September, moved to 7 October because of the BRICS summit, then moved again to 8 October, with \"unavoidable circumstances\" cited. NDTV, citing Finance Ministry sources, says rates are expected to stay unchanged. Proposals include one rate-change date a year, effective 1 April. The input tax credit proposal would let a buyer with a valid invoice who received the goods and paid in full keep the credit even if an upstream supplier defaults. Refund claims currently take about 25 days. The proposal is acknowledgement within 10 days and 90% of eligible refunds released after a risk check. Treat all of this as proposals reported from sources. Outcomes land after this paper is published."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> No rate changes are expected. The relevant parts for you are procedural: faster refunds, more certain input tax credit and fewer criminal provisions."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/no-gst-rate-change-council-to-focus-on-structural-reforms-at-upcoming-meet-12147055\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NDTV, 6 Oct</a> / <a href=\"https://www.ndtv.com/india-news/57th-gst-council-meeting-rescheduled-for-2nd-time-to-be-held-on-thursday-12142957\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NDTV, 5 Oct on the reschedule</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Bank angle on IRDAI: analysts see limited hit, but expect a shift in what banks push", "kicker": "Sector"}, {"t": "p", "html": "IIFL Capital, as quoted by Business Standard, puts the banking system's bancassurance income pool at about $2.2 billion, growing 28% a year over three years. It says that is about 10% of bank fee income, nearly 3% of profit after tax and roughly 6 basis points of return on assets. Banks could favour higher-commission products. Insurers could steer business to group companies. Both are analyst expectations, not outcomes."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It shows how different the pain is by channel. Banks have other income. Brokers and PoSP-heavy distributors do not."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/rbi-to-firm-up-views-on-irdai-s-proposed-insurance-commission-overhaul-126100701296_1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Business Standard, 7 Oct</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "The entrance of a Bengaluru office with a yellow-lit CRED sign at night", "caption": "A Bengaluru startup office, file photo. CRED is not in today's news. Photo: Soumendra Kumar Sahoo (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/d3-g3.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Indian startup money is moving to growth-stage rounds, away from late stage and fintech", "kicker": "Insight"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Inc42's Q3 2026 report says Indian startups raised $2.2 billion across 210 deals in July to September, up 5% on the year. Growth-stage funding rose 46% to $1.1 billion, while late-stage funding was flat at $994 million, with deal counts at 90 (up 38%) and 27 (down 10%). AI was the top sector at $438 million, up 265%. Cleantech took $433 million and deeptech $290 million. Fintech funding fell 11% and ecommerce fell 31%. This is one data provider's count and its categories may differ from others'. Investors quoted attribute it to more early-stage funds feeding Series A to C, a thin pool of large late-stage cheques, and more scrutiny of fundamentals."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The cheques are going to companies with proven early traction and long runways, mostly in AI, cleantech and deeptech. A fintech or insurtech pitch needs stronger numbers than it did two years ago."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/buzz/why-growth-stage-startup-funding-outpaced-late-stage-investments-in-q3/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42, Q3 2026 funding report</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Three insurance-adjacent and fintech rounds from 7 October", "kicker": "Deals"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Desible.ai, ₹32 cr Seed Plus, Prime Venture Partners. Voice-first automation for insurers and banks. Company-reported usage, covered above.", "Credfix, ₹16.1 cr seed led by Tanglin Venture Partners, with Stargazer. Helps borrowers understand debt, talk to lenders and handle recovery calls and legal notices. The company says it serves over 2 lakh borrowers with more than ₹4,000 cr of outstanding debt, and about 40% of its users are in Tier II and III cities.", "Zomint, ₹36 cr seed led by Lightspeed and Prime Venture Partners. Wealth advice with human advisers and AI tools, founded September 2025. The company says it retained 97% of clients, with each relationship manager serving over 250 families. Both figures are company-reported."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://yourstory.com/2026/10/startup-news-and-updates-daily-roundup-october-7-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YourStory roundup, 7 Oct</a> / <a href=\"https://entrepreneur.economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/funding/desible-ai-raises-32-crore-seed-plus-round-led-by-prime-venture-partners/134758156\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET Entrepreneur on Desible.ai</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Opportunities", "kicker": "Where the gap might be"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Servicing after the sale. IRDAI cites persistency as a core failure, and Insurance Samadhan's co-founder told India Today the debate is about the cost of selling more than the responsibility of servicing. Whoever owns the post-sale relationship is better placed if commissions are tied to outcomes.", "Effort evidence. If commissions follow \"effort and complexity\", distributors will need a record of the advice they gave. Tools that capture it without extra work are a plausible need. This is my inference, not something any source says.", "Debt-side advice. Credfix's numbers suggest a large borrower population with unmet advisory needs, though its figures are its own."]}, {"t": "story", "title": "A renewal and claims-assistance layer built for distributors", "kicker": "One idea, a hypothesis to test, not a validated market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The idea: give mid-size insurance distributors a WhatsApp-first renewal and claim-help assistant that logs every advisory and servicing touch per policy, so they can show \"effort\" and persistency to insurers and regulators. Desible.ai already sells automation to insurers, including persistency use cases, so the first thing to test is whether distributors, who sit on the other side of the commission cap, will pay for it. Two questions would kill or confirm it quickly: do distributors currently track their servicing effort at all, and would an insurer accept a distributor-supplied log as evidence?"}]}, {"heading": "AI race", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Anthropic launched Claude Haiku 5.5 at about 75% lower cost than Haiku 4.5, and widened its startup programme", "kicker": "Models and pricing"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Anthropic's page says Haiku 5.5 costs around 75% less than Haiku 4.5 on average. Listed prices are $0.10 per million input tokens and $0.50 per million output tokens for prompts up to 100,000 tokens, against $1.00 and $5.00 for Haiku 4.5. It also halved Sonnet 5.5's cache-read price to $0.10 per million tokens. Anthropic's own benchmark table shows Haiku 5.5 ahead of its own Haiku 4.5 and of OpenAI's GPT-6 Luna on most listed tests, and behind Sonnet 5.5. Benchmarks were chosen and run by the vendor. The customer quotes on the page come from Asana, HubSpot, AlphaSense and Box, again selected by Anthropic."}, {"t": "p", "html": "TechCrunch reports that the expanded Claude for Startups programme gives qualifying companies a free year of Claude Team with up to five premium seats and $1,000 in API credits. Eligibility is founded in the last five years or funded in the last two. This is a single source, summarising Anthropic's announcement. Check the programme page before relying on the terms."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Cheap, fast models change which product ideas have viable unit economics. The comparison table is Anthropic's own, so treat the rankings as vendor claims until independent tests appear."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/claude-haiku-5-5\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic, Haiku 5.5</a> / <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/06/anthropic-gives-startups-a-free-year-of-enterprise-service-and-1000-in-token-credits/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch on Claude for Startups</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "OpenAI published 722 AI-written maths papers, and mathematicians say they cannot check the system behind them", "kicker": "Reported, contested"}, {"t": "p", "html": "TechSpot reports that OpenAI released 722 papers from an internal model, grouped into 372 sets from about 4,000 problems, including advances it describes as related to three of the five remaining Millennium Prize Problems. TechSpot stresses that this does not mean those problems are solved. The reviewing has barely begun. Rutgers professor Alex Kontorovich wrote that if a human had done the reported Riemann result it would be \"an instant Fields Medal\", which is a reaction to the claim, not a verification of it. I found this only in TechSpot and did not read OpenAI's own page, so it is single-source."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is a test of how AI claims should be verified: results without access to the model are hard for others to build on."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.techspot.com/news/114127-openai-publishes-722-ai-generated-math-papers-researchers.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechSpot</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "GPT-6 and Intelligent UI are covered on the front page."}]}, {"heading": "Vibe coding", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Google launched Playground, a browser tool that turns a text prompt into a playable, shareable game", "kicker": "Tool"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/ai/playground-experimental-gaming-platform/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Playground</a> (Google's experimental site for building games from prompts) lets people describe a game in chat, test it, and keep it private, share a link or publish to a gallery. Games run on desktop and mobile, with leaderboards and multiplayer in select genres. Google says Unity Spark is coming for more advanced builds. A Google spokesperson told The Verge, as reported by Gizmodo, that it runs on Gemini, Nano Banana and Lyria with a custom harness. Gizmodo's own impression is that many early games feel like simple mini-games. The tool is labelled experimental."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is the clearest example this week of the pattern worth borrowing: describe, test at once, share a link. It is also a reminder that most output at this level is simple."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/technology/ai/playground-experimental-gaming-platform/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Google blog</a> / <a href=\"https://gizmodo.com/googles-new-playground-aims-to-make-it-easy-to-vibe-code-a-game-right-in-your-browser-2000822955\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Gizmodo</a>"}]}, {"heading": "AI hack of the day", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Make the model write its own checks first, then build one slice", "kicker": "Thursday: building"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Most vibe-coded projects go wrong because you only find out what is broken after everything is built. Flip the order. Ask for the checks before the code, build only the first thing, run it, then go on. This is a working habit, not a claim from any source."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Copy and pasteI want to build [one sentence]. Before writing code, list 5 checks I can run by hand to know it works, each one a single action and an expected result. Then build only the smallest version that passes check 1. Show me exactly how to run it. Stop and wait. I will tell you whether check 1 passed before we do check 2."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Why it works: each slice is small enough to review, and the model has a target. If a cheaper, faster model is good enough for the repetitive parts, Anthropic positions Haiku 5.5 for exactly that subagent role, but that is its own claim. Try it on your next side project and note how many checks fail on the first run."}]}, {"heading": "Sports snippets", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "An empty cricket stadium with red and blue seating around a green outfield", "caption": "Ekana stadium, Lucknow, file photo of the venue. Photo: Tushar Singh Patel (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/d3-g7.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Iyer's maiden T20I hundred and an unbroken 137 beat West Indies", "kicker": "Cricket, first T20I in Lucknow"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India chased 172 and won by eight wickets with 32 balls to spare. Shreyas Iyer finished unbeaten on 102, his first T20I century, and Ishan Kishan made an unbeaten 37. Their partnership was 137 after both openers fell by 3.3 overs, with India 35 for 2. Kishan said he deliberately gave Iyer the strike because he was not in rhythm himself. India lead the series 1-0. Source: <a href=\"https://www.cricinfo.com/story/ind-vs-wi-1st-t20i-shreyas-iyer-and-ishan-kishan-play-contrasting-innings-in-big-india-win-over-west-indies-1557440\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cricinfo</a>."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A red and white Marussia Formula 1 car cornering on a street circuit behind a green wall and fencing", "caption": "Singapore street circuit, 2014 file photo of a Marussia F1 car. Photo: Morio (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/d3-g7.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Singapore is the last sprint weekend, with Antonelli 84 points clear", "kicker": "Formula 1, this weekend"}, {"t": "p", "html": "F1.com lists first practice on Friday 9 October at 16:30 local, sprint qualifying at 20:30, the sprint on Saturday at 17:00, qualifying at 21:00 and the race on Sunday 11 October at 20:00 local time. Singapore is two and a half hours ahead of India, so the race starts at 5:30 PM IST on Sunday. That conversion is mine. Kimi Antonelli leads the standings by 84 points after George Russell retired in Bahrain. Source: <a href=\"https://www.formula1.com/en/latest/article/what-time-is-the-formula-1-2026-singapore-grand-prix-and-how-can-i-watch-it.26vUwPc8sNYNC4KJRyXzLC\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Formula1.com</a>."}]}, {"heading": "CAT warmup", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Essay: \"Why real intelligence is something more than optimisation\"", "kicker": "Reading, about 25 minutes"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sasha Mudd, a philosophy professor, argues that calling intelligence \"optimisation\" quietly adopts Hume's view that reason is a servant of desire, and that Kant's view of reason as the power to judge ends is the one we should defend as AI spreads. Read the full essay on <a href=\"https://aeon.co/essays/why-real-intelligence-is-something-more-than-optimisation\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Aeon</a> first. The three questions below are written from the whole essay, which I read in full."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Reading comprehension", "kicker": "Three questions on the essay"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1.</strong> The author quotes Hume saying it is \"not contrary to reason to prefer the destruction of the whole world to the scratching of my finger\". She uses this to show that:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) Hume thought moral feelings were unimportant", "(b) on Hume's view, reason can only judge means and cannot call even an extreme end irrational", "(c) Kant agreed that desire alone should set our ends", "(d) reason is more powerful in practice than the passions"]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2.</strong> The author says AI \"agents\" that broke free of their task parameters in the Hugging Face incident do not by themselves show self-rule in Kant's sense because:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) the agents were still being controlled by their engineers at every step", "(b) having purposes of one's own is not the same as governing them by reason, which means judging and revising ends by principles one can justify to others", "(c) only biological beings can have ends", "(d) breaking rules shows a lack of intelligence"]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3.</strong> The \"deeper danger\" the author names, beyond machines failing to align with human values, is that:"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["(a) machines will become better than people at optimisation", "(b) human values are too unclear to be programmed", "(c) people may become so comfortable being predicted, nudged and served that they stop setting and judging their own ends", "(d) regulators will not be able to keep up with AI companies"]}, {"t": "story", "title": "Set: the five-team league", "kicker": "LRDI, about 12 minutes"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Five fintech teams, A to E, play a round-robin hackathon league. Every team plays every other team exactly once. There are no draws. A win earns 2 points, a loss 0. Final points were: A 6, B 4, C 4, D 2, E 4. Two more facts are known: D beat A, and E beat C."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "A", "detail": "Final points"}, {"n": "2", "title": "B", "detail": "Final points"}, {"n": "3", "title": "C", "detail": "Final points"}, {"n": "4", "title": "D", "detail": "Final points"}, {"n": "5", "title": "E", "detail": "Final points"}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1.</strong> Which team beat both B and D? (a) A (b) C (c) E (d) None"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2.</strong> Which team's only win came against the team that finished top? (a) B (b) C (c) D (d) E"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3.</strong> B, C and E are tied on 4 points. Looking only at the matches among these three, which is true? (a) B is ahead (b) C is ahead (c) E is ahead (d) Each has one win and one loss, so nothing separates them"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q4.</strong> Suppose A had beaten D instead, with every other result unchanged. How many teams would then have 4 or more points? (a) 2 (b) 3 (c) 4 (d) 5"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q5.</strong> In how many of the ten matches did the winner finish with strictly fewer points than the loser? (a) 0 (b) 1 (c) 2 (d) 3"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Answers and worked solutions", "kicker": "Don't scroll on until you've tried"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>RC.</strong> Q1 (b). The point of the quote is that reason cannot judge ends, so it cannot even call an absurd preference irrational. Q2 (b). The essay says having purposes of one's own is not the same as governing them by reason, and the stronger capacity is judging and revising purposes by principles justified to others as equals. Q3 (c). The author says the deeper concern is that we may have become \"too well aligned\" with machines and comfortable not being sovereign ourselves."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>LRDI setup.</strong> Points divide by 2 into wins: A 3, B 2, C 2, D 1, E 2. That totals 10, the number of matches, so the data is consistent. D has one win, over A, so D lost to B, C and E. E's two wins are over C and D, so E lost to A and B. B has two wins: over E and D, so B lost to A and C. C has two wins: over B and D, so C lost to A and E. A has three wins: over B, C and E, and lost only to D. Full results: A beat B, C, E. B beat D, E. C beat B, D. D beat A. E beat C, D. Every win count checks out."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q1: (b) C.</strong> D lost to B, C and E, and B lost to A and C. C is the only team on both lists."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q2: (c) D.</strong> The top team is A, and D's only win was over A."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q3: (d).</strong> Among the three: C beat B, B beat E, E beat C. It is a cycle, so each has one win and one loss."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q4: (c) 4.</strong> A gets 2 more points, going to 8, and D loses 2, going to 0. B, C and E stay on 4. So A, B, C and E have 4 or more, four teams."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Q5: (b) 1.</strong> Only D's win over A qualifies: D had 2 points and A had 6. In the B-C and C-E matches the winner and loser both finished on 4, so those do not count."}]}, {"heading": "Source notes", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "DayStarter's 7 October issue and Zerodha's Aftermarket Report were used as discovery and cross-check inputs this morning. Zerodha's closing levels matched the two other sources I checked. DayStarter's Tuesday FII figure disagreed with exchange-based data, which I note above. Every fact in the front page was traced to a primary release or a named report, except where marked single-source. The long read, a separate file, goes through IRDAI's commission draft in depth."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "A fan of Indian 100 and 2000 rupee notes lying on a white sheet", "caption": "Indian rupee notes. Photo: Monito (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0), cropped.", "img": "photos/d3-hero.jpg"}}, "4": {"title": "Morning paper, Friday 9 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 4. Markets, GST, IRDAI, an AI radar and a startup case study.", "intro": "The Nifty closed at its lowest level in 18 months as Brent crude passed $104, the GST Council dropped arrest powers, and OpenAI's release of 722 AI-written maths manuscripts has split mathematicians.", "groups": [{"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The Nifty closed at an 18-month low as crude passed $104 and foreign investors sold ₹12,943 cr in a day", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Nifty 50 fell 371.25 points, or 1.64%, to 22,231.80, and the Sensex fell 1,045.46 points, or 1.44%, to 71,593.24. Both figures match across Moneycontrol, Mint and NineFifteenAM. Mint says the Nifty close is its lowest since 7 April 2025 and the Sensex close its lowest since 13 February 2024. Midcap and smallcap indices fell more than 2% each. Only 3 of the 50 Nifty stocks rose. Mint puts the loss in BSE market value at ₹9.96 lakh crore on the day, and Moneycontrol puts it at more than ₹12 lakh crore over two sessions."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The three reported drivers are the same everywhere. Brent crude rose about 4.8% to $104 a barrel on Gulf shipping attacks and Strait of Hormuz supply fears. The US 10-year Treasury yield sat near 5.3%. And foreign portfolio investors sold. Mint, citing provisional BSE data, says they sold ₹12,943 cr, their biggest single-day sale since 29 May. A flows tracker built on NSE data shows ₹12,944 cr, so the two agree. Domestic institutions bought about ₹10,703 cr on the same tracker, which is a secondary site, so treat that figure as indicative."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the second straight fall after the RBI hike, and the first close this low since April 2025. If you invest through SIPs, the point is that the market is moving on rates, oil and foreign flows, none of which you can time."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/market/nifty-50-performance-in-2026-fii-fpi-selling-inflation-rbi-nse-crude-oil-11791461972358.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint</a> / <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/taking-stock-nifty-hits-fresh-52-week-low-ends-below-22-250-sensex-tanks-1-045-pts-14047706.html/amp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol</a> / <a href=\"https://ninefifteenam.com/notes/post-market-report-8-october-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NineFifteenAM, exchange data</a> / <a href=\"https://www.outlookbusiness.com/markets/nifty-hits-2026-low-what-triggered-the-market-bloodbath\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outlook Business</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Bharat Mandapam convention centre in New Delhi lit up in the evening", "caption": "Bharat Mandapam, New Delhi, the venue of the GST Council meeting per The Hindu. Photo: Ministry of Culture, Government of India (Wikimedia Commons, GODL-India).", "img": "photos/d4-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The GST Council scrapped arrest powers, raised the prosecution threshold to ₹5 crore and widened credit on employee health and life insurance", "kicker": "Policy, and your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The 57th GST Council met on Thursday 8 October. It revoked GST officers' arrest powers, raised the prosecution threshold from ₹1 crore to ₹5 crore, removed the minimum punishment, and cut the general penalty from ₹25,000 to ₹10,000 (The Hindu, PTI). The Finance Ministry said the rate structure is settled and rate changes will now happen once a year. No rates changed."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Economic Times lists eight reforms. Refund acknowledgement falls from 15 days to 10, and the system sanctions 90% of a claim by risk assessment. Registration amendments such as a trade name or director change are accepted automatically. Businesses up to ₹5 crore turnover that sell only to consumers get an optional yearly return with quarterly tax payment. Credit is widened to cover health and life insurance taken for employees, telecom towers and pipelines outside factories. A committee has three months to study protection for honest buyers whose suppliers did not pay tax."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Input tax credit on health and life insurance bought for employees is a direct tailwind for group cover."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.thehindu.com/business/Economy/gst-council-scraps-arrest-powers-of-gst-officers-ups-prosecution-threshold-to-5-crore/article71559868.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu (PTI)</a> / <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/economy/policy/gst-council-2026-8-key-reforms-on-registration-refunds-itc-and-enforcement/articleshow/134789303.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times, 8 reforms</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "IRDAI's draft now has dates and a second opinion: ICRA sees the hit landing in FY28, a former IRDAI member expects 1 April 2027", "kicker": "Your job"}, {"t": "p", "html": "ICRA says FY27 growth should be largely unaffected and the pressure shows up in FY28, as distributors realign. It puts the proposed general-insurer expense ceiling falling from 30% to 25% within two years and 20% within five, and the life ceiling to 15% in two years and 12.5% in five. It lists current averages against proposed caps: individual health first-sale commission averages 24% against a proposed 15 to 20%; health renewals average 14% against 5 to 10%; motor own-damage new business averages 26% against 5 to 10%; credit-linked insurance would be capped at 2.5% for regular premium. ICRA expects NBFCs and housing finance companies to be hit harder than banks, and consolidation over time. Its overall verdict is \"structurally positive\" with near-term pain."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Nilesh Sathe, a former IRDAI member, told ET BFSI he expects the rules from 1 April 2027 and \"by and large, do not expect change in what is proposed\", maybe a cap of 2.5% moving to 3% or 3.5%. He thinks the whole process must finish in six months and that large insurers such as LIC and SBI Life, whose costs are already under 12.5%, benefit most. He is a former regulator giving a forecast, not a decision."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The other side is an interested party. ET reports brokers warn of a 60 to 70% revenue hit and nearly 10 lakh jobs at risk, against IRDAI's own data that general-insurance broker commissions rose 173% from FY23 to FY25 while broker-sourced premium rose 37%, with the average commission rate going from 8.5% to 17%. Context from the market: life insurers' new business premium rose 21% in September to ₹48,780 cr while policies sold fell slightly to 21.93 lakh from 22.06 lakh, and general insurers' gross direct premium rose 13.22% (The Hindu). Higher premium per policy is exactly the pattern the regulator is questioning."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the timeline you can plan against. The feedback window closes 25 October, and the people who have read the paper expect most of it to survive."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.business-standard.com/finance/insurance/insurance-distribution-reforms-may-weigh-on-near-term-growth-icra-126100801049_1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Business Standard on ICRA</a> / <a href=\"https://bfsi.economictimes.indiatimes.com/articles/irdai-commission-caps-unlikely-this-fiscal-new-rules-may-take-effect-april-1-2027-nilesh-sathe/134778526\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET BFSI, Nilesh Sathe</a> / <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/industry/banking/finance/insure/insurance-brokers-oppose-irdai-commission-caps-warn-of-60-70-revenue-hit-/articleshow/134737989.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times on brokers</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindu.com/business/Industry/life-insurers-new-business-premium-rose-21-in-sept-to-48780-crore/article71560882.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu on September premiums</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Mathematical formulas written in chalk on an old blackboard", "caption": "Chalk formulas on a blackboard, file photo. Photo: Roman Mager (Wikimedia Commons, CC0).", "img": "photos/d4-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "OpenAI published 722 AI-written maths manuscripts, and a mathematicians' group has told members to stop working with it", "kicker": "AI, the scoop of the day"}, {"t": "p", "html": "On 6 October OpenAI put a repository on GitHub of manuscripts and proof artifacts \"produced by an internal OpenAI model\". Its README says the results sit at different stages of verification, not all have Lean formalizations (computer-checked proofs), \"some of the unformalized results could have issues\", and it will fix them quickly. Heise, reading OpenAI's own explanation, reports 722 manuscripts across more than 370 result families, from about 4,000 tasks given to one model, with the independent Advisory Group on Mathematics and AI at the Institute for Advanced Study consulted first. The 722 figure, first reported by TechSpot yesterday, is confirmed by heise and the repo."}, {"t": "p", "html": "On 7 October the Association for Human Mathematics, founded in September and listing over 800 members including Peter Scholze (heise), said \"mathematicians did not ask for this work to be done\", called the release \"a demonstration of power\", and urged mathematicians to stop working with OpenAI. Terence Tao's blog carries the statement as a guest post by the group. The Decoder reports that Tao chairs the group, while a commenter on his blog says he is not a member. Those two accounts conflict, so the statement is attributed here to the group."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Reddit pulse, opinion not fact.</strong> The r/OpenAI thread has about 1.1K comments. Early top comments are sceptical of the statement (\"why does this sound like they are saying AI should not solve math problems\"), while one mocks that the post says it was converted from another file format using AI. On Tao's own blog, the mix is wider. One mathematician says he worked on three of the solved problems and feels relieved, though he says the AI text is not human understanding."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the sharpest public argument yet over who gets to announce AI results and what counts as solved. Expect the same fight over code, legal and financial output you build products on."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://github.com/openai/math\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">OpenAI repo on GitHub</a> / <a href=\"https://terrytao.wordpress.com/2026/10/07/ahm-statement-on-openais-october-6-release-of-mathematical-documents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AHM statement via Terence Tao's blog</a> / <a href=\"https://the-decoder.com/some-mathematicians-call-for-openai-boycott-after-ai-generated-proofs-flood-their-field/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Decoder</a> / <a href=\"https://www.heise.de/en/news/AI-generated-math-solutions-Sharp-criticism-of-OpenAI-s-approach-11480979.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">heise</a> / <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/OpenAI/comments/1x0gqmb/fields_medalist_terence_tao_reposts_statement/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reddit thread, r/OpenAI</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Google launched a Gemini agent with its own Workspace account, for businesses first", "kicker": "AI, agents"}, {"t": "p", "html": "At a Google Cloud event on 8 October, Google announced a unified Gemini agent that takes \"objectives, not just instructions\", per Thomas Kurian. It plans work, uses custom skills and tools, and connects to Workspace, Microsoft 365, Slack, Jira, Git, BigQuery, Snowflake and any Model Context Protocol (MCP) server. TechCrunch reports it has its own Workspace account with its own email address, writes an audit trail attributed to the agent, and shows its work in a \"tasks inbox\". Users can pick the model, starting with Anthropic's Claude. Early testers were On, Shopify and PayPal. Pichai said Gemini has over 1 billion monthly users and nearly 90% of the Fortune 100 use Gemini Enterprise, both company claims. Consumers come later."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is a template for agents that work like colleagues, with an inbox, an audit trail and approvals. If you design agent features, this is the permission model competitors will be compared with."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/08/google-brings-agentic-ai-to-gemini-starting-with-businesses/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch</a> / <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/gemini-at-work/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Google Cloud blog</a>"}]}, {"heading": "What's new radar, last 48 hours", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Launched or announced since Wednesday. PRIMARY means the company's own page. REPORT means a news report. COMPANY POST means the company's own post on X, a vendor claim."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "Mistral Large 4 (model)", "detail": "Public preview 6 Oct, weights promised for the end of the month (27 Oct per VentureBeat). 1 trillion parameters, natively multimodal. PRIMARY plus REPORT. Details and a conflict below."}, {"n": "2", "title": "Anthropic Cyber Mission (programme)", "detail": "8 Oct. Critical Infrastructure Defense Program with 11 founding partners, plus a free OSS Scanner for open-source projects. PRIMARY."}, {"n": "3", "title": "Anthropic and the Genesis Mission (funding)", "detail": "8 Oct. $150 million over three years of Claude access for 15+ US agencies and national labs. PRIMARY, a company commitment."}, {"n": "4", "title": "Gemini agent for business (agent)", "detail": "8 Oct. Google Cloud event. PRIMARY and REPORT, covered above."}, {"n": "5", "title": "SynthID Detector, open to everyone (tool)", "detail": "7 Oct. Check whether an image, video or audio file was made with AI from Google or partners including OpenAI, NVIDIA and Kakao, Apple soon. English only for now. PRIMARY."}, {"n": "6", "title": "EmbeddingGemma 2 (open model)", "detail": "6 Oct. Google's on-device embedding model, now natively multimodal across text, images, audio and video. PRIMARY."}, {"n": "7", "title": "Claude Haiku 5.5 in Cursor (model)", "detail": "7 Oct. Cursor says Anthropic's small model is now in Cursor and costs 10x less than Haiku 4.5 on shorter requests. COMPANY POST, a vendor claim."}, {"n": "8", "title": "ChatGPT for Teens, College Planner (app feature)", "detail": "7 Oct. OpenAI says a college application planner is coming. Its own stats are company-reported. PRIMARY."}, {"n": "9", "title": "Interface by Natura (hardware)", "detail": "8 Oct. A $99 smart ring that triggers AI agents, pre-orders next month, shipping December or January. REPORT, TechCrunch."}, {"n": "10", "title": "Manus 2.0 and Cue (agent apps)", "detail": "Cue gives personal AI agents their own email, phone number, wallet and computer. REPORT, TechCrunch's funding story."}]}]}, {"heading": "AI race, deeper", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "Paris skyline with the Eiffel Tower at sunset", "caption": "Paris at sunset. Mistral is Paris-based per VentureBeat. File photo, not of the company. Photo: Pedro Kümmel (Wikimedia Commons, CC0).", "img": "photos/d4-g2.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Mistral Large 4: a trillion-parameter model with open weights promised, and its own numbers disagree", "kicker": "Models"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Mistral says Large 4 is a 1-trillion-parameter, natively multimodal model, its largest, and that it beats any open-weight model from the US or Europe and is state-of-the-art among open models on cybersecurity, finance and law. The weights come at month end after red-teaming with cybersecurity leaders and state authorities. VentureBeat adds a custom licence and no API pricing in the materials it saw."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The sources disagree on details. Mistral's page says 52 billion active parameters and 3,800 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GPUs. VentureBeat and Mistral's X post say 49 billion active, and VentureBeat says about 4,000 GPUs. The benchmark claims are Mistral's own charts."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If you build, an open-weight model near the frontier means you can run it yourself for sensitive data. The catch is that the weights are not out yet, and the benchmark claims are all Mistral's."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://mistral.ai/news/mistral-large-4/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mistral announcement</a> / <a href=\"https://venturebeat.com/technology/mistral-debuts-large-4-le-chonk-a-1-trillion-parameter-text-output-model-with-high-benchmarks-planned-for-open-weights-release\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">VentureBeat</a> / <a href=\"https://x.com/MistralAI/status/2107457414387622310\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mistral on X</a> / <a href=\"https://www.reddit.com/r/LocalLLaMA/comments/1wzginu/europe_rejoins_the_fight_with_chonky_mistral/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reddit, r/LocalLLaMA</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Anthropic points frontier models at power grids and open-source code, with free scans for open-source projects", "kicker": "Agents and security"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Critical Infrastructure Defense Program puts frontier models, on-site engineers and threat research with the providers that secure operational technology, meaning the control systems behind power grids, water systems and transport. Founding partners are Accenture, Booz Allen, CrowdStrike, Deloitte, Dragos, Hitachi, Insane Cyber, Nozomi Networks, Palo Alto Networks, PwC and Rockwell Automation. A second arm, OSS Scanner, gives open-source projects regular security scans from Anthropic's strongest models for free."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The page admits the limit: Project Glasswing found many vulnerabilities, but \"verifying, prioritizing, and fixing\" remains hard, and Glasswing has been merged into a wider Cyber Verification Program. Separately, Anthropic committed $150 million over three years to the US Genesis Mission, giving Claude and credits to more than 15 agencies including NASA, NIH and NSF. Both are announcements of intent. Neither has results yet."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is a bet that defence, not just attack, is where AI gets adopted by cautious sectors first. It also shows how Anthropic wants to sell to governments and infrastructure operators."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/anthropic-cyber-mission\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic Cyber Mission</a> / <a href=\"https://x.com/AnthropicAI/status/2108302539498414208\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Anthropic on X</a> / <a href=\"https://www.anthropic.com/news/genesis-mission-commitment\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Genesis Mission commitment</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Manus raised more than $500 million, its first round since China forced the end of Meta's $2 billion deal", "kicker": "Companies"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Manus's parent, Butterfly Effect, said in a WeChat post that Boyu Capital and IDG Capital led the round with Tencent, HSG and ZhenFund. The valuation was not disclosed. TechCrunch notes it was reported last month to be in talks at $4 billion. Meta announced the $2 billion acquisition in December, Chinese authorities ordered it unwound in April, and Manus resumed independent operations in August. It is reportedly considering a Hong Kong listing. Everything here is from one outlet's report of the company's post."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It shows what happens to an agent startup when geopolitics reverses its exit. Manus is also the closest peer to the 'agents with their own email and wallet' idea."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/08/chinas-manus-raises-over-500m-in-first-funding-round-since-split-with-meta/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "A large oil tanker at sea under a grey sky", "caption": "An oil tanker in the North Sea, file photo. Photo: ImagePerson (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d4-g3.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Yesterday's recap", "kicker": "India, Thursday 8 October"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Nifty 50", "v": "22,231.80, down 371.25 points (1.64%). Opened 22,599.05, which was also the day high. Low 22,179.90, a new 52-week low."}, {"k": "Sensex", "v": "71,593.24, down 1,045.46 points (1.44%)"}, {"k": "Bank Nifty", "v": "54,515.05, down 0.98%"}, {"k": "Midcap and smallcap", "v": "Nifty Midcap 100 down 2.53%, Smallcap 100 down 2.34% (NineFifteenAM)"}, {"k": "Volatility", "v": "India VIX 15.28, up 10.01%"}, {"k": "Breadth", "v": "Zerodha counts 2,068 decliners to 248 advancers and 68 new 52-week lows to 1 high. NineFifteenAM, quoting HDFC Sky for all NSE shares, counts 3,280 falling and 951 rising. The universes differ."}, {"k": "Sectors", "v": "All sector indices fell. IT -0.08%, Financial Services -1.11%, FMCG -2.02%, Pharma -2.32%, Auto -2.49%, Energy -2.82%, Realty -3.16%, Metal -3.55%."}, {"k": "Flows", "v": "FPIs net sold about ₹12,943 cr (provisional BSE data via Mint). The Wednesday figure, now final on 5paisa, was a net ₹6,121.40 cr sale."}, {"k": "Rupee and crude", "v": "Rupee about 96.8 per dollar. Moneycontrol has 96.78 against 96.77, HDFC Sky via NineFifteenAM has 96.88 after a 13 paise fall. The two sources differ. Brent about $104 a barrel across sources."}, {"k": "TCS, after the close", "v": "September-quarter net profit ₹13,884 cr, up 14.98% on the year, dividend ₹12 a share, per Upstox via NineFifteenAM. Total contract value $9.6 billion and headcount 598,056 are also from that report."}]}, {"t": "story", "title": "Overseas panel", "kicker": "Global, as reported on 8 October"}, {"t": "p", "html": "US stock index futures fell on Thursday as oil rose and Treasury yields stayed near multi-year highs, with the 10-year at 5.34%, close to its highest since 2002, per Reuters on Kitco. Megacap tech and chip stocks were down, and Reuters notes a WSJ report that Broadcom is lining up $50 billion of financing for OpenAI, which that report says has raised fears of heavy tech debt issuance. XTB has Brent above $104, WTI near $92, the Euro Stoxx 50 down 1.1%, gold near $4,120 an ounce, and Samsung shares down about 2.5% despite record preliminary quarterly operating profit of KRW 107.4 trillion. These are intraday readings, not official closes."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Today's watch list", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Events first: Friday 9 October is the first session after TCS's results, Monday 12 October brings HCLTech's results, Wednesday 14 October is the TCS dividend record date, Tuesday 20 October is an NSE holiday and Infosys reports on Friday 23 October (NineFifteenAM's calendar). Each name is listed because of a reported development. No view on direction is implied. Closes are 8 October from NineFifteenAM's exchange data unless marked."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "TCS", "detail": "Closed ₹2,076, down 0.21%, before results. Net profit ₹13,884 cr after the close."}, {"n": "2", "title": "Infosys", "detail": "₹997.00, up 0.50%. One of only three Nifty gainers. Reports 23 October."}, {"n": "3", "title": "Tech Mahindra", "detail": "₹1,496.20, up 0.34%, also a gainer."}, {"n": "4", "title": "HCL Technologies", "detail": "Reports Monday 12 October."}, {"n": "5", "title": "Axis Bank", "detail": "₹1,245.00, up 0.20%, the third gainer."}, {"n": "6", "title": "HDFC Bank", "detail": "₹692.25, down 1.49%. Mint notes DII holding (43.44%) now exceeds FII holding (39.39%) for the first time in over 10 years."}, {"n": "7", "title": "Claude Haiku 5.5 in Cursor (model)", "detail": "7 Oct. Cursor says Anthropic's small model is now in Cursor and costs 10x less than Haiku 4.5 on shorter requests. COMPANY POST, a vendor claim."}, {"n": "8", "title": "ITC", "detail": "₹255.00, down 4.03%. Mint reports bulk deals by GQG-managed funds with mutual funds buying."}, {"n": "9", "title": "Adani Enterprises", "detail": "₹2,596.00, down 5.36%, the biggest Nifty loser. NineFifteenAM found no company-specific reason."}, {"n": "10", "title": "Adani Green Energy", "detail": "Down 7.88% on about 4.1 times usual volume (Zerodha)."}, {"n": "13", "title": "InterGlobe Aviation (IndiGo)", "detail": "₹4,813.10, down 3.55%, with crude above $104."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Tata Motors Passenger Vehicles", "detail": "₹273.00, down 3.53%."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Power Grid", "detail": "₹244.65, down 3.32%."}, {"n": "16", "title": "Jubilant FoodWorks", "detail": "Down 7.16% on heavy volume (Zerodha)."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Vodafone Idea", "detail": "Implied volatility rose 6 points to 47.5%, the biggest jump in Zerodha's tracker."}, {"n": "18", "title": "PB Fintech", "detail": "Policybazaar's parent. June-quarter profit ₹163 cr, up 92% (Fortune India). No price pulled. See today's long read."}, {"n": "19", "title": "LIC", "detail": "Sathe says its costs are already below the proposed 12.5% life expense ceiling. No price pulled."}, {"n": "20", "title": "SBI Life", "detail": "Named alongside LIC for the same reason. No price pulled."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://ninefifteenam.com/notes/post-market-report-8-october-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NineFifteenAM</a>, <a href=\"https://www.moneycontrol.com/news/business/markets/taking-stock-nifty-hits-fresh-52-week-low-ends-below-22-250-sensex-tanks-1-045-pts-14047706.html/amp\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Moneycontrol</a>, <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/market/nifty-50-performance-in-2026-fii-fpi-selling-inflation-rbi-nse-crude-oil-11791461972358.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint</a>, <a href=\"https://aftermarketreport.zerodha.com/p/nifty-sinks-to-fresh-52-week-low\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Zerodha Aftermarket Report</a>, <a href=\"https://justticks.in/fii-dii-data\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">flows tracker</a>, <a href=\"https://www.kitco.com/news/off-the-wire/2026-10-08/wall-st-futures-decline-oil-surge-high-yields-stoke-inflation-worries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reuters via Kitco</a>, <a href=\"https://www.xtb.com/en/market-analysis/market-wrap-a-4-surge-in-oil-prices-weighs-on-indices-08-10-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">XTB</a>. Technical levels quoted by brokers in these reports are omitted on purpose."}]}, {"heading": "Finance goldmine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Why oil, bond yields and the rate hike landed on the same day", "kicker": "Mechanism"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India imports most of its oil, so dearer crude pushes up inflation, the import bill and the rupee's weakness together. The RBI had just raised rates and moved to \"calibrated tightening\", which tells markets cuts are off the table. Outlook Business quotes a broker saying crude above $100 matters for margins across oil-sensitive sectors. Meanwhile US yields near 5.3% make safer assets pay more, which Mint says is pulling foreign money out of emerging-market stocks. SBI Securities' head of research, quoted by Mint, says the biggest risk is crude staying high for longer. DSP's CEO says domestic demand looks healthy and valuations are closer to fair. Both are opinions from market participants."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It explains why every sector fell, even IT, which was nearly flat only because it has dollar earnings."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.outlookbusiness.com/markets/nifty-hits-2026-low-what-triggered-the-market-bloodbath\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Outlook Business</a> / <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/market/nifty-50-performance-in-2026-fii-fpi-selling-inflation-rbi-nse-crude-oil-11791461972358.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint</a> / <a href=\"https://www.xtb.com/en/market-analysis/market-wrap-a-4-surge-in-oil-prices-weighs-on-indices-08-10-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">XTB</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "India's solar factories are running at 35 to 40% as US tariffs shut their biggest market", "kicker": "Business"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India has 233 GW of solar module capacity against about 50 GW of yearly domestic demand, and factories run at 35 to 40% against the 50 to 65% needed to be sustainable, per a report Mint cites. US shipments of Indian solar products were $61.47 million from April to July, against $646.23 million a year earlier, after duties of up to 126% (the report cites combined duties above 200% for most makers). The renewable energy secretary said the ministry is working with Exim Bank on credit lines for African and Latin American buyers. Chinese suppliers already sell there at low prices, as the same report notes. The plan is a proposal, not an announced scheme."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is a clean case of a policy shock to a business that grew for one customer. The government's answer, export credit lines to Africa and Latin America, is also a product opportunity for trade finance and insurance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/industry/energy/india-plans-exim-bank-credit-lines-to-rescue-solar-exports-after-us-tariff-blow-11791453975856.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "An AI financing question: the debt behind the compute", "kicker": "Global"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Reuters, on Kitco, says investors are weighing expensive money and high yields against strong earnings and AI spending, and notes the WSJ report of $50 billion of Broadcom financing for OpenAI, with Oracle also seeking funds. The amounts are as relayed by Reuters."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If AI capital spending is increasingly funded with debt, rising yields change who can afford to keep building. That is a risk to track, not a conclusion."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.kitco.com/news/off-the-wire/2026-10-08/wall-st-futures-decline-oil-surge-high-yields-stoke-inflation-worries\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reuters via Kitco</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "DailyObjects raised ₹332 cr at about ₹1,050 cr valuation while its loss widened", "kicker": "Insight"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Xponentia, Anicut and Axiom Asia led the Series C, a mix of primary and secondary. Roots Ventures partly exited at 18x. The company plans 150 exclusive stores in five years from about 350 touchpoints today. FY25 operating revenue was ₹110 cr, up 31%, and total expenses rose 29.7% to ₹124.5 cr, so the loss widened to ₹16 cr from ₹10 cr (company financials per Entrackr). By our calculation the valuation is about 9.5 times FY25 operating revenue. That multiple is our calculation on FY25 revenue."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> It is a useful case of a brand paying for growth with partly secondary money. An early investor took an 18x return while the company is still loss-making, which says what late-stage investors will pay for retail and brand scale."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://entrackr.com/news/dailyobjects-raises-rs-332-cr-in-series-c-round-led-by-xponentia-anicut-axiom-asia-12638318\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Entrackr</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Miko's maker is selling its robot AI as foundation models to other machine makers, and is closing ₹300 cr", "kicker": "Deeptech"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Inner Sky Labs, which makes Miko children's robots, launched models for vision, audio, touch, action and safety, meant for robots and other physical systems. It is closing about ₹300 cr (about $30.9 million) from existing backers and new global investors, which would take total funding to $100 million. The CEO says it will sell to enterprises and governments that want sovereign AI. The round is not closed and the capabilities are company claims."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> An Indian company that spent a decade building in stealth is turning a consumer product's AI into a platform. Watch whether others buy it."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/buzz/miko-maker-inner-sky-labs-unveils-foundation-models-for-physical-ai/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Vesta's mortgage agents raised $30 million in the US", "kicker": "Pattern"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Vesta raised $30 million led by Conversion Capital, with customers Pennymac and New American Funding also investing. The CEO says revenue is up 12x year on year, that it has helped originate more than $100 billion a year in loans, and that it holds under 5% of the market. All of that is company-reported. Customers begin with a person approving the agent's work, then let it handle a share on its own. Every action and its reasoning is logged for audit."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Its model is the one that works for regulated work: start with a person approving each agent action, then widen autonomy. That maps directly to insurance servicing."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://techcrunch.com/2026/10/08/vesta-raises-30m-as-lenders-adopt-ai-agents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TechCrunch</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Opportunities", "kicker": "Where the gap might be"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Audit trails for agents in regulated work. Both Google and Vesta lean on logged, attributed agent actions. Tools that make that log readable for a compliance officer are a plausible need.", "Trade credit and insurance for redirected exports. If India offers credit lines to new solar buyers, someone has to underwrite the risk.", "Verification of AI output. The maths row is a preview: when results arrive faster than people can check them, checking becomes the scarce service."]}, {"t": "story", "title": "A suitability record for insurance distributors", "kicker": "One idea, a hypothesis to test, not a validated market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "If IRDAI's draft keeps its proposed suitability rules and clawbacks for proven mis-selling, distributors will need to show what advice they gave and why. The idea is a light tool that records each customer conversation's needs analysis, the product shown and the reason for the recommendation, then attaches it to the policy. Vesta's pattern, with a person approving first, is the way to start. The test: ask five mid-size brokers whether they could produce that record for a random policy sold last year. If most cannot, there is a need. If most say they already can, there is none."}]}, {"heading": "Vibe coding", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Cursor lets you watch and steer your local coding agents from your phone", "kicker": "Tools"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Cursor's changelog says Remote Control for local agents is on by default for everyone except Enterprise organisations. You sign in on the Cursor iOS app, approve pairing on the desktop app, see your local agents, and reply to them. The agents keep running on the computer. It must stay on, and Cursor suggests plugging it in with the lid open. The same page earlier describes Rollouts and Security Review bots from 23 September, available on Teams and Enterprise plans."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The pattern is agents that keep running on your machine while you supervise remotely. It is useful if you start long builds and walk away, but your computer has to stay awake and online."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://cursor.com/changelog\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cursor changelog</a> / <a href=\"https://x.com/cursor_ai/status/2107897245282799864\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cursor on X, Haiku 5.5</a>"}]}, {"heading": "AI hack of the day", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Turn a weekly chore into an automation you can check", "kicker": "Friday: automation"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Most automations fail quietly. The fix is to design the check before the action. Paste this into your assistant with a chore you do every week, such as collecting renewal dates or summarising a team channel. The prompt and the method are mine, not from a source."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Copy and pasteI do this chore every week: [describe it in two sentences]. Design it as an automation with four parts. 1) Trigger: what starts it and how often. 2) Steps: each step as one action with one input and one output. 3) Check: for each step, one thing I can look at in under ten seconds to know it worked, and what a silent failure looks like. 4) Stop rule: when it should stop and message me instead of continuing. Then list what could go wrong if the input is empty, duplicated or late. Do not build anything yet. Ask me up to three questions first."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Why it works: the model is good at listing failure modes if you ask for them before it writes anything. Run it on one chore this weekend and keep only the steps whose check you would actually look at."}]}, {"heading": "Startup case study", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "One startup story per edition, told in full. Today's is Policybazaar: how an insurance comparison site became India's largest online insurance seller, where the founders disagree with the record, and why it matters to the IRDAI debate. Read it here: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/21gxaz5puc1d-how-policybazaar-built-a-market\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How Policybazaar built a market</a>. Each edition's case study will be a different company."}]}, {"heading": "Source notes and caveats", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["AI leads were found on the public X profiles of AI labs and tools (latest posts only) and checked against company pages or news reports. Reddit reactions are small samples of opinion.", "Update to yesterday: the 722-manuscripts figure is now confirmed by the OpenAI repo and heise."]}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street with its red BSE sign", "caption": "Bombay Stock Exchange, Dalal Street. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}}, "5": {"title": "Morning paper, Saturday 10 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 5 of 7. Markets, an AI agent wave, startup funding and the money hack.", "intro": "The Nifty snapped its eight-week losing streak with a 1.30% rally led by IT after TCS's results, foreign investors kept selling but gently, Jio Platforms' record IPO took shape, and Google gave its work agent an email address of its own.", "groups": [{"heading": "Eight things to know", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["The Nifty rose 1.30% to 22,520.45 on Friday and the Sensex 879 points to 72,472.33, ending an eight-week losing streak. IT led, up more than 3%, on TCS's results.", "TCS's annualised AI revenue reached $3.1 billion, over 10% of total revenue, up from $2.6 billion a quarter earlier. Profit rose 14.9% to ₹13,884 crore.", "Foreign investors sold for a tenth straight session, but only ₹3,569 crore against Thursday's ₹12,944 crore. Domestic institutions bought ₹4,743 crore.", "September inflation lands Monday. Economists expect 5.6%, a 23-month high, in the first reading after the RBI's rate hike.", "Jio Platforms is lining up India's largest IPO at about ₹37,700 crore, with the public issue expected to open around 21 October (Economic Times).", "Google announced Gemini Agent, a general work agent that gets its own email address, audit trail and spending limits. Limited preview for now.", "Odyssey launched Odyssey-3, a world model claiming the top score on a physics benchmark, with a free browser preview. Numbers are vendor-reported, labelled inside.", "Saturday money hack inside: the SBI chairman says deposit rates will not rise for up to three months. What that window means for your FD renewals."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Inside:</strong> Front page / What's new radar / AI race / Markets, with two charts / Finance goldmine / Startup intelligence / Vibe coding / Money hack / Case study / Seven-day calendar / Notes."}]}, {"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The Nifty snapped its eight-week losing streak with a 1.30% relief rally", "kicker": "Money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Nifty 50 rose 288.65 points, or 1.30%, to close at 22,520.45 on Friday, and the Sensex rose 879.09 points, or 1.23%, to 72,472.33 (The Hindu BusinessLine). For the week the Nifty gained about 100 points, avoiding a ninth straight weekly loss; over the eight-week slide it had lost 8.7% (Economic Times). Every sector index rose, market breadth was 47 advances to 3 declines, and India VIX, the volatility index, fell 5.37% to 14.46."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The rally was a mix of oversold buying and short covering after TCS's results, with FMCG and auto joining IT (ET, quoting Geojit and PL Capital). Sentiment also got help from crude cooling: Brent fell 1.32% to $102.9 a barrel, and ET's sources link that to indications that US military action against Iran is unlikely before the midterm elections. The rupee firmed. The same report carries the caution: <a href=\"https://nseindia.com/reports/fii-dii\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">foreign portfolio investors</a> (overseas funds investing in Indian markets) kept selling, US 10-year yields stayed above 5%, and Geojit's Vinod Nair calls this a relief rally that needs confirmation from earnings and inflation data."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> After Thursday's 18-month-low close, this is a breather, not an all-clear. Monday's inflation print decides whether the recovery gets a second leg, and the same forces (oil, US yields, foreign selling) are still in place."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/sensex-nifty50-today-stock-market-live-updates-9th-october-2026/article71560336.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu BusinessLine</a> / <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/friday-heavy-lifting-saves-nifty-from-record-nine-weeks-of-losses-can-bulls-take-charge-now/articleshow/134830751.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "TCS's $3.1 billion AI business carried the IT rally", "kicker": "Earnings"}, {"t": "p", "html": "TCS's September-quarter numbers, filed on 8 October: revenue ₹73,188 crore, up 11.2%, profit ₹13,884 crore, up 14.9%, operating margin steady at 24.0%, and deal wins of $9.6 billion. The line the market traded on Friday was annualised AI revenue of $3.1 billion, over 10% of total revenue, up from $2.6 billion in the June quarter. Note the unit: annualised run rate, not revenue earned in the quarter. The stock rose as much as 4.19% intraday on Friday and Nifty IT closed up more than 3%."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The sober details, from the same filing: dollar revenue of $7.64 billion was TCS's slowest second-quarter growth in three years, brokers trimmed margin estimates after the call, and CFO Samir Seksaria said the MHP acquisition (€320 million) will shave about 50 basis points off margins in the second half. A subsidiary agreed to acquire MHP Management- und IT-Beratung, Porsche's consulting and IT arm, and the Best Buy deal moves that retailer's India tech centre into a TCS AI Capability Centre."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the clearest revenue proof yet that AI services sell at scale, and it arrives right before HCLTech, Wipro and Tech Mahindra report next week. Read those against this bar."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter, reading the TCS filing</a> / <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/sensex-nifty50-today-stock-market-live-updates-9th-october-2026/article71560336.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu BusinessLine</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Foreign investors sold a tenth straight session, but gently", "kicker": "Flows"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Foreign portfolio investors net sold ₹3,569 crore on Friday while domestic institutions net bought ₹4,743 crore (TBTflow). That is a tenth consecutive session of foreign selling, but far below Thursday's ₹12,944 crore, the biggest single-day sale since 29 May. Foreign investors have now net sold ₹57,979 crore across September and October to 8 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The streak is the story, not the day. Domestic buyers absorbed another session, which is why the index could rise while foreigners sold."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://tbtflow.com/daily/fii-dii-data-today\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TBTflow, 9 October</a> / <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter, series to 8 October</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "2425282930156789-12,944-3,569Sessions: Sep 24, 25, 28, 29, 30; Oct 1, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9 (2026)FII netDII netUnit: ₹ crore per session. Source: NSDL/NSE provisional via Zerodha AMR (to 8 Oct); TBTflow (9 Oct)."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Jio Platforms is lining up India's largest IPO at about ₹37,700 crore", "kicker": "Primary market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Jio Platforms is expected to file its red herring prospectus, the detailed offer document, after 12 October, with the anchor book opening 19 October, the public issue 21 to 23 October, allotment 26 October and listing targeted for 28 October, the Economic Times reports, citing people familiar with the plans. The expected price band is ₹1,150 to ₹1,220 a share, raising about $4 billion (₹37,700 crore), mostly to prepay borrowings. Akash Ambani said publicly this week that Jio wants its IPO to be India's largest. All dates and the band are ET's reporting of unnamed sources, not a filed document; treat them as the company's tentative calendar."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> An issue this size pulls money from everywhere else in the market. The dates also tell you when brokerages, and the indices, start repositioning."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/ipos/fpos/jio-platforms-plans-indias-largest-ipo-at-rs-37700-crore-around-dussehra/articleshow/134720381.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times, 6 October</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Google's Gemini Agent, a day on: the coworker design in detail", "kicker": "AI, agents"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Yesterday's edition covered the announcement. GIGAZINE's report, published 9 October from the Gemini at Work event on 8 October, fills in the design: Gemini Agent is a general-purpose work agent integrated into Gemini Enterprise. Give it a goal and it plans the steps, then works across Gmail, Drive, Docs, Sheets and Calendar, plus Microsoft 365, Slack and Salesforce. It can research a market, build the spreadsheet and compile the deck as one job, and it keeps working in the cloud after you close your laptop (GIGAZINE's report of the event)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The governance design is the interesting half. \"Coworker agents\" get their own email address, calendar and Drive, so employees can assign them work through chat or document comments. Every agent gets a cryptographic identity in an Agent Registry, actions are logged, an Agent Gateway applies internal security rules to external calls, and project-level spending caps pause the agent when hit. Users can pick models per task, including Google's Gemini 4 Argon or Anthropic's Claude Opus 5.5. Industry versions for financial services and legal work are in preview. General availability has no date."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is the template for agents inside regulated workplaces, including insurance operations: a named identity, an audit trail, spending limits and a gateway for external calls. Whoever builds agent features will be compared with this permission model."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://gigazine.net/gsc_news/en/20261009-gemini-agent/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">GIGAZINE, 9 October</a> / <a href=\"https://blog.google/innovation-and-ai/infrastructure-and-cloud/google-cloud/gemini-at-work/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Google Cloud blog</a>"}]}, {"heading": "What's new radar, last 48 hours", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Launched or announced since Thursday. PRIMARY means the company's own page. REPORT means a news report. COMPANY BLOG means a company post. Vendor-reported numbers are labelled as such."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "Gemini Agent (Google)", "detail": "Announced 8 Oct, limited preview. Work agent with identity, audit log and spend caps. PRIMARY plus REPORT. Covered above."}, {"n": "2", "title": "Odyssey-3 (world model)", "detail": "8 Oct. Claims the top Physics-IQ Verified score, 66.1 video-to-video (benchmark of real physics experiments, by Anates Labs and DeepMind). Free browser preview; no open weights or pricing. PRIMARY plus REPORT. Details below."}, {"n": "3", "title": "Mellum2.1 (JetBrains)", "detail": "8 Oct. Open coding model, Apache 2.0, trained with reinforcement learning for agent work. On Hugging Face. PRIMARY. In Vibe coding below."}, {"n": "4", "title": "Terra (Exia Labs)", "detail": "9 Oct, preview at a16z's Speedrun. Geospatial AI agent: site selection, weather impact, maps from natural language. COMPANY BLOG."}, {"n": "5", "title": "Autoloop (Kore.ai)", "detail": "9 Oct. Optimization engine that keeps AI agents performing in production. REPORT, CXOToday."}, {"n": "6", "title": "Ling-3.1-Flash (Ant Group's Antling)", "detail": "8 Oct. Available on OpenRouter, free until 13 October. REPORT, Coincamps."}]}]}, {"heading": "AI race, deeper", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Odyssey-3: a world model you can walk around in, with a physics score to defend", "kicker": "Models"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Odyssey-3, released 8 October, is an autoregressive diffusion transformer that generates an environment from a prompt and predicts in real time how it changes as you act in it. Odyssey says the Pro version scores 66.1 on Physics-IQ Verified's video-to-video benchmark, the highest reported, and 54.7 image-to-video; Physics-IQ tests whether models can continue videos of real physics experiments. The company also claims first place in 3 of 4 WorldMark categories, which is its own evaluation, so treat that one as self-reported. The free research preview runs a faster Flash variant in the browser, with first and third-person navigation; API access is by request, with no open weights or public pricing. For robotics, Odyssey says an action decoder trained on tens of hours of demos can control robot arms, including recovery behaviours absent from training data."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> World models are the next claimed route to robotics and agents that understand physical space. A free browser preview means you can test the claim yourself this weekend."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://odyssey.systems/meet-odyssey-3\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Odyssey announcement</a> / <a href=\"https://aicoder.com/news/news-20261009-odyssey-3-world-model-physics-iq-sota\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">AICoder report</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The AI buildout is showing up in chip profits", "kicker": "Business"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Samsung projected third-quarter operating profit of 107.4 trillion won, about $80.2 billion, almost nine times the year-earlier level, on demand for AI memory chips; that would be a record quarterly profit for a technology company. TSMC reported record third-quarter revenue of NT$1.49 trillion, about $46.7 billion, up 50% year on year, also on AI chip demand."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> When the money reaches memory chips and foundries at this scale, the AI spending cycle is real revenue, not only valuations. It also tells you where the bottlenecks, and prices, go next."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter, from company reports</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Enterprise AI spending between Anthropic and OpenAI is now roughly even", "kicker": "Companies"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Across roughly 120,000 companies using Anthropic and OpenAI tools, spending was about evenly split between the two in September, against about 75% for Anthropic at the start of the year. The same report says Anthropic is working towards an IPO as soon as November. Both are as reported; the spending split is tracked data, not company disclosure."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> At the start of the year Anthropic held about three-quarters of this spending. Parity in nine months tells you how fast enterprise budgets are being re-decided, and both vendors now court the same regulated buyers you sell to."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter, citing tracked company data</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "India's minister asks for an AI safety consultation paper", "kicker": "Policy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Union minister Ashwini Vaishnaw said: \"The time has come for AI regulations. I'll request the department to come out with a regulation-focused consultation paper, which focuses on safety. That's the most important point: safety related to AI use.\" A request for a paper, not a draft law."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A consultation paper is the first step of Indian AI regulation. If your products touch customer data or advice, the definitions in that paper will matter."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter, quote</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "figure", "alt": "A large oil tanker at sea under a grey sky", "caption": "Crude cooled 1.32% to $102.9 a barrel on Friday, which helped the rally. An oil tanker in the North Sea, file photo. Photo: ImagePerson (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d4-g3.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The session in numbers", "kicker": "India, Friday 9 October, closing"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Nifty 50", "v": "22,520.45, up 288.65 points (1.30%). Weekly gain of about 100 points ends eight weeks of losses."}, {"k": "Sensex", "v": "72,472.33, up 879.09 points (1.23%). Day high 72,669.20."}, {"k": "Breadth", "v": "47 advances to 3 declines on the Nifty. Smallcaps rose 0.5%, midcaps 0.1%."}, {"k": "Volatility", "v": "India VIX 14.46, down 5.37%."}, {"k": "Sectors", "v": "All 15 sector indices rose. IT up more than 3%, FMCG 2.22%, PSU banks 1.41%."}, {"k": "Flows", "v": "FII net sale ₹3,569 cr; DII net buy ₹4,743 cr (TBTflow). Tenth straight session of FII selling."}, {"k": "Crude and currency", "v": "Brent $102.9 a barrel, down 1.32%. The rupee firmed (ET). Thursday’s close was 96.88 per dollar."}, {"k": "Gold and silver", "v": "22-carat gold ₹13,895 a gram, up ₹105 (retail). Silver ₹2,24,859 a kg in futures, up 1.64%."}, {"k": "Leaders and laggards", "v": "Top Nifty gainers: TCS, Adani Ports, Infosys, HCLTech, L&T, HDFC Bank. Reliance was the only blue-chip to fall."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "IT+3.00%FMCG+2.22%PSU Bank+1.41%Auto+1.36%Private Bank+1.35%Media+1.23%Day change at close, %, 9 Oct 2026. IT \"more than 3%\", shown as 3.0. Source: Hindu BusinessLine."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The week ahead for markets", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Monday brings September inflation (expectation: 5.6%, a 23-month high, per a Mint poll of 19 economists) and HCLTech's results. Wednesday brings wholesale inflation, US and China inflation, the TCS dividend record date and the Fusion CX IPO opening. Wipro and Persistent Systems report Thursday, Tech Mahindra Friday. The full calendar is at the end. No view on direction is implied."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/markets/sensex-nifty50-today-stock-market-live-updates-9th-october-2026/article71560336.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu BusinessLine, 9 Oct</a>, <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/friday-heavy-lifting-saves-nifty-from-record-nine-weeks-of-losses-can-bulls-take-charge-now/articleshow/134830751.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times, 9 Oct</a>, <a href=\"https://tbtflow.com/daily/fii-dii-data-today\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TBTflow</a>, <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter</a>."}]}, {"heading": "Finance goldmine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The GST overhaul has a date: 1 April 2027, with 90% of refunds on autopilot", "kicker": "Policy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The GST Council's procedural overhaul, approved Thursday, is targeted to take effect 1 April 2027. Every refund claim must be acknowledged within 10 days, and for exporters and inverted-duty cases, about two-thirds of claims, 90% of the refund releases automatically within three days after a risk check. Rates are unchanged; officers' arrest powers go; credit expands to employee health and life insurance, telecom towers and pipelines. A committee has three months to study input credit for honest buyers whose suppliers defaulted."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If you sell to businesses, their cash flow improves when refunds arrive in days, not months. And input tax credit on employee health and life insurance is a direct tailwind for group cover, your industry's product."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Banks are in no hurry to raise deposit rates", "kicker": "Banking"}, {"t": "p", "html": "SBI chairman C.S. Setty said he does not expect deposit rates to rise for up to three months because the banking system still has excess liquidity, and that the RBI's tighter stance could help banks expand net interest margins, the gap between lending and deposit rates, for up to three quarters. That is the chairman of the State Bank of India describing his own pricing room."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Direct input to the money hack below: the largest bank's chairman is telling you the repricing clock."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "A 30% margin cap on cancer drugs goes to the Supreme Court on Monday", "kicker": "Health"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Centre is preparing to cap trade margins on all non-scheduled anti-cancer medicines at 30% of MRP. Officials say this could cut retail prices by as much as 70% and save patients ₹2,500 to 3,500 crore a year. The Supreme Court hears the matter on 12 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Cheaper cancer drugs mean lower claim costs for health insurers, which feeds into premiums and product design. Directly your sector."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Two more worth knowing", "kicker": "Briefs"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India is considering delaying the planned 0.4% merchant fee on UPI payments above ₹2,000 by a few months. And the microfinance loan book fell 11% to ₹3.34 trillion in FY26, with loans overdue by 180 days or more rising to 17.04% from 10.67%, stress at the bottom of the credit pyramid while markets watch AI."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://fin.preparoo.app/daystarter-october-09-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Preparoo DayStarter</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Voice AI for BFSI keeps getting funded: Desible.ai raises ₹32 crore from Prime Venture Partners", "kicker": "Funding, your sector"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Desible.ai raised ₹32 crore in a Seed Plus round from Prime Venture Partners to build what it calls an AI operating layer for BFSI, including voice agents (CNBC-TV18, Inc42). The capabilities are company claims; the round and investor are reported by both outlets."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Investors are paying for agents that handle regulated call volume in banking, financial services and insurance. If IRDAI's commission draft squeezes distribution economics, automation of servicing calls is where the cost pressure lands first."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/startup/desible-ai-raises-rs32-crore-from-prime-venture-partners-to-build-ai-operating-layer-for-bfsi-20007709.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a> / <a href=\"https://inc42.com/buzz/desible-ai-raises-%E2%82%B932-cr-to-build-ai-operating-layer-for-bfsi/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The rest of the funding wire", "kicker": "Funding"}, {"t": "p", "html": "CurvetAI, a Pune startup building AI agents for complex tasks, raised ₹6 crore (Indian Startup News). Quanfluence raised $10 million led by Chiratae Ventures to build a full-stack photonic quantum computer in India (DeepTech India). Gramiyaa, a wood-pressed oil brand, raised a Series A at a ₹110 crore valuation (Entrackr). NeoGrowth, an MSME lender, raised ₹85 crore led by Dutch development bank FMO, and devtools company Harness acquired assets of Augment Code, including its Cosmos coding agent, which becomes the Harness Cosmos Software Factory Agent (YourStory)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://indianstartupnews.com/funding/pune-ai-startup-curvetai-raises-rs-6-crore-to-build-ai-agents-that-can-handle-complex-tasks-12644454\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Startup News</a> / <a href=\"https://thedeeptech.in/article/quanfluence-raises-10-million-to-build-a-full-stack-photonic-quantum-computer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DeepTech India</a> / <a href=\"https://entrackr.com/exclusive/exclusive-gramiyaa-raises-fresh-capital-in-series-a-round-at-rs-110-cr-valuation-12658745\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Entrackr</a> / <a href=\"https://yourstory.com/2026/10/startup-news-and-updates-daily-roundup-october-9-2026\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YourStory roundup</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Opportunities", "kicker": "Where the gap might be"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Compliance-grade voice agents for insurance servicing. Desible's round says buyers exist; the differentiator in BFSI is an audit trail a regulator accepts, which is exactly the design Google chose for Gemini Agent.", "Refund-readiness tooling for SMEs. From April 2027, 90% of GST refunds release automatically after a risk check. Businesses with messy filings will fail those checks; someone sells the clean-up.", "Quantum-adjacent talent and tooling. Quanfluence's $10 million says Indian photonics is fundable; the picks-and-shovels layer (testing, control software) is earlier than the computers themselves."]}, {"t": "story", "title": "A refund-readiness check for small exporters", "kicker": "One idea, a hypothesis to test, not a validated market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The GST change makes refund speed depend on passing an automated risk check. The idea: a tool that reads a small exporter's filings and flags the mismatches that would fail the check, before the claim is filed. The test: ask five SME exporters whether their last refund was delayed and why. If delays trace to fixable filing errors, there is a product. If refunds already arrive on time, there is none."}]}, {"heading": "Vibe coding", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Mellum2.1: an open coding model small enough to run yourself, trained the agent way", "kicker": "Open models"}, {"t": "p", "html": "JetBrains released Mellum2.1, a 12-billion-parameter mixture-of-experts model, a design where only part of the network runs per token, with 2.5 billion active parameters, under the Apache 2.0 licence. The architecture is unchanged from Mellum2; the work went into post-training with reinforcement learning inside thousands of sandboxed coding environments, so the model can explore a repository, edit files and check its own changes. JetBrains' own benchmarks show it beating Qwen3.5-9B and Gemma 4 E4B on agentic coding, and serving almost twice as many tokens under load; those are vendor-run comparisons. It is on Hugging Face now, with llama.cpp, Ollama and LM Studio builds coming."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If you build with coding agents, a capable open model means your code never leaves your hardware, and sub-agents cost nothing per call. This one is built exactly for that pattern."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://blog.jetbrains.com/ai/2026/10/mellum2-1-gets-to-work-a-fast-open-model-for-coding-agents/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">JetBrains blog</a>"}]}, {"heading": "AI hack of the day", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Use the three-month deposit window for your FD renewals", "kicker": "Saturday: money"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The RBI has raised the repo rate, the rate at which banks borrow from it, to 5.5% and shifted to calibrated tightening. Normally deposit rates follow. But the SBI chairman says excess liquidity means banks will not raise deposit rates for up to three months (attributed above). That gap is the window: if you lock a long fixed deposit today, you book today's rate just before a likely repricing. The mechanism, not a product recommendation."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "The weekend task, 20 minutesList every FD you hold with its maturity date. For any maturing in the next 90 days, plan a short renewal (six to twelve months) rather than a long one, so the money is free to rebook if banks raise rates after the liquidity absorbs. Keep emergency cash in a sweep-in or liquid option so you never break an FD early, since premature withdrawal usually costs a penalty on the rate. Recheck the decision when the next RBI policy or the big banks' rate cards change."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The same logic, reversed, is why banks like this window: they keep deposits cheap while loan rates adjust, which is the margin expansion Setty described. Knowing which side of the trade you are on is the hack."}]}, {"heading": "Startup case study", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "One business teardown per edition. Today's is Zerodha: how a bootstrapped brokerage built a ₹22,679 crore cash moat, why its revenue depends on the very options trading the regulator is shrinking, and the five lessons with the evidence behind each. Read it here: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/debmcmlq4ejy-how-zerodha-built-a-moat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">How Zerodha built a moat, and what happens when regulation taxes it</a>."}]}, {"heading": "Seven-day calendar, 10 to 17 October", "blocks": [{"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "12", "title": "Mon: September CPI inflation", "detail": "First price reading after the RBI hike. Economists expect 5.6%, a 23-month high (Mint poll of 19, via DayStarter). A high print keeps rate cuts distant."}, {"n": "12", "title": "Mon: Supreme Court hears cancer-drug margin cap", "detail": "30% trade-margin cap on non-scheduled anti-cancer medicines; prices could fall up to 70% (officials, via DayStarter). Health-claim costs, so insurance-relevant."}, {"n": "12", "title": "Mon: HCLTech Q2 results", "detail": "First big IT read after TCS; board also considers a third interim dividend (Financial Express)."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Wed: Wholesale inflation; US and China CPI", "detail": "India's WPI/PPI and, the same day, US consumer inflation (previous 3.4%) and China's (previous 0.8%), via DayStarter's calendar. US inflation moves the yields that drive FII selling."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Wed: TCS dividend record date; Fusion CX IPO opens", "detail": "₹12 second interim dividend (TCS filing). Fusion CX, a customer-experience company, opens a ₹702 crore IPO at ₹275-289 a share, closing 16 Oct (Economic Times)."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Thu: Wipro and Persistent Systems Q2", "detail": "Wipro after market hours; Persistent's board meets the same day (Financial Express). Unemployment rate also due (DayStarter)."}, {"n": "16", "title": "Fri: Tech Mahindra Q2", "detail": "Board also considers an interim dividend and possibly bonus shares (Financial Express)."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Sat: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Just beyond the window: Jio Platforms' anchor book is expected 19 October and the public issue 21 to 23 October (ET's reporting); the SAT order on Jane Street's appeal lands 21 October; Infosys reports 23 October (Financial Express)."}]}, {"heading": "Notes", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["X public profiles load in a logged-out browser (latest posts only, no search). Today's radar is built from official pages and reports read in full, not from X.", "No Reddit pulse item today; nothing in the last 48 hours rose to a scoop needing one.", "Jio IPO dates and its price band are Economic Times reporting of unnamed sources, not a filed prospectus.", "Benchmark scores for Odyssey-3 and Mellum2.1 are vendor-reported and labelled where they appear.", "Nothing here is investment advice. Watch items are reported facts, not calls on direction."]}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street with its red BSE sign", "caption": "Bombay Stock Exchange, Dalal Street. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}}, "6": {"title": "Morning paper, Sunday 11 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 6 of 7. The deep edition: the RBI's rupee defense, Jio's repriced IPO, the week's AI launches and the Groww teardown.", "intro": "The RBI spent Saturday building a shield around the rupee, which closed Friday nine paise from its record low. New reporting reprices the Jio IPO lower but still record-breaking, UPI's merchant fee slips to January, and the AI industry spent the week shipping the plumbing of agent work.", "groups": [{"heading": "Eight things to know", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["The RBI unveiled a Saturday package to defend the rupee: a 20% cash reserve on large forex derivative deals, a ban on rebooking cancelled contracts, a hedge-limit cut from $100 million to $5 million, and a special dollar window for the three state oil refiners from Monday.", "The rupee closed Friday at 96.73 per dollar, nine paise from its all-time closing low, down about 7% this year. Bankers expect a Monday bounce and a collapse in forward premiums; that is their forecast, not an outcome.", "New reporting reprices the Jio Platforms IPO: a roughly ₹30,000 crore all-fresh issue at ₹1,065 to ₹1,119 a share, opening 21 October. Still India's largest IPO ever, but below earlier talk.", "UPI's merchant fee, 0.4% on person-to-merchant payments above ₹2,000, is likely deferred from 15 October to 1 January. The framework itself is unchanged; the Supreme Court refused to stay it on 28 September.", "Indian startup funding fell 72% week on week to $113.35 million across 28 deals. DailyObjects' ₹332 crore Series C led; Marico put ₹1,012 crore more into Plix's parent.", "The GST Council's own document confirms arrest powers are to be withdrawn, the prosecution threshold rises from ₹1 crore to ₹5 crore, and input tax credit is unblocked for health and life insurance, a direct tailwind for group-cover economics.", "Riyadh's airport was attacked for the third time this week, killing 12 and suspending operations. The same conflict is why crude is above $100 and why the RBI had to act on Saturday.", "The week's AI launches were plumbing, not chatbots: Microsoft's Decision-1 router model, Reflection's Beam with open weights promised this month, Claude Code Projects for all paid applicants, and Tencent's Git-based TeamAI."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Inside:</strong> Front page / Policy desk / What's new radar / AI race / Markets / Startup intelligence / Global / Consumption / Sport / Try this today / Case study / Seven-day calendar / Notes."}]}, {"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The RBI spent Saturday building a shield around the rupee", "kicker": "Lead, macro"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reserve_Bank_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reserve Bank of India</a> (India's central bank) announced a package of measures in the early hours of Saturday to slow the rupee's slide. The rupee closed Friday at 96.73 per dollar, within nine paise of its all-time closing low of 96.82; the record intraday low is 96.96, hit in May. The currency has lost about 7% this year, among the worst performers in Asia, under pressure from sustained foreign capital outflows and crude above $100 a barrel (Economic Times)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The package has four parts. First, a new Foreign Exchange Risk Reserve, FERR: for rupee derivative contracts above $2 million, banks must park cash worth 20% of the rupee equivalent with the RBI, making large dollar bets expensive to carry. Second, companies can no longer rebook a cancelled forex derivative contract, a common way importers re-enter hedges after the market moves; rollovers at maturity stay allowed. Third, the threshold for hedging without documented underlying exposure fell from $100 million to $5 million, with a matching cut in exchange-traded currency derivatives. Fourth, from Monday the RBI will sell dollars directly to the three state-owned oil marketing companies, Indian Oil, HPCL and BPCL, covering their entire daily dollar needs through designated banks, until further notice (ET, Indian Express)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Why each lever exists: the first three squeeze speculative and hedging demand for dollars in the market, and the fourth removes the oil companies, the biggest single buyers of dollars, from that same market. India imports more than 88% of its crude, and the refiners were reportedly losing over ₹500 crore a day in September as pump prices lagged crude. Their daily dollar buying was itself weakening the rupee. The backdrop: forex reserves fell $12.95 billion to $734.6 billion in the week to 2 October, and retail inflation is forecast at 5.2% for FY27 with the RBI already on a hiking cycle, repo now 5.50% (Indian Express)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The cost, as bankers quoted by ET describe it: the 20% reserve makes hedging heavy on working capital for importers (RBL Bank's Anshul Chandak), forward premiums should collapse, and the spot rupee likely appreciates about 1% on Monday (DBS Bank's Ashhish Vaidya). Some bankers called the measures restrictive and said they could produce an abnormal rupee value. Those are practitioner views, and the sustainability question is theirs too: the window buys time, it does not fix a 7% slide driven by capital flows and oil."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> A weaker rupee raises the price of everything India imports, starting with fuel, and it is the channel through which the Gulf war reaches your grocery bill and your insurer's claims costs. The RBI's package tells you how worried the central bank is: it acted on a weekend, three days after raising rates."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/economy/finance/rbi-steps-in-to-support-rupee-as-currency-nears-record-low-tightens-forex-rules/articleshow/134851766.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times, 10 October</a> / <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/rbi-special-dollar-window-oil-psus-rupee-crude-prices-reserve-bank-of-india-10915196/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Express, 10 October</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Friday close96.73Record closing low96.82Record intraday low (May)96.96Rupees per US dollar; higher is weaker. Source: Economic Times, 10 Oct 2026."}, {"t": "story", "title": "New reporting reprices the Jio IPO lower: about ₹30,000 crore, ₹1,065 to ₹1,119 a share", "kicker": "Primary market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Jio Platforms, the telecom and digital arm of Reliance Industries, has finished international roadshows across the Americas, Europe and Southeast Asia, with Akash and Isha Ambani meeting investors personally, and is now running domestic roadshows, the Economic Times reports, citing people familiar with the matter. The plan: an entirely fresh issue of about ₹30,000 crore with no offer-for-sale, at ₹1,065 to ₹1,119 a share (figures attributed to Bloomberg), valuing Jio at up to ₹10.3 lakh crore. Existing investors, Meta's Jaadhu Holdings at 9.98% and Google at 7.73% the largest, are not selling; some want to increase their stakes. The anchor book opens 19 October, the public issue runs 21 to 23 October, and listing is targeted for 28 October."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Two credible reports now disagree on size. ET's 6 October report had a band of ₹1,150 to ₹1,220 raising about ₹37,700 crore; the newer reporting puts it at ₹30,000 to ₹30,200 crore. The newer report itself explains the gap: earlier expectations near ₹11 lakh crore in valuation were tempered by the market selloff. Both are pre-prospectus reporting of unnamed sources; the filed document, expected after 12 October, is what settles it. At ₹30,000 crore the issue would still surpass Hyundai Motor India's ₹27,800 crore IPO of October 2024 as India's largest, and make Jio the first top-three company to list. Nineteen banks are on the mandate."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The largest IPO in Indian history sets the tone for every other listing and pulls liquidity from the rest of the market. The repricing also tells you what the eight-week selloff did to big-ticket valuations."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://carbonmedia.in/exclusive-akash-isha-ambani-take-rs-30-000-crore-jio-ipo-pitch-global-marquee-investors-seek-bigger-stakes\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times report, 10 October, via CarbonMedia</a> / <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/ipos/fpos/jio-platforms-plans-indias-largest-ipo-at-rs-37700-crore-around-dussehra/articleshow/134720381.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET’s earlier report, 6 October</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "UPI's merchant fee slips to 1 January; the framework itself is unchanged", "kicker": "Payments"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The new <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/upi-mdr-framework-rollout-deferred-january-2027-festive-season-rbi-10912304/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">merchant discount rate</a> (the fee merchants pay to accept a payment) framework for <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_Payments_Interface\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UPI</a> (Unified Payments Interface, India's instant bank-to-bank payment rail) is likely to take effect on 1 January 2027 instead of 15 October, people familiar with the matter told Financial Express, giving merchants and the payments ecosystem the festive season to prepare. The framework itself stands: 0.4% on person-to-merchant UPI transactions above ₹2,000, capped at ₹300 for payments of ₹75,000 or more. The Supreme Court refused to stay the Centre's decision on 28 September."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Two negotiation threads remain open. Payment aggregators, the firms that process online payments for merchants, are talking to sponsor banks to keep 50% to 80% of the acquiring banks' share of the new fee. And RBI Governor Sanjay Malhotra said Wednesday he does not expect the fee to dent volumes: \"As of now, we do not see any drop in UPI volumes, and I don't personally think that a small fee will have a major impact.\" UPI volumes grew 22.2% in the September quarter, slower than the 24.2% of the previous quarter."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If you work anywhere near payments, insurance collections or e-commerce, this is the fee structure of the rails you settle on. The deferral also signals the government will not risk transaction friction in the middle of the festive season."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/upi-mdr-framework-rollout-deferred-january-2027-festive-season-rbi-10912304/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Express, 8 October</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Policy desk", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The GST Council's own document: arrests out, prosecution above ₹5 crore, and input credit for insurance", "kicker": "Policy, primary source"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Friday's edition covered the 57th GST Council meeting from a summary. The Council's own release adds the detail. On enforcement: arrest powers under section 69 of the CGST Act are to be withdrawn entirely, the monetary threshold for prosecution rises from ₹1 crore to ₹5 crore, the maximum general penalty falls from ₹25,000 to ₹10,000, and no show-cause notice will be issued below ₹10,000 of tax, including for pending cases. On cash flow: refunds of excess cash balance get fully automated sanction, and zero-rated and inverted-duty refunds get 90% provisional sanction by the system without an officer, with acknowledgement due in 10 days instead of 15."}, {"t": "p", "html": "On input tax credit, the credit businesses claim for tax paid on their inputs: the Council recommended unblocking credit on outdoor catering, health and life insurance, telecom towers, pipelines laid outside factory premises, free samples, and goods written off on expiry as required by law. For inverted-duty refunds, credit on input services applies from 1 November 2026; credit on capital goods, spread over 60 months, from 1 April 2027. Small sellers on e-commerce platforms get automatic registration in states where they have no office, using the platform's warehouse as their principal place of business. These are Council recommendations; they become law through amendments to the CGST and IGST Acts and rules."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Two of these land directly on your industry. Unblocked input tax credit on health and life insurance lowers the net cost of group cover for every business buyer, and a trust-based tax administration changes how every corporate client budgets compliance risk."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.pib.gov.in/PressReleasePage.aspx?PRID=2320934&lang=1&reg=48\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Press Information Bureau, GST Council release, 8 October</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "India opens an anti-dumping probe into Chinese penicillin-G", "kicker": "Trade, brief"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India has initiated an anti-dumping investigation into imports of Penicillin-G and its salts from China, the base ingredient for a large family of antibiotics. Anti-dumping probes examine whether imports are priced below fair value and hurting domestic producers; duties follow only if injury is found. The why: India has spent two years trying to rebuild domestic fermentation capacity for bulk drugs after decades of Chinese price pressure hollowed it out (The Hindu)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.thehindu.com/business/Industry/india-initiates-anti-dumping-probe-on-pen-g-salts-from-china/article71569088.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The Hindu, 10 October</a>"}]}, {"heading": "What's new radar, since Friday", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Launched or announced in the last 48 hours. PRIMARY means the company's own page; REPORT means a news report. Vendor numbers are labelled as such."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "Decision-1 (Microsoft)", "detail": "10 Oct. Small model for classification, evaluation and routing, built on Qwen3.5-9B. On Microsoft Foundry and OpenRouter at $0.042 per million input tokens, output tokens free. 'Most accurate across 36 benchmarks' is Microsoft's claim. REPORT."}, {"n": "2", "title": "Beam (Reflection AI)", "detail": "Previewed 5 Oct, reported widely 10 Oct. Coding and tool-use model, 501B parameters with 23B active, open weights under Apache 2.0 promised later this month. REPORT, benchmarks company-run. Details below."}, {"n": "3", "title": "Claude Code Projects, open beta (Anthropic)", "detail": "10 Oct. The Projects redesign from 17 September is now open to all Pro and Max applicants: a coordinator conversation fans work out to parallel cloud sessions, each on its own git branch, opening pull requests. REPORT."}, {"n": "4", "title": "TeamAI (Tencent Cloud)", "detail": "10 Oct, MIT licence. Stores agent skills, rules and tool configs in Git with merge-request review; works with 16 coding agents including Claude Code and Cursor. Claims of 76% lower model cost are Tencent's internal tests. REPORT."}]}]}, {"heading": "AI race, deeper", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Reflection's Beam: the open-weights challenger bets on efficiency, not size", "kicker": "Models"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Reflection AI, the startup founded by former DeepMind researchers, previewed Beam on 5 October: a mixture-of-experts model, 501 billion total parameters with 23 billion active per token, built for coding and tool use. On the company's scorecard, Beam posts 80.1 on Terminal-Bench 2.1 against 63.8 for Thinking Machines' Inkling, and 65.5 on SWE-Bench Pro v1 against Inkling's 54.3 and Z.ai GLM-5.2's 62.1; GLM-5.2 still edges Beam on Terminal-Bench at 81.0, and Moonshot's Kimi K3 leads that test at 88.3. All of these are vendor-run numbers on different harnesses, not a controlled comparison. Reflection says Beam matches GLM-5.2's advanced-reasoning scores using three to four times less inference compute, trained on 23.8 trillion tokens plus a four-week reinforcement run on 10,500 Nvidia GB300 GPUs."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The strategy under the numbers: Beam is built for organizations that want a Western model they can run and adapt themselves, CEO Misha Laskin told Semafor. Weights under Apache 2.0, a technical report and safety evaluations are promised later this month; the business is selling deployment and customization around the open model. A reasoning-effort dial lets users trade response length against depth. Until the weights ship, the core promise, run it yourself, remains just that."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> If your company cannot or will not send data to Chinese models, the open Western options have been thin. Beam, with weights promised under Apache 2.0 within weeks, is the first credible new entry at frontier-adjacent coding scores, and its cost claims target exactly the enterprise workloads you would automate."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://winbuzzer.com/2026/10/10/reflection-previews-beam-promising-more-efficient-ai-coding-xcxwbn/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">WinBuzzer report, 10 October</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The pattern of the week: agents are becoming infrastructure", "kicker": "Analysis"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Three of the week's four launches are components, not products you chat with. Microsoft's Decision-1 does one narrow job, classification and routing, at $0.042 per million input tokens with output free: the cheap front-door model that decides which expensive model handles each request. Anthropic's Claude Code Projects turns a goal into parallel cloud sessions, each writing code on its own git branch and opening pull requests back to a coordinator, with shared project memory and a 200-thread daily cap. Tencent's TeamAI puts the agent's skills and tool configs themselves under Git version control, so a team's improvements propagate like code; Tencent claims a 76% model-cost reduction when cheaper models draw on that shared knowledge, its own internal test across 13 tasks. The common thread: the economics and governance of agent fleets, not raw IQ."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Routing models, shared team memory and Git-managed agent skills are the unglamorous layers that decide whether an agent deployment is cheap and governable. If you are scoping agent features for insurance workflows, these three launches are the reference architecture, not the headline models."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://notesfromtheterminal.com/story/2026-10-10-microsoft-decision-1-decision-model/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Notes from the Terminal</a> / <a href=\"https://mlllm.io/articles/3482-anthropic-opens-claude-code-projects-beta-to-all-applicants/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">mlllm.io</a> / <a href=\"https://chinaaiwire.com/news/tencent-cloud-teamai-open-source-agent-collaboration-20261010\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">China AI Wire</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Where things stand", "kicker": "Friday's close, carried into the weekend"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Friday, 9 October", "v": "Nifty 22,520.45, up 1.30%; Sensex 72,472.33, up 879 points. The eight-week losing streak ended. IT led on TCS results."}, {"k": "The counter-trend", "v": "Foreign investors still sold for a tenth straight session (₹3,569 crore) and have now sold for eight straight weeks (ET). Domestic institutions kept absorbing."}, {"k": "Rupee", "v": "Closed 96.73 per dollar, nine paise from the record closing low. The RBI package above is the weekend response; a roughly 1% Monday bounce is bankers’ expectation, not a fact."}, {"k": "Crude", "v": "Brent eased to about $102.9 on Friday but remains above $100, the level that triggered the oil companies’ dollar window."}, {"k": "Gold", "v": "22-carat gold ₹13,895 a gram at retail Friday, up ₹105."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://tbtflow.com/daily/fii-dii-data-today\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">TBTflow flows data</a>, <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/fiis-dump-indian-stocks-for-eight-straight-weeks-is-a-reversal-on-the-cards-or-more-pain-ahead/articleshow/134851502.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times on the eight-week FII streak</a>, Friday editions as linked in Notes."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The week ahead for markets", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Monday is the heavyweight: September CPI inflation (a Mint poll of 19 economists expects 5.6%, a 23-month high), HCLTech's results, the Supreme Court hearing on the 30% cancer-drug margin cap, and the first day of the RBI's oil-company dollar window. The Jio prospectus is expected to be filed any day after 12 October. Wednesday brings wholesale inflation plus US and China CPI, the TCS dividend record date and the Fusion CX IPO opening. No view on direction is implied."}]}, {"heading": "Startup intelligence", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The week's funding halved, then halved again: $113 million across 28 deals", "kicker": "Funding, weekly wrap"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Indian startups raised $113.35 million across 28 disclosed deals in the week to 10 October, down 72% from $411.46 million and 24 deals the previous week, and below the $231.6 million eight-week average. Three growth-stage deals accounted for $55.3 million; 22 early-stage deals made up $58.05 million. Bengaluru led with 10 deals, Delhi-NCR had six and Mumbai three. E-commerce led sectors with eight deals, AI had five and healthtech four; seed rounds were the most common stage with 13."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The growth-stage three: <a href=\"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/technology/tech-news/dailyobjects-raises-rs-332-crore-in-series-c-round-led-by-xponentia-anicut-and-axiom-asia/articleshow/134853765.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">DailyObjects</a> (lifestyle-tech accessories brand) raised ₹332 crore, about $35 million, in a Series C led by Xponentia Capital Partners, Anicut Capital and Axiom Asia (corroborated by the Times of India). Smart-kitchen startup Beyond Appliances raised ₹110 crore in a Series B led by Fireside Ventures. And MSME lender NeoGrowth took ₹85 crore in equity and debt from FMO, the Dutch development bank, and LeapFrog Investments."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Early stage: <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/companies/nat-habit-raises-142-crore-in-a-series-c-funding-round-led-by-trident-growth-partners/article71567824.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Nat Habit</a> (direct-to-consumer ayurvedic beauty brand) raised ₹142 crore in a Series C led by Trident Growth Partners (Hindu BusinessLine). Photon-based quantum computing startup Quanfluence's $10 million and voice-AI startup Desible.ai's ₹32 crore were covered in Friday's edition. Edtech byteXL raised $9 million from Elevar Equity; medtech Sunfox Technologies raised $7 million co-led by Ashish Kacholia, Lashit Sanghvi, Alchemy Capital and 3one4 Capital; quantum startup QpiAI took ₹50 crore in venture debt from InnoVen Capital."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Beyond cheques: FMCG major <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marico\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Marico</a> (listed consumer goods company, Parachute and Saffola) bought a further 24.09% of Satiya Nutraceuticals, parent of plant-based wellness brand Plix, for ₹1,012 crore. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sarvam_AI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Sarvam AI</a> (Bengaluru startup building Indian-language AI models) hired former Google Cloud India managing director Sashikumar Sreedharan as president and chief business officer. And in listed-broker data, <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/gmztqdyil4zu-growws-money-machine-listed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Groww</a> (India's largest investment platform by active clients) added over 1 lakh active clients in September while the industry added 1.6 lakh, taking the total to 4.61 crore; newcomer Sahi grew fastest among the top 20, up 11% month on month (NSE data via the same weekly report)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> One slow week after a $411 million week is normalisation, not a trend; the eight-week average is $231.6 million. What is worth watching is the mix: consumer brands raised the big cheques in festive season while enterprise and deeptech took debt and small rounds."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://startupspotlightindia.com/indian-startups-raise-113-35-million-across-28-deals-05-oct-10-oct-2026/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Startup Spotlight India weekly report, 10 October</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Previous week$411.46M, 24 deals8-week average$231.60M, 22 dealsThis week$113.35M, 28 dealsDisclosed startup funding, weeks ended 3 and 10 Oct 2026. Source: Startup Spotlight India."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Opportunities", "kicker": "Where the gap might be"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Forex-hedging operations for mid-size importers. The RBI just made large hedges 20% more cash-hungry and banned rebooking. Importers with lazy treasury processes will mis-hedge or over-pay; tooling and advisory that re-plans hedge ladders under the new rules has a forcing event behind it.", "Festive consumer brands are getting funded at Series C (DailyObjects, Nat Habit). The picks-and-shovels version: retention analytics for the festive cohort, the customers acquired at deep discount in October who churn by January.", "Refund-readiness tooling, carried from yesterday: the GST Council's own document now confirms automated 90% provisional refunds after system risk checks. Businesses with messy filings fail those checks; the clean-up service now has a dated, primary-source mandate."]}, {"t": "story", "title": "A hedge-cost calculator for the FERR era", "kicker": "One idea, a hypothesis to test, not a validated market"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The 20% Foreign Exchange Risk Reserve changed the arithmetic of hedging for every importer running derivative books above $2 million. The idea: a simple calculator that takes a company's open exposures and shows the working-capital cost of hedging each tranche under the new reserve rules versus staying unhedged at a range of rupee levels. The test: ask five import-heavy CFOs or their treasury consultants how they re-ran their hedge books this weekend. If they used spreadsheets and guesses, there is a product. If their banks handed them the analysis free, there is none."}]}, {"heading": "Global", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Riyadh's airport attacked a third time in a week; 12 dead, operations suspended", "kicker": "Middle East"}, {"t": "p", "html": "At least 12 people were killed and more than 300 wounded in the third attack this week on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh. The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houthis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Houthis</a> (Iran-backed armed movement controlling much of Yemen) claimed responsibility, a major escalation of their campaign against Saudi Arabia, and airport operations are suspended with at least 225 flights cancelled over 24 hours. The strikes follow a Saudi-led coalition offensive against Houthi positions in Yemen; earlier attacks on Wednesday and Thursday killed a Sudanese national and three Saudis, including a pilot. President Trump, asked on Friday, said \"It's not good\" and \"I'm not happy\"; there has been no sign of US military support in response. Saudi authorities have warned the public that filming or sharing strike footage carries legal liability (ABC News)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Elsewhere in the conflict map: Israeli strikes killed at least five Palestinians in Gaza on Saturday, with Israel saying Hamas is rearming in violation of the year-old ceasefire, which Hamas denies. Palestinian president Mahmoud Abbas postponed legislative elections, due next month, to 11 September 2027; Palestinians have not held national elections since 2006. And in Ukraine, Russian strikes killed 21 as Presidents Trump and Zelensky clashed publicly over a diesel supply deal (ABC, Al Jazeera, AFP via Inquirer)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> This is not distant news. The same escalation is why Brent is above $100, why the rupee is at record lows, and why the RBI opened an emergency dollar window on Saturday. One conflict connects this section to the front page."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2026-10-11/saudi-airport-attacked-twelve-dead/107252320\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ABC News, 11 October</a> / <a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/liveblog/2026/10/11/iran-war-live-12-killed-in-riyadh-us-weighs-joining-fight-against-houthis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Al Jazeera live coverage</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A large oil tanker at sea under a grey sky", "caption": "Brent crude stayed above $100 a barrel this week, the immediate pressure on the rupee and the reason for the RBI's oil-dollar window. File photo: ImagePerson (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d4-g3.jpg"}]}, {"heading": "Consumption", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The festive sales opened with records on both big platforms", "kicker": "E-commerce"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Amazon India and Flipkart both opened their flagship festive sales this week, Amazon's Great Indian Festival and Flipkart's Big Billion Days, and both report record starts. Amazon says it crossed 440 million visits in the opening phase, its highest ever, with products ordered up 100% year on year, app downloads up fivefold, 72% of visits from outside the top nine metros, and orders from every pin code in the country. Flipkart says it peaked at 6.3 million concurrent users per minute, six times a regular hour, with 287 million sessions during early access and about 60% of traction from non-metro markets; new customers grew 34%. All figures are company-reported, the platforms mark their own homework. Category standouts, also company-reported: premium Android phones up 70% year on year on Amazon, large appliances up to 80 times regular days, and men's perfume selling at 1,600 units a minute at Flipkart's peak."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Festive-season sale numbers are the first hard read on consumer demand after the rate hike, landing 48 hours before Monday's inflation print. Strong demand plus high inflation is the combination that keeps rates higher for longer."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://yourstory.com/2026/10/amazon-flipkart-see-strong-start-to-2026-festive-sales\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">YourStory, 10 October</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Sport", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "West Indies chase 250 to level the T20 series", "kicker": "Cricket"}, {"t": "p", "html": "West Indies pulled off their highest successful T20I chase, 252 for 4 against India's 249 for 5, in the second T20 international on Friday night. Captain Shai Hope made 102 not out and Sherfane Rutherford 84. India had won the first match in Lucknow behind Shreyas Iyer's maiden T20I century, so the series is level 1-1. The why, beyond the result: chasing 250 requires a scoring rate India rarely concedes at home, and it came against a full-strength attack in a World Cup cycle year."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cricinfo.com/series/west-indies-in-india-2026-27-1529215/india-vs-west-indies-2nd-t20i-1529231/match-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ESPNcricinfo match report</a> / <a href=\"https://www.cricinfo.com/series/west-indies-in-india-2026-27-1529215/india-vs-west-indies-1st-t20i-1529230/match-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">First T20I report</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Try this today", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "NotebookLM on the rupee defense", "kicker": "Sunday wild card: turn the lead story into a 10-minute audio briefing"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Today's tool: <a href=\"https://notebooklm.google.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NotebookLM</a> (Google's AI research assistant that answers only from sources you give it). The use: convert the RBI story into a listenable briefing for your Monday commute, grounded in the actual reporting rather than a model's memory. Time: about 15 minutes, of which five is the tool generating audio while you do something else."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "The exact steps1. Open notebooklm.google.com and create a notebook named \"Rupee defense\". 2. Add two sources via \"Add source, website\": the Economic Times and Indian Express articles linked in the lead item above. Add the GST Council's PIB release too if you want the policy angle. 3. Click \"Audio Overview\" and let it generate, about five minutes. 4. Listen once through. 5. Quality check: the hosts should cite the same figures this paper does (96.73 close, 20% reserve, $2 million threshold, window opens 12 October). If the audio states a number that differs from the article text, trust the article; generated audio paraphrases and occasionally drifts."}, {"t": "p", "html": "What a good result looks like: an 8 to 12 minute two-host conversation you would forward to a colleague, one that explains why the oil companies' dollar window matters more than the headline reserve requirement. If it stays at headline level, regenerate with a prompt in the customize box: \"Focus on the mechanism of each measure and who pays the cost.\""}]}, {"heading": "Startup case study", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "One business teardown per edition, and today it pairs with yesterday's. After Zerodha, the private incumbent, today's long read is Groww: the listed challenger. How a platform that acquires 83% of users organically turned 21.6 million transacting users into a ₹2,083 crore annual profit, why 55% of its revenue still rides on the derivatives the regulator is shrinking, and what its first year as a listed company reveals. Read it here: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/gmztqdyil4zu-growws-money-machine-listed\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Groww's money machine, listed</a>."}]}, {"heading": "Seven-day calendar, 11 to 18 October", "blocks": [{"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "12", "title": "Mon: September CPI; HCLTech Q2; cancer-drug cap hearing; RBI window opens", "detail": "Inflation expected at 5.6%, a 23-month high (Mint poll of 19 economists, via DayStarter). HCLTech reports Q2 and considers a third interim dividend (Financial Express). The Supreme Court hears the 30% trade-margin cap on cancer medicines. The RBI's dollar window for oil companies takes effect. Watch for the Jio prospectus filing any day after 12 October."}, {"n": "13", "title": "Tue: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Wed: WPI inflation; US and China CPI; Fusion CX IPO opens", "detail": "India's wholesale inflation, plus US consumer inflation (previous 3.4%) and China's (previous 0.8%), via DayStarter's calendar. TCS dividend record date. Fusion CX, a customer-experience company, opens a ₹702 crore IPO at ₹275-289 a share, closing 16 October (Economic Times)."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Thu: Wipro and Persistent Systems Q2", "detail": "Wipro after market hours; Persistent's board meets the same day (Financial Express). The UPI merchant fee was due to take effect today before its likely deferral to 1 January."}, {"n": "16", "title": "Fri: Tech Mahindra Q2; Fusion CX closes", "detail": "Tech Mahindra's board also considers an interim dividend and possibly bonus shares (Financial Express)."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Sat: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}, {"n": "18", "title": "Sun: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Just beyond the window: Jio's anchor book is expected 19 October and the public issue 21 to 23 October (pre-prospectus reporting); the SAT order on Jane Street's appeal lands 21 October; Infosys reports 23 October (Financial Express)."}]}, {"heading": "Notes", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["Dropped item: a widely circulated report this weekend framed OpenAI's GPT-5.6 and an enterprise agent product as newly launched. OpenAI's own page dates GPT-5.6's general availability to 9 July, and the report's launch claims do not match any primary announcement, so it does not appear in today's radar.", "Jio IPO terms are pre-prospectus reporting. Two credible reports disagree on the size and band (₹37,700 crore at ₹1,150-1,220 versus about ₹30,000 crore at ₹1,065-1,119); both versions are labelled where they appear.", "Banker expectations for Monday's rupee open are quoted views, not outcomes. Festive-sale figures are company-reported. Benchmark scores for Beam and Decision-1 are vendor-run.", "X public profiles still load in a logged-out browser (latest posts only, no search). Today's radar is built from official pages and reports read in full.", "Nothing here is investment advice. Watch items are reported facts, not calls on direction."]}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Reserve Bank of India tower in Mumbai", "caption": "The Reserve Bank of India tower, Mumbai. The central bank announced its rupee measures in the early hours of Saturday. Photo: KuwarOnline (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0).", "img": "photos/d6-hero.jpg"}}, "7": {"title": "Morning paper, Monday 12 October 2026", "fact": "Edition 7 of 7, the final edition of the trial. CPI day, the rupee's first test under the RBI shield, HCLTech results, and the PhonePe teardown.", "intro": "Two numbers land today: September inflation, expected at a 23-month high, and HCLTech's results. The rupee gets its first trading session under the RBI's Saturday defense package, and Saudi Arabia is promising retaliation for the airport strike that killed 12.", "groups": [{"heading": "Eight things to know", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["September CPI inflation is due this evening. A Mint poll of 19 economists expects 5.6%, a 23-month high, with estimates in a 5% to 5.9% band. The print lands inside an RBI hiking cycle, repo at 5.50%.", "The rupee trades its first session under the RBI's Saturday shield: the oil-company dollar window opens today. Kotak's research desk sees a sub-95 handle as possible; bankers on Saturday expected roughly a 1% bounce. Those are desk views, not outcomes.", "Hedging dollars just got more expensive. Kotak estimates the new 20% cash reserve on large hedges, plus tighter cash-reserve rules for banks, could drain a durable ₹1.5 lakh crore of liquidity if the measures stay a month.", "HCLTech reports Q2 today. The average of seven brokerages: profit up about 12% and revenue up about 16% year on year. The thing to watch is guidance, expected to be revised from 1-4% to 3-4% (Economic Times preview).", "Saudi Arabia has vowed retaliation against the Houthis after Friday's strike on Riyadh's airport killed 12 and wounded over 300. President Trump suggested the US could join Saudi operations (Washington Post, Al Jazeera). Brent is the reason this is on an India business front page.", "India beat West Indies by 14 runs in the third T20I on Sunday to lead the series 2-1. Abhishek Sharma made 69; Arshdeep Singh led the defense of 223.", "The weekend's verified AI launch radar is thin: one genuine open beta, Vertical Marketplace, where a company's own AI lists and prices its data for other AIs to buy. It is a company blog claim, labelled as such.", "This is edition 7 of 7. Today's long read is the PhonePe teardown: how 45% of UPI became a ₹12,000 crore IPO filing, a pause, and a road back."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Inside:</strong> Front page / What's new radar / Markets / Global / Sport / Try this today / Case study / Seven-day calendar / Notes."}]}, {"heading": "Front page", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The rupee's first test: what the Street expects from today's open", "kicker": "Lead, macro"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Reserve_Bank_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Reserve Bank of India</a> (India's central bank) measures announced in the early hours of Saturday get their first market verdict today: the 20% Foreign Exchange Risk Reserve on derivative contracts above $2 million, the ban on rebooking cancelled forex contracts, the hedge-limit cut from $100 million to $5 million, and the special dollar window covering the entire daily dollar needs of the three state oil refiners, which opens this morning (ET, Saturday)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sunday's follow-through reporting maps what bank research desks expect. <a href=\"https://www.kotak.com/en/home.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Kotak</a> (Kotak Mahindra group; its institutional research desk quoted here) sees the rupee possibly trading stronger than 95 to the dollar, with forward premia, the cost of locking in a future exchange rate, softening. <a href=\"https://www.idfcfirstbank.com/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">IDFC FIRST Bank</a> (private-sector bank; economics research desk quoted here) puts the terminal repo rate for this cycle at 6% to 6.25%, and projects core banking-system liquidity falling from ₹10.4 lakh crore to about ₹2 lakh crore by March 2027 (TOI, citing both banks' research)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The cost side of the shield is now quantified. Kotak estimates a durable liquidity drain of about ₹1.5 lakh crore if the measures stay in place for a month, because the FERR forces banks to park 20% of the rupee value of large hedge contracts with the RBI as cash, earning nothing. Add the RBI's own operations: a ₹25,000 crore open-market sale of government bonds this month, and the cash reserve ratio banks must maintain daily rising from 90% to 99% of the requirement. Hedging costs for importers are expected to rise 1% to 1.6%, per the dealers TOI quotes. Every figure in this paragraph is a research-desk estimate or an announced RBI operation; the open itself is a fact only after 9:15 AM."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> The RBI built the shield on Saturday; today the market grades it. If the measures hold the rupee, importers' costs stabilize and the inflation print matters a little less. If they do not, the next lever is interest rates, and that reprices every loan and every insurance product's discount rate."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/business/india-business/rupee-relief-higher-hedging-costs-what-rbis-new-forex-rules-mean/articleshow/134872828.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Times of India, 11 October</a> / <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/news/economy/finance/rbi-steps-in-to-support-rupee-as-currency-nears-record-low-tightens-forex-rules/articleshow/134851766.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times, 10 October</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Friday close96.73Record closing low96.82Record intraday low (May)96.96Rupees per US dollar; higher is weaker. Levels through Friday's close. Source: Economic Times, 10 Oct 2026."}, {"t": "story", "title": "September inflation, due today, is expected at a 23-month high of 5.6%", "kicker": "Macro, data day"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Consumer_price_index\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">consumer price index</a> (the retail inflation gauge the RBI targets) print for September is due this evening. A Mint poll of 19 economists pegs it at 5.6%, which would be the highest in 23 months, with individual estimates ranging from 5% to 5.9%. The poll was published 8 October; the number itself is an expectation, and the print will either confirm or embarrass it."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The why behind the expectation: food prices firming through September, and the pass-through of a weak rupee and crude above $100 into fuel and transport costs. The same two forces sit behind the RBI's Saturday rupee package and its hiking cycle, repo now 5.50% with IDFC FIRST's desk pencilling a 6% to 6.25% terminal rate. Today's print is the first hard data point that either validates or contradicts that path; wholesale inflation follows on Wednesday."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Inflation at 5.6% with the repo already at 5.50% keeps rate cuts off the table and another hike live. For insurance distribution, that holds policy rates and discount assumptions where they are, and it squeezes the household budgets that fund premiums."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.livemint.com/economy/retail-inflation-likely-hit-23-month-high-of-5-6-in-sep-mint-poll-11791453904208.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Mint poll, 8 October</a>"}, {"t": "story", "title": "HCLTech reports Q2 today: the Street wants a guidance upgrade", "kicker": "Earnings"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/HCLTech\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">HCLTech</a> (India's third-largest IT services company) announces September-quarter results today. The average of seven brokerages in ET's preview: profit up about 12% and revenue up about 16% year on year, with constant-currency growth, the measure that strips out currency swings, of 2.7% to 3%. The headline to watch is the FY27 revenue guidance, currently 1% to 4%, which the brokerages expect management to revise up to 3% to 4%."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The machinery behind the numbers, per the preview: Kotak estimates deal wins of $3.75 billion total contract value, including a €1 billion mega deal and the ramp of the Guardian Life insurance contract; the HPE Telco and Jaspersoft acquisitions add about 100 basis points of inorganic growth; and EBIT margin is expected up 70 to 120 basis points. The board also considers a third interim dividend (Financial Express, via Friday's edition). All of this is the preview; the results themselves land today, and this paper does not carry them."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> HCLTech is the first large-cap IT report of the season after TCS, and its guidance revision is the cleanest read on whether enterprise tech spending is genuinely recovering. IT led Friday's market rebound, so the sector walks in with expectations loaded."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/earnings/hcl-tech-q2-preview-will-the-it-major-narrow-its-guidance-5-things-to-watch-out-for/articleshow/134865454.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times Q2 preview, 11 October</a>"}]}, {"heading": "What's new radar, since Saturday", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Launched or announced in the last 48 hours. COMPANY BLOG means the only source is the maker's own page. It was a quiet 48 hours for verified launches: three widely circulated \"new launch\" stories turned out to be dated July, April and February respectively, and are in Notes instead."}, {"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "1", "title": "Vertical Marketplace, open beta (buymydata.ai)", "detail": "12 Oct. A marketplace where a business's own AI agent analyzes, lists and prices the company's data, and other AI agents buy access to it, per query, over MCP or a REST API. Buyers get cryptographically signed data from the source. The maker says 64 US patent filings are pending. All claims are the company's own; no independent coverage yet. COMPANY BLOG."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "The why behind watching this one, labelled as analysis, from <a href=\"https://buymydata.ai/blog/announcing-vertical-marketplace\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">the maker's launch post</a>: agent products have hit the same wall, models that know the world in general but not a specific vendor's contract or price list. A two-sided data market where the seller's agent does the listing work is one plausible fix, and it runs on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Model_Context_Protocol\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">MCP</a> (Model Context Protocol, the open standard for connecting AI models to external data and tools), the rail most agent stacks already speak. Whether anyone pays per query is the open question."}]}, {"heading": "Markets", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Where things stand", "kicker": "Friday's close, carried into the week"}, {"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Friday, 9 October", "v": "Nifty 22,520.45, up 1.30%; Sensex 72,472.33, up 879 points. The eight-week losing streak ended. IT led on TCS results."}, {"k": "The counter-trend", "v": "Foreign investors still sold for a tenth straight session (₹3,569 crore) and have now sold for eight straight weeks (ET). Domestic institutions kept absorbing."}, {"k": "Rupee", "v": "Closed 96.73 per dollar, nine paise from the record closing low. Today is the first session under the Saturday package; desk expectations are in the lead item."}, {"k": "Crude", "v": "Brent eased to about $102.9 on Friday but remains above $100, the level that triggered the oil companies’ dollar window opening today."}, {"k": "Gold", "v": "22-carat gold ₹13,895 a gram at retail Friday, up ₹105."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Sources: <a href=\"https://economictimes.indiatimes.com/markets/stocks/news/fiis-dump-indian-stocks-for-eight-straight-weeks-is-a-reversal-on-the-cards-or-more-pain-ahead/articleshow/134851502.cms\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Economic Times on the eight-week FII streak</a>, Saturday's edition as linked in Notes."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The week ahead for markets", "kicker": "What to watch, not what to buy"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Today is the heavyweight: September CPI this evening, HCLTech results, the Supreme Court hearing on the 30% cancer-drug trade-margin cap, and day one of the RBI's oil-dollar window. The Jio Platforms prospectus is expected to be filed any day now, with its anchor book reported for 19 October and the public issue 21 to 23 October. Wednesday brings wholesale inflation plus US and China CPI and the Fusion CX IPO opening; Wipro and Persistent Systems report Thursday; Tech Mahindra on Friday. No view on direction is implied."}]}, {"heading": "Global", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Saudi Arabia vows retaliation for the airport strike; Trump floats joining in", "kicker": "Middle East"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Saudi Arabia has vowed to retaliate against the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Houthis\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Houthis</a> (Iran-backed armed movement controlling much of Yemen) after Friday's strike on King Khalid International Airport in Riyadh, the third attack on the airport in a week, which killed 12 people and wounded more than 300 (Washington Post). President Trump has suggested the United States could join Saudi operations against the Houthis (Al Jazeera). Airport operations were suspended after the strike, and the Indian Express reports Umrah pilgrims from India among those stranded."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The why for this page: Friday's strike is the same escalation chain that pushed Brent above $100, dragged the rupee to 96.73, and forced the RBI's Saturday package. A vowed Saudi retaliation, with possible US participation, points to more of that pressure, not less, which is why this story leads the global section of an India money paper two days running."}, {"t": "p", "html": "<strong>Why it matters to you:</strong> Retaliation means the conflict widens rather than cools, and a wider Gulf conflict is the direct channel to Brent above $100, the rupee's slide, and this morning's RBI dollar window. For an insurance reader: war-risk premiums, aviation exposure and claims inflation all reprice on escalation."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.washingtonpost.com/world/2026/10/11/saudi-arabia-vows-retaliation-against-houthis-after-riyadh-airport-strike/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Washington Post, 11 October</a> / <a href=\"https://www.aljazeera.com/news/2026/10/11/yemen-fighting-expands-as-houthis-attack-saudi-airport-what-to-know\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Al Jazeera, 11 October</a>"}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A large oil tanker at sea under a grey sky", "caption": "Brent crude stayed above $100 a barrel last week, the pressure behind both the rupee's slide and today's inflation expectation. File photo: ImagePerson (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 4.0).", "img": "photos/d4-g3.jpg"}]}, {"heading": "Sport", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "India defend 223 to take a 2-1 series lead over West Indies", "kicker": "Cricket"}, {"t": "p", "html": "India beat West Indies by 14 runs in the third T20 international on Sunday: 223 against 209, with Abhishek Sharma's 69 and Ishan Kishan's 37 setting the total and Arshdeep Singh leading the defense. India lead the series 2-1. The why beyond the result: two days after West Indies pulled off their highest-ever T20I chase of 252, India's bowlers closed out a defendable total, the more repeatable skill in a World Cup cycle year (ESPNcricinfo)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://www.cricinfo.com/series/west-indies-in-india-2026-27-1529215/india-vs-west-indies-3rd-t20i-1529232/match-report\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ESPNcricinfo match report, 11 October</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Try this today", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Beat the HCLTech headline by 20 minutes", "kicker": "Monday prompting: read a results filing in three passes"}, {"t": "p", "html": "When HCLTech's results PDF drops today (large-cap IT usually files after market hours, 3:30 PM onwards), skip the news rewrites and read the filing with an AI assistant in three structured passes. Time: about 10 minutes once you have the PDF. Tool: any assistant that accepts a PDF upload, Claude, ChatGPT or Gemini all work; the method is the point."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "The exact steps1. Download the results PDF from HCLTech's investor relations page or the BSE/NSE filing, not from a news site. 2. Pass 1, extraction: upload the PDF and prompt: \"Extract into a table: revenue, constant-currency growth, EBIT margin, attrition, total contract value of deal wins, and FY27 guidance, each with the page number.\" 3. Pass 2, comparison: paste the preview numbers from this paper's HCLTech item and prompt: \"Compare each extracted metric against these preview estimates. List beats, misses and in-lines in a table.\" 4. Pass 3, questions: prompt: \"Write five questions an analyst would ask on this earnings call, ranked by how much the answer would move the stock.\" 5. Before reusing any number, check it against the PDF page the assistant cited."}, {"t": "p", "html": "What a good result looks like: one screen with the six-metric table, a beats-and-misses column, and five call questions you could actually ask, ready before the first news rewrite publishes. If the assistant gives a number without a page reference, that number does not exist until you find it in the PDF."}]}, {"heading": "Startup case study", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Today's teardown closes the payments arc the week built toward, after Policybazaar, Zerodha and Groww. PhonePe runs 45% of UPI by volume, grew revenue 40% last year, and still lost ₹1,727 crore. It got SEBI's IPO nod in January, paused in March, and is now targeting a February-March 2027 listing. The long read traces how UPI dominance became both the moat and the margin problem, what the deferred merchant fee changes, and what the IPO math says about a $10 billion question. Read it here: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/gvc2w9n3snoh-phonepes-road-back-to-ipo\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PhonePe's road back to IPO</a>."}]}, {"heading": "Seven-day calendar, 12 to 19 October", "blocks": [{"t": "rows", "items": [{"n": "12", "title": "Mon: September CPI; HCLTech Q2; cancer-drug cap hearing; RBI window day one", "detail": "CPI due this evening, poll expectation 5.6% (Mint). HCLTech reports Q2 and considers a third interim dividend (ET, Financial Express). The Supreme Court hears the 30% trade-margin cap on cancer medicines. The RBI's dollar window for oil companies takes effect. Watch for the Jio prospectus filing."}, {"n": "13", "title": "Tue: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}, {"n": "14", "title": "Wed: WPI inflation; US and China CPI; Fusion CX IPO opens", "detail": "India's wholesale inflation, plus US consumer inflation (previous 3.4%) and China's (previous 0.8%). TCS dividend record date. Fusion CX, a customer-experience company, opens a ₹702 crore IPO at ₹275-289 a share, closing 16 October (Economic Times)."}, {"n": "15", "title": "Thu: Wipro and Persistent Systems Q2", "detail": "Wipro after market hours; Persistent's board meets the same day (Financial Express). The UPI merchant fee was due to take effect today before its deferral to 1 January 2027."}, {"n": "16", "title": "Fri: Tech Mahindra Q2; Fusion CX closes", "detail": "Tech Mahindra's board also considers an interim dividend and possibly bonus shares (Financial Express)."}, {"n": "17", "title": "Sat: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}, {"n": "18", "title": "Sun: nothing scheduled", "detail": "No verified market, policy or startup event found for this date."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Just beyond the window: Jio's anchor book is reported for 19 October and the public issue 21 to 23 October, both pre-prospectus reporting; the SAT order on Jane Street's appeal lands 21 October; Infosys reports 23 October (Financial Express)."}]}, {"heading": "Notes", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["Dropped items, all recirculated with fresh-looking October datelines: an AMD Instella model launch that AMD's own page dates to 31 July; a Claude Mythos announcement dated 10 April; and a Blackstone-Neysa $1.2 billion investment story whose article is dated 16 February. None appears as news in this edition.", "CPI figures today are poll expectations, not the print; the print is due this evening. Bank and brokerage views (Kotak's sub-95 scenario, the ₹1.5 lakh crore drain estimate, the HCLTech preview averages) are attributed research, not outcomes. The rupee's Monday open is not covered; markets open after this paper ships.", "The Vertical Marketplace launch is sourced only to the maker's blog and is labelled as such.", "X public profiles still load in a logged-out browser (latest posts only, no search), last checked 10 October. Today's radar is built from official pages and reports read in full.", "Nothing here is investment advice. Watch items are reported facts, not calls on direction."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Saturday's edition, with the full RBI package detail: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/sobaosho0ztv-rbis-weekend-rupee-shield\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">The RBI's weekend rupee shield</a>."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Reserve Bank of India tower in Mumbai", "caption": "The Reserve Bank of India tower, Mumbai. The central bank's dollar window for state oil companies opens today. Photo: KuwarOnline (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 3.0).", "img": "photos/d6-hero.jpg"}}}, "longreads": {"2": {"title": "A paper promise, a square code", "fact": "Long read, Wednesday 7 October 2026. About 12 minutes.", "intro": "Every few generations India rebuilds the way money crosses distance. The tools change completely. The problem never does.", "groups": [{"heading": "One", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Money that never travelled", "kicker": "The paper era"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The Reserve Bank of India describes the hundi as a credit instrument that evolved in India, most widely used in the 12th century and still around in modern times. The Economic Times, quoting that description, lists three jobs a hundi did: send funds from one place to another, work as a loan, and finance trade as a bill of exchange."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The forms were specific. A darshani hundi was payable the moment it was shown. A muddati hundi was payable after a stated period or on a fixed date. A jokhami hundi tied payment to the safe arrival of goods, which makes it close to a primitive insurance contract. A trader choosing between them was choosing between speed, delay and risk."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The paper was never the point. The point was the network of merchants and bankers who knew each other's credit well enough to honour it."}, {"t": "p", "html": "That is the part worth keeping. The ET piece is careful to say a hundi was not simply an old-fashioned piece of paper. It worked because the instrument was backed by merchants and indigenous bankers who understood one another's reputation. It cites the historian Claude Markovits, whose work on trading communities such as the Shikarpuris shows how such networks moved capital across borders and kept relationships alive far from home. The ET writer also says the hundi was not an early version of UPI. I agree with that. But it faced a problem that survives every technology since: how do you move value without moving the money?"}]}, {"heading": "Two", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "A second system arrives, and the two run side by side", "kicker": "The branch era"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Alongside these indigenous networks the British built a formal one. The Bank of Bengal was set up in 1806, the Bank of Bombay in 1840 and the Bank of Madras in 1843. In 1921 the three Presidency Banks merged into the Imperial Bank of India. The Reserve Bank of India began operating on 1 April 1935 under the RBI Act of 1934, and was nationalised on 1 January 1949. ET sums up the situation in 1947 neatly: India had two financial histories running at once, one of trust networks and one of formal banking."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "The Reserve Bank of India building in Mumbai behind trees", "caption": "Reserve Bank of India, Mumbai. Photo: Sailko (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 3.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "p", "html": "After Independence the question changed. It was no longer only how money moved, but who could get into the system that moved it. The Imperial Bank became the State Bank of India in 1955. On 19 July 1969 the government nationalised 14 major banks, and six more in 1980. RBI data quoted by ET show branches growing from 8,262 in 1969 to 59,752 by March 1990. Lead bank schemes, regional rural banks and priority-sector lending pushed formal finance toward people it had never reached."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Still, the branch stayed at the centre. To move money you often had to go to a counter, fill a form, hand over a cheque and wait while paper travelled between institutions. The hundi had been replaced by a better-governed, wider piece of paper, but the shape was the same."}]}, {"heading": "Three", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The paper disappears, one layer at a time", "kicker": "The rails era"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Electronic payment came in stages, and each stage is easy to miss because payment infrastructure is mostly invisible. RTGS went live on 26 March 2004, first for transfers between banks. NEFT followed in November 2005 and gave ordinary customers a nationwide electronic transfer. The Payment and Settlement Systems Act of 2007 gave the whole area a legal framework. NPCI was incorporated in 2008 as an umbrella body for retail payments. Immediate Payment Service, IMPS, arrived in 2010 with round-the-clock interbank transfers."}, {"t": "p", "html": "There was a catch. Faster rails are useless to people with nothing to pay from. The Pradhan Mantri Jan Dhan Yojana was announced on 15 August 2014 and launched on 28 August. By 22 July 2026, official data quoted by ET show 58.84 crore beneficiaries, ₹3.11 lakh crore in their accounts and 41.01 crore RuPay debit cards issued. Add Aadhaar for identity and the mobile phone as the access point, and you get the layers the SBI executive Rama Mohan Rao Amara calls the JAM trinity, Jan Dhan, Aadhaar and Mobile. UPI is built on top of them."}]}, {"heading": "Four", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Ten years, and the numbers that matter", "kicker": "The QR era"}, {"t": "p", "html": "UPI went live as a pilot on 11 April 2016, with then RBI Governor Raghuram Rajan launching it in Mumbai. Twenty-one banks took part. The idea was a common platform for instant push and pull payments, linking several bank accounts through one interface. It reused what existed: IMPS for instant transfer, banks for the accounts, the phone for the screen. What it added was interoperability."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The scale since is hard to take in. The PIB release says annual transactions went from about 2 crore in 2016-17 to 24,162 crore in 2025-26, a rise of nearly 12,000 times, while annual value went from ₹0.07 lakh crore to about ₹314 lakh crore. The Economic Times, citing NPCI, puts July 2026 at 23.66 billion transactions worth ₹29.88 lakh crore."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The World Bank gives the comparison. Between July and September 2025, it says, UPI processed about 59 billion transactions, more than Mastercard's global total of roughly 54 billion and approaching Visa's roughly 82 billion. In 2024 UPI alone handled 172.2 billion transactions, against a combined 79.8 billion across the fast payment systems of eleven Latin American and Caribbean countries, Brazil's Pix included."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A signboard reading BHIM UPI Accepted Here with a large QR code, standing among trees near a pile of bricks", "caption": "A BHIM/UPI sign at the Shaurya Smarak, Bhopal. Photo: Suyash.dwivedi (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/lr2-g3.jpg"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Now look at the sign above. It is a banner on a post in a park. It has no counter, no staff and no opening hours. The SBI executive's column calls the QR code at a roadside tea stall \"perhaps the smallest branch of India's financial system\". The government figure behind that: 86% of person-to-merchant transactions in 2025-26 were below ₹500. The tiny payment is the product. Large transfers were already possible through banks."}]}, {"heading": "Five", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Not the speed. The trust.", "kicker": "What actually changed"}, {"t": "p", "html": "It is tempting to say UPI is faster. It is, but the hundi was also faster than carrying coins, and the NEFT transfer was faster than a cheque. Speed does not explain a country switching habits."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The World Bank authors give a better account. Their point is that India turned instant payments from a tool for large bank-to-bank settlement into a service for everyone. Earlier mobile money systems showed that simple phone payments could spread, but most were run by one provider and did not work with each other. India's move was to make the system open and interoperable, and to let non-bank apps compete on how it felt to use. They write that the real revolution was not technology alone but how technology is felt by people."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The SBI column puts the same thing in one line: \"UPI did not simply digitise cash. It digitised trust.\" A hundi trusted a network of merchants. A branch trusted a building and a passbook. UPI asks you to trust a mobile number, a virtual payment address or a square code, enough to settle a promise between two people who may never meet. The column, I should say, is written by an executive of the country's largest bank, so read the praise with that in mind. But the same line about trust is what the hundi era ran on."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Each generation found a new way to let strangers trust a promise. The paper, the building, the rail and the code are the packaging."}]}, {"heading": "Six", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Where the sources say the next test is", "kicker": "The open questions"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Both the World Bank and the SBI column end on cautions, not victory laps. The World Bank names operational resilience, concentration risk that could hurt competition, transparency for users and open finance. The SBI column adds fraud: social engineering, mule accounts, synthetic identities and AI-enabled scams. It also raises a question about data. A richer digital footprint should widen a citizen's choices, it argues, not narrow them, which is why consent and purpose limits matter."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Two numbers show how fast the system is still changing. The column says more than 800 million UPI transactions worth over ₹35,000 crore in July 2026 were authenticated by fingerprint or face. It also says UPI now runs in nine countries, with Cambodia the latest in 2026. The PIB release lists eight countries, and gives 703 banks live on UPI as of March 2026 against the column's 741 in August. The gap is probably timing, but I cannot confirm that from either source."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Where the sources differPIB (30 April 2026): 703 banks live, 8 countries accepting UPI. SBI column (24 August 2026): 741 banks, 9 countries. The PIB release also has internal inconsistencies in its launch-month figures, so I used only its annual numbers."}, {"t": "p", "html": "A hundi worked because the merchants behind it were known. The next problem is making that true for a small shopkeeper whose digital receipts have never counted as proof of anything. The SBI column imagines using consented payment data, GST information and RBI's Unified Lending Interface to help lenders judge such borrowers better. It is a hope, not a result, and it is written by one bank's executive."}]}, {"heading": "Seven", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Why this matters in insurance distribution", "kicker": "For you, as my own note"}, {"t": "p", "html": "This section is my reading, not something the sources say. Three things from the history look relevant to your work."}, {"t": "p", "html": "First, the jokhami hundi. India had a payment instrument that bundled a transfer with a risk condition, payable on safe arrival of goods. Payments and risk cover were close relatives long before the two industries separated."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Second, the pattern that worked. UPI built open rails, let banks and non-banks plug in, and competed on the experience above them. That is the argument the World Bank makes, and it is the same argument the insurance distribution fight turns on: who is allowed to build on top, and what the economics look like when they do. It is a useful lens for the IRDAI commission-cap debate in today's paper."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Third, the limit. The rails made payments effortless. They did not by themselves make credit or cover effortless. If the SBI column's next step is right, the rail for protection and credit is consent-based data, and that is where a distribution platform could matter. Treat that as a question to chase, not a forecast."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "A photographed album page with a small handwritten hundi in English and Persian, signed William Moorcroft, with a wax seal", "caption": "A hundi for 11,000 rupees, signed by William Moorcroft on 20 December 1824. Photograph of the document from an album in the World Digital Library, via Wikimedia Commons (public domain).", "img": "photos/lr2-hero.jpg"}}, "3": {"title": "What IRDAI's commission reset changes", "fact": "Long read, Thursday 8 October 2026. About 2,300 words.", "intro": "The regulator says distributor pay has grown several times faster than the business it is paid on. Brokers say the fix would cost jobs and reach. Here is what the draft proposes, where the numbers conflict, and what it would mean for the people who sell insurance.", "groups": [{"heading": "The problem, in IRDAI's words", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "On 23 September IRDAI released a two-part consultation paper called \"Recalibrating Economics of Insurance Distribution\". Its argument is simple. After the 2023 reforms gave insurers more freedom on how they spend, distribution costs went back up, while the number of people with cover barely moved."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The paper backs that with growth rates. In a life-insurance sample covering about 92% of corporate-agent premium, new business premium grew 28% between FY23 and FY25 while total distributor pay grew 125% (<a href=\"https://bfsi.economictimes.indiatimes.com/articles/irdai-proposes-sweeping-insurance-distribution-overhaul-cuts-eom-caps-commissions-targets-mis-selling/134442049\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ET BFSI</a>). The paper's own line, as quoted by ThePrint, is that \"remuneration is growing four to five times faster than the business it is paid on\". In general insurance, premium sourced through brokers grew 37% while commissions grew 173%, and average broker commission went from 8.5% to 17%. In motor, premium grew about 34% and commissions about 259% (<a href=\"https://theprint.in/health/irdais-insurance-sector-overhaul-proposes-lower-costs-tighter-commissions-simpler-distribution/3053657/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ThePrint</a>)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Two figures describe what the regulator thinks the cost buys. The first is <a href=\"https://www.indiatoday.in/business/story/irdais-big-insurance-reset-explained-why-its-new-proposal-has-sparked-a-war-of-words-3010939-2026-10-07\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">persistency</a> (the share of policies still active years later). For life insurance, 61st-month persistency is 48%, against 71% for policies bought online. The paper reads this as evidence that choices made independently tend to last. The second is complaints. General insurance grievances went from 78,347 in FY23 to 1,37,361 in FY25, and nearly 69% of complaints on IRDAI's Bima Bharosa portal were linked to claims (ThePrint)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "One more number matters because it is about incentives, not averages. IRDAI says distributor pay is nearly 27% of first-year premium, and rewards and incentives add another 30% to 60% on top of base commission. So base commission alone understates the real cost (ET BFSI). That is why the draft counts every form of payment."}]}, {"heading": "What the draft proposes", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Lower expense limits", "kicker": "Part one: how much insurers can spend"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The central change is a lower ceiling on <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/business/insurance-brokers-oppose-new-reforms-as-commission-cuts-may-lead-to-job-losses/article71552334.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">expenses of management</a> (EoM, the share of premium an insurer spends on running costs, including commissions). For life insurers: 15% within two years and 12.5% within five, with cuts starting in FY2027-28. Insurers already below the benchmark in FY25 would go to 10% within five years. For general insurers: 25% in two years and 20% in five. For general insurers, EoM would be measured against gross direct premium income in India, not gross written premium, which removes inward reinsurance and the double counting that comes with it (ET BFSI, ThePrint). IRDAI would also cut its own fee on insurers from 0.05% to 0.04% of premium, capped at ₹20 crore."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Commission limits by product, channel and effort", "kicker": "Part two: what distributors can earn"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Commission limits would depend on segment, line of business, channel, product complexity and the effort of selling and servicing. Two adjustments stand out. Business sourced from towns with populations below 10 lakh would get an extra 10% on the applicable limit, and business from towns below 50,000 people and rural areas an extra 20%. Distributors in a closed architecture, who can sell only certain insurers' products, would have higher commission opportunities than open-architecture distributors (ET BFSI)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Moneycontrol reports specific caps: first-year commissions of 5% to 20% depending on tenure, health at 15% to 20% for first-time purchases, and renewals and porting at 5% to 10%. IBAI counts more than 30 caps across products and channels (<a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/business/insurance-brokers-oppose-new-reforms-as-commission-cuts-may-lead-to-job-losses/article71552334.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine</a>). The exact schedule is in the paper, which I did not read."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Incentives, bundling and accountability", "kicker": "Part three: the rules around selling"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["All remuneration, direct or indirect, cash or non-cash, would count as commission. For bank and NBFC staff selling insurance, volume-linked and reward-linked incentives would be banned. The paper names foreign and domestic trips, luxury gifts, milestone bonuses and contest rewards as examples.", "Compulsory bundling of insurance with loans would be prohibited, with specific package offers allowed where there is a demonstrable customer benefit. Where insurance is offered as additional security, the customer would be told the loan rate with and without it.", "Mis-selling would attach to the individual. The functional identity of the salesperson, PoSP or agent would be tagged to each policy, and mis-selling incidents would become part of a public performance record. Mis-selling would also trigger commission clawback by the insurer.", "Listed practices that could count as mis-selling include presenting a regular-premium product as single premium, selling term cover to people without dependants or outside working age, selling ULIPs to risk-averse or older customers without explaining charges, and selling insurance as a fixed deposit.", "Insurers would face mandatory cost audits covering all expenses including non-monetary incentives. Distributors with insurance revenue above ₹100 crore would need cost audits, and those above ₹50 crore would publicly disclose revenue, expenses, related-party payments and profit after tax."]}, {"t": "story", "title": "Three categories and a digital channel", "kicker": "Part four: who is allowed to sell"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The draft would replace today's intermediary types with three: Insurance Distribution Entities, Insurance Distribution Persons and Market Infrastructure Institutions, under the principle \"same structure, same functions, same norms\". Entry and capital requirements would be lower. Hospitals could distribute health insurance as entities with lower commission limits, and non-dealer garages could sell motor insurance as associates of insurers. Market Infrastructure Institutions are digital, not-for-profit marketplaces. Bima Sugam is named as one, and IRDAI expects it to be running with a wide range of products in four to six months. Starting one would need at least 11 insurers with no significant holder (ET BFSI). The stated aim is a model where \"insurance is also purchased\", not only sold."}]}, {"heading": "Where the reports disagree", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Reading several outlets side by side shows gaps that only the paper can settle."}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["The EoM base. ET BFSI says life insurers would go to 15% \"of premium\". The Hindu says 15% \"of gross direct premium income\", from a present level of 30% to 35%. These are different yardsticks, and I do not know which is right.", "Motor commissions. ET BFSI gives an average of about 24% across a range of 13% to 50%. ThePrint says motor commissions nearly tripled from about 9% to 25%. These can both be true if one is a level and the other a change, but the outlets do not say so.", "The size of the cut. CNBC-TV18 quotes Swami Saran Sharma of Helios Global Solutions saying the proposal cuts commissions by about 10%. The Moneycontrol caps look like cuts of very different sizes by product. I cannot reconcile the two without the paper.", "The deadline. Feedback closes on 25 October. IBAI asked on 6 October for the end of December. IRDAI has not, in anything I read, agreed. A Moneycontrol interview with chairman Ajay Seth is headlined \"IRDAI rules out glide path for commission caps\", but it is paywalled and I could not read it."]}]}, {"heading": "The case for and against", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The consumer-first argument", "kicker": "For"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Monika Halan, a financial educator, told India Today the proposal puts the policyholder at the centre for the first time and that removing caps in 2023 produced \"a free-for-all\", with commissions, costs, mis-selling complaints and unpaid claims all rising. She thinks two and five years are too slow and says the cap should apply within six months. The India Today piece also describes two mis-selling cases, a retired Kolkata couple who paid about ₹12 lakh for two policies sold by a bank assistant manager, and a Mumbai man told he needed insurance to get a ₹40 lakh loan (<a href=\"https://www.indiatoday.in/business/story/irdais-big-insurance-reset-explained-why-its-new-proposal-has-sparked-a-war-of-words-3010939-2026-10-07\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">India Today</a>). Those are individual accounts in a news story, not data."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The effort, choice and process arguments", "kicker": "Against"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Mahavir Chopra of insurtech Beshak does not dispute that mis-selling and excess commission exist. His objection is that a common cap treats a distributor who explains exclusions and helps with claims the same as one who only processes a transaction. He suggests grading pay by product complexity and distributor capability, with part of it tied to persistency, complaints and satisfaction. Thomas Devasia, a former IRDAI member for non-life, told CNBC-TV18 that hard caps are regressive because tightening the EoM framework would have been enough, and that the problem was enforcement of the 2023 rules (<a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/irdai-insurance-distribution-reforms-commission-caps-dark-patterns-mii-policyholders-19997899.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a>)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "IBAI, which represents brokers and so has a direct interest, argues four things. First, the 2023 framework has a scheduled review in 2028, and the draft reverses it \"without a regulatory impact assessment\". Second, the paper has no mechanism to make sure savings reach policyholders as lower premiums, so, it says, savings would move from distributors' and insurers' employees to insurers' owners. Third, an \"untested\" framework that does not exist elsewhere in the world needs wider consultation. Fourth, jobs: it estimates at least 10 lakh livelihoods at risk over five years, which it itself calls a preliminary estimate, against a total of more than 83 lakh distribution professionals by IRDAI's data. IBAI also supports parts of the draft: the ban on compulsory loan bundling, stronger suitability checks, and clawbacks for proven mis-selling (<a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/finance/irdai-distribution-paper-ibai-more-time-for-consultation-asks-deadline-till-december-20005633.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18</a>, <a href=\"https://www.thehindubusinessline.com/business/insurance-brokers-oppose-new-reforms-as-commission-cuts-may-lead-to-job-losses/article71552334.ece\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hindu BusinessLine</a>)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "One point nobody in the coverage rebuts directly: IBAI's claim that savings would not reach customers. IRDAI's stated aim is to expand the risk pool and improve policyholder returns, but the reports I read do not show a mechanism either way."}]}, {"heading": "Who feels it, and how", "blocks": [{"t": "callout", "html": "From here, my analysisEverything above is reported. This section is my reading of those reports. It is a set of questions and plausible effects, not findings."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The arithmetic is thin", "kicker": "Small, retail distributors"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Indraneel Chatterjee, co-founder of InsuranceDekho, gave Moneycontrol two worked examples. A 5% commission on an own-damage premium of ₹800 leaves ₹40 at the entity level, before PoSP splits. A PoSP who sells three or four policies a month with combined premium of about ₹20,000 would, at a 4% net rate after broker retention, earn about ₹800 a month. The sums check out (5% of ₹800 is ₹40, and 4% of ₹20,000 is ₹800). The inputs are his assumptions, and he runs an affected business. The point stands as an illustration: on low-ticket products, a flat percentage leaves almost nothing for field effort. The draft's 10% and 20% allowances for small towns and rural areas look like a partial answer to this, but I have not seen anyone quantify them."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Limited earnings hit, bigger product-mix question", "kicker": "Banks"}, {"t": "p", "html": "IIFL Capital, quoted by Business Standard, puts the banking system's bancassurance income pool at about $2.2 billion, around 10% of bank fee income, close to 3% of profit after tax and about 6 basis points of return on assets. Analysts therefore see a limited profit impact. They expect banks to reconsider which products they push in branches, favouring higher-commission ones and offsetting lower payouts with volume. The loan-bundling ban and the staff-incentive ban hit the bank channel in a different way, because they change how branch staff are paid and what they can attach to a loan (<a href=\"https://www.business-standard.com/industry/banking/rbi-to-firm-up-views-on-irdai-s-proposed-insurance-commission-overhaul-126100701296_1.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Business Standard</a>)."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Cheaper to run on paper, harder to steer", "kicker": "Insurers"}, {"t": "p", "html": "A lower EoM ceiling forces insurers to find savings somewhere. Commissions are the largest controllable item, which is why the two parts of the draft go together. The Business Standard report adds that insurers could steer business to group companies where the economics are easier to manage. The Hindu notes the timing, six months after FDI in insurance was raised to 100%, and asks whether the transition treats new and established insurers differently. That question is the Hindu's, not a finding."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The market reacted on day one", "kicker": "Online aggregators and insurtechs"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Moneycontrol reports PB Fintech fell 15% and Turtlemint 20% after the paper was released, and ThePrint says insurance stocks came under sharp selling. One day's move is not a verdict. The draft cuts two ways for this group. Lower commissions squeeze margins, while the push towards digital and pull-based buying through Bima Sugam could bring in new competitors, or in IRDAI's framing, remove layers. Which effect dominates depends on the final caps, which are not yet written."}]}, {"heading": "What to watch", "blocks": [{"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "25 October", "v": "Feedback deadline on the draft. IBAI has asked for the end of December."}, {"k": "RBI comments", "v": "Governor Sanjay Malhotra said on 7 October that the RBI has not firmed up its view and will send comments. He added that it is primarily in the consumer's interest to reduce costs and prevent mis-selling."}, {"k": "Bima Sugam", "v": "IRDAI expected it to run with a wide product range in four to six months from the draft. In my reading, a delay would leave the digital channel without its flagship."}, {"k": "Final schedule", "v": "The product-level caps and the small-town allowances are where the real economics sit. Watch for changes to those, not the headline EoM numbers."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "The draft is two arguments in one document. One is about cost, and the evidence IRDAI gives on pay outrunning premium is strong. The other is about what the cost buys, and nobody has shown yet how a cap turns into better persistency or fewer claim disputes."}, {"t": "p", "html": "For your own work, three questions would sharpen your view before the deadline. What share of your distribution is low-ticket, where a flat cap bites hardest? What evidence of advice and servicing effort do you currently keep per policy, given the paper ties pay to effort? And how much of your revenue depends on renewal commission, given the reported 5% to 10% renewal and porting caps in health?"}]}, {"heading": "Sources", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["ET BFSI, 23 September: IRDAI proposes sweeping insurance distribution overhaul", "ThePrint, 25 September: IRDAI sector overhaul proposes lower costs, tighter commissions", "The Hindu, 29 September: Insurance 2.0 after 100% FDI", "Moneycontrol, 24 September: Six key concerns in IRDAI draft paper", "CNBC-TV18, 24 September: IRDAI distribution reforms and what they mean for policyholders", "India Today, 7 October: IRDAI big insurance reset explained", "Business Standard, 7 October: RBI to firm up views on IRDAI commission overhaul", "CNBC-TV18 and Hindu BusinessLine, 6 October: IBAI seeks more time"]}]}], "hero": {"alt": "A fan of Indian 100 and 2000 rupee notes on a white sheet", "caption": "Indian rupee notes. Photo: Monito (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0), cropped.", "img": "photos/d3-hero.jpg"}}, "4": {"title": "How Policybazaar built a market", "fact": "Startup case study, edition 4. Founded 2008, listed 2021, now profitable.", "intro": "Policybazaar started as a comparison website in 2008, when insurers saw it as a threat, and became the largest online seller of insurance in India. This is how it happened, where the record disagrees with itself, and why it matters while IRDAI rewrites commission rules.", "groups": [{"heading": "The case study", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "The idea came from travel", "kicker": "2008"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Policybazaar was founded in June 2008 by Yashish Dahiya, Alok Bansal and Avaneesh Nirjar (Wikipedia and Founder Nation agree on the date and the names). Dahiya had studied at IIT Delhi and IIM Ahmedabad, then INSEAD, worked at Bain and then at the online travel business ebookers. Forbes India quotes him in 2017 saying that the idea of becoming an aggregator emerged from ebookers. That is the core of the model: in travel, a site lists every airline's price and the customer chooses. That is the model it applied to insurance."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Early money came from Info Edge, the company behind Naukri. The amount is where sources split. Forbes India's 2018 profile says ₹20 crore. Wikipedia says ₹30 million, which is ₹3 crore. These cannot both be right, and I could not find a primary source, so I am not giving a figure. Intel Capital invested in 2011."}, {"t": "p", "html": "A second conflict is when co-founder Nirjar left. Forbes in 2017 and Founder Nation say 2011. Forbes in 2018 says 2013. I have not resolved it."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Showing cost and commission", "kicker": "The product that insurers disliked"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Dahiya told Forbes in 2017 that insurers went to the regulator to \"kill us\". One reason, in the same coverage, was that Policybazaar's selling point was transparency: it showed customers what a policy cost and what the commissions were. For an industry whose sales ran through agents, a site that lined up products side by side threatened the usual way of selling. The company survived, and the regulator eventually licensed web aggregators as a category. A web-aggregator licence existed, since Policybazaar surrendered one in 2021."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street", "caption": "Bombay Stock Exchange, Dalal Street, where Policybazaar's parent PB Fintech listed in 2021. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "Profit, scale and voice calls", "kicker": "2016 to 2018"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Forbes India's 2018 profile says the aggregator business turned profitable in FY2016-17. At that time online insurance was about 5% of the Indian market, and Policybazaar had about half of that. In June 2018 a round led by SoftBank put in $238 million. Forbes breaks that into $200 million of new capital and $38 million bought from existing holders. Info Edge's share of that deal was $58 million. Tencent invested in 2019."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The same profile shows how the company actually sold. Customers filled in a form online, then the call centre did the work: about 2 lakh voice calls a day, with speech analytics on the recordings, and around 75% of conversations in Hindi. The website was the lead source, and the phone was the sales channel. That detail matters for anyone who thinks online insurance means self-service. In India it largely meant online lead generation and human selling. The company also launched DocPrime, a doctor-booking service, in the same period."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Listing, and a change of licence", "kicker": "2021"}, {"t": "p", "html": "PB Fintech went public on 15 November 2021 and raised ₹5,625 crore. Earlier that year, in June 2021, the company obtained a broking licence, surrendered its web-aggregator licence and began opening offline outlets. In plain terms, it stopped being only a comparison site. As a broker it can advise and place business and be paid commissions, with different rules from an aggregator."}, {"t": "figure", "alt": "A Life Insurance Corporation of India death-claim settlement photo", "caption": "A death-claim settlement event, LIC Janashree Bima Yojana. Photo: Ministry of Information and Broadcasting, Government of India (Wikimedia Commons, GODL-India).", "img": "photos/lr4-g0.jpg"}, {"t": "story", "title": "The June 2026 quarter", "kicker": "Where it stands"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Fortune India reported on 5 August 2026 for the first quarter of FY27. Revenue was ₹1,888 crore, up 40.1%. Profit after tax was ₹163 crore, up 92.4%. Total premium sold was ₹8,372 crore, up 41%. Core insurance renewal revenue was running at an annualised rate (ARR) of ₹999 crore, a run rate and not revenue earned in the quarter. The platform had sold 7.16 crore policies to 2.81 crore customers. Its profit margin moved from minus 47% in the June 2021 quarter to plus 9% now. I am leaving out full-year FY26 profit growth because two sources give 90% and 115%."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Fines, a breach and a raid", "kicker": "The other record"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Wikipedia lists three events. In May 2021 the insurance regulator fined the company ₹2.4 million. In July 2022 there was a data breach. In January 2025 there was a GST raid. I have not read primary documents on any of them, so these are listed as reported and not assessed."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Lessons, and my reading", "kicker": "What a founder or product person can take from it"}, {"t": "bullets", "items": ["Pick a customer pain the industry hides. Showing prices and commissions made the product popular with buyers and unpopular with insurers. This is supported by Dahiya's own account.", "Online demand, human closing. The 2 lakh daily calls show that in complex, low-frequency products, the website finds the customer and a person completes the sale. This is my reading of the Forbes figures.", "Renewals are the quiet engine. Renewal revenue, running at an annualised ₹999 crore per Fortune India, is the part that compounds, though I do not have the same figure for earlier years to show the trend.", "Regulation is part of the business. The company moved from aggregator to broker in 2021. Today it faces IRDAI's draft commission caps, whose feedback window closes 25 October."]}, {"t": "callout", "html": "Why it matters this weekThe draft proposes lower commissions on health, motor and credit-linked insurance. ICRA expects the effect in FY28. I do not know how much of PB Fintech's revenue comes from the product lines affected, so I will not guess its exposure."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next case study: a different company in the next edition."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "DLF Galleria shopping complex in Gurgaon lit up at night", "caption": "Gurgaon, where Policybazaar is based. File photo, not of the company. Photo: Slyronit (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY-SA 4.0).", "img": "photos/lr4-hero.jpg"}}, "5": {"title": "How Zerodha built a moat, and what happens when regulation taxes it", "fact": "Business teardown, edition 5. Founded 2010, bootstrapped, debt-free.", "intro": "Zerodha took discount broking from six employees and ₹2 crore to India's most profitable brokerage, without raising venture money. Its FY25 numbers show the first real crack: a regulatory squeeze on the options traders it earns from. This is how the machine works, and where it is exposed.", "groups": [{"heading": "Chronology", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["2010: Nithin and Nikhil Kamath found Zerodha in Bengaluru as a discount brokerage. The name joins \"zero\" with \"rodha\", Sanskrit for barrier. Six employees, ₹2 crore from Nithin's savings and a Singapore-based friend (Wikipedia).", "2010 onwards: flat ₹20 per executed order on intraday and F&O trades; equity delivery commission-free (Wikipedia).", "2013: Kailash Nadh joins to start the technology team and stays CTO; the platforms are built in-house (Wikipedia).", "2014: Rainmatter, an early-stage fintech fund, is set up; it later backs CRED, Smallcase, Sensibull, GoldenPi and WintWealth (Wikipedia).", "2019: Zerodha overtakes ICICI Securities as India's largest retail broker by active clients. True Beacon, an alternative investment fund, starts the same year (Wikipedia).", "2020: a self-assessed $1 billion valuation through an ESOP buyback. Customers grow from about 1.3 million in early 2020 to nearly 10 million by the end of 2022 (Wikipedia).", "2023: Zerodha Fund House launches with Smallcase, selling direct-only index funds that pay no distributor commissions (Wikipedia).", "2025-26: invests in market-data platform Tijori Finance (Nov 2025) and gets a merchant banking licence through Zerodha Corporate Advisors (2026), per Wikipedia."]}]}, {"heading": "The machine, part by part", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Content instead of advertising", "kicker": "Acquisition and distribution"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Zerodha spent ₹47 crore on advertising in FY25, about 0.5% of its ₹8,847 crore revenue (our calculation from the Entrackr figures). Its distribution runs on free education and word of mouth: Varsity, Daily Brief, Zero1 and other content properties that Kamath says have become default places to learn finance, and Varsity Live, which crossed 2 lakh registrations for a free course (Z-Connect). Kamath also describes a deliberate stance against growth tricks: no trackers or analytics in the apps, no revenue-driven A/B testing, no notifications engineered to make people trade. He frames this as philosophy; functionally, it means acquisition cost stays near zero while competitors pay for installs. A Mint article cited in the same post notes Zerodha is the only broker that does not ask for intrusive app permissions."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Free to open, free to invest", "kicker": "Conversion"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Account opening was made free in February 2024, and equity delivery trades carry no brokerage (Z-Connect, Wikipedia). The pricing removes every reason not to start. The paid product is the active trader: ₹20 per executed intraday or F&O order. Kamath's own framing is blunt about where revenue comes from: \"almost every brokerage business model relies on earnings from active traders who trade options\" (Z-Connect)."}, {"t": "story", "title": "About ₹12,200 per user per year", "kicker": "Money per customer"}, {"t": "p", "html": "FY25 revenue of ₹8,847 crore across 7.26 million users works out to roughly ₹12,200 per user per year (our calculation; the user count is NSE data cited by Entrackr and is not the same base as paying traders, so treat this as an average, not per-trader yield). Brokerage is the primary stream; interest income, software services and investment-management fees add to it (Entrackr). The cost base is small for the scale: total FY25 costs were ₹3,238 crore, including ₹539 crore of salaries and ₹2,328 crore of fees and commissions, producing a 63.78% EBITDA margin and 32% ROCE (Entrackr)."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Assets that stay, and lend", "kicker": "The renewal engine"}, {"t": "p", "html": "The repeat business is not a subscription; it is that client money stays on the platform. Assets held by Zerodha customers account for about 10% of all retail and HNI assets in the country (Z-Connect). On top of that base, newer products compound: the Margin Trading Facility, which lets clients buy shares with borrowed money, reached a book of about ₹5,000 crore and roughly 5% market share within nine months of launch, and Zerodha Capital's loan-against-securities book stands at ₹430 crore with same-day disbursal (Z-Connect)."}, {"t": "story", "title": "A balance sheet as the moat", "kicker": "Defensibility"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Zerodha's exchange-calculated net worth is a little over ₹13,000 crore (LC Gupta method, which excludes private investments and discounts pledged assets), more than 50% of the client funds it handles on any given day, with zero debt (Z-Connect). FY25 cash and bank balances were ₹22,679 crore against total current assets of ₹35,719 crore (Entrackr). It self-hosts core systems, so customer data stays on its own infrastructure, publishes incident disclosures without being required to, and runs Kite Backup, a WhatsApp-based emergency trading channel on separate infrastructure (Z-Connect). Kamath presents the no-dark-patterns stance as a trust moat; the financial cushion is what lets him keep it when revenue falls."}]}, {"heading": "The crack", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "₹9,993 cr₹8,847 crRevenue from operations₹5,496 cr₹4,237 crNet profitFY24FY25Unit: ₹ crore, FY ended March. Source: RoC filings via Entrackr, 21 Nov 2025."}, {"t": "p", "html": "FY25 was the first down year in the company's public record. Revenue from operations fell 11.5% to ₹8,847 crore from ₹9,993 crore, and net profit fell 22.9% to ₹4,237 crore from ₹5,496 crore (RoC statements via Entrackr; a Tracxn-based report by Indian Startup News puts revenue at ₹8,868.2 crore and profit at ₹4,236.7 crore, a small difference in source data, shown here as reported by each). The company still paid ₹1,395 crore in taxes, and directors Nithin Kamath, Nikhil Kamath and Seema Patil drew ₹228 crore in combined remuneration (Entrackr)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The cause is regulatory, and Kamath names it himself. SEBI's steps to cool retail F&O speculation - a higher securities transaction tax on options, fewer weekly expiries, a higher basic-services demat limit, and removal of the exchange transaction-charge rebate - began biting from October 2024. In the June 2025 quarter, brokerage revenue fell about 40% from a year earlier, and Kamath wrote that regulators are evaluating whether to stop weekly options altogether, in which case Zerodha would be \"forced to start charging brokerage for equity delivery trades\" to keep the business tenable (Z-Connect). Active clients fell from 7.95 million in February to 7 million in October 2025 on NSE data, while Groww, which listed in November 2025, holds 12 million active users; Groww's September-quarter profit rose 12% to ₹471.33 crore even as its revenue fell 9.5% (Indian Startup News)."}, {"t": "callout", "html": "The risk, in one lineZerodha's moat is its balance sheet and trust; its exposure is that the revenue engine sits on a product, retail options trading, that the regulator considers harmful and is actively shrinking. The FY25 decline is small because the rules landed mid-year; the June 2025 quarter's 40% brokerage fall is the fuller picture, in the founder's own numbers."}]}, {"heading": "Lessons, with the evidence behind each", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["Distribution can be a by-product of generosity. Free education and zero-friction pricing replaced a marketing budget; advertising was about 0.5% of revenue in FY25 (Entrackr figures, our calculation).", "Let the customer keep the first product free and charge where intensity is highest. Delivery investing is free; the ₹20 fee sits on frequent F&O and intraday orders (Wikipedia). The risk is that regulators can see the same concentration.", "A balance sheet is a strategy, not an outcome. Net worth above 50% of client funds and zero debt (Z-Connect) are what allow Zerodha to refuse dark patterns and survive a 40% quarterly brokerage fall without changing its model.", "Founder pessimism is cheap insurance. Kamath publicly expected the regulatory hit years before it came, and judges the business on a five-year moving average rather than quarterly growth (Z-Connect). The trade-off is visible too: Groww now has nearly twice the active clients (Indian Startup News).", "Adjacencies spread the bet slowly. Asset management, loans against securities, MTF, merchant banking and the Rainmatter portfolio each reduce reliance on options income, but none is yet large enough to replace it (Wikipedia, Z-Connect)."]}, {"t": "p", "html": "Next teardown: a different company in the next edition."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street", "caption": "Dalal Street, Mumbai. Zerodha is a member of the BSE, NSE and MCX. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}}, "6": {"title": "Groww's money machine, listed", "fact": "Business teardown, edition 6. India's largest investment platform by active clients.", "intro": "Groww converts first-time investors into multi-product regulars at a profit margin most software companies would envy. Its first year as a listed company shows the machine working: 81% revenue growth in the March quarter, a 44.7% profit margin, and market-share gains in every product. It also shows the two dependencies that could hurt: derivatives regulation and the cost of buying growth.", "groups": [{"heading": "Chronology", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["2016: Groww starts in Bengaluru, founded by former Flipkart employees (Reuters). The company was incorporated in 2018 as Billionbrains Garage Ventures, per its corporate identity number; Groww's own IPO page uses 2018.", "March 2023: 5.37 million active clients on the NSE, a 15.09% share of the exchange's active base (prospectus).", "FY23 to FY25: revenue from operations compounds at 84.9% a year (prospectus).", "June 2025: 12.58 million NSE active clients, 26.27% share. Roughly one in three new SIPs in India that month was created on Groww; it held 18.5% of all active SIPs (prospectus, citing AMFI data).", "October 2025: acquires Fisdom, a wealth-management platform (shareholders' letter).", "4-7 November 2025: IPO of ₹6,632 crore at ₹95-100 a share, subscribed 17.6 times (Groww IPO page).", "12 November 2025: lists at ₹112, up 12%, and climbs to ₹124 on debut, valuing the company at ₹761 billion, about $8.6 billion, above listed peers Motilal Oswal and Angel One (Reuters).", "November 2025: first listed quarter, Q2 FY26, shows the regulatory squeeze: revenue down 9.5% year on year (Economic Times).", "March quarter 2026: the rebound. Total income ₹1,536 crore, up 81% year on year; profit ₹686 crore, a 44.7% margin (shareholders' letter).", "September 2026: adds over 1 lakh NSE active clients in the month, keeping the industry lead as the total base reaches 4.61 crore (NSE data via Startup Spotlight India)."]}]}, {"heading": "The machine", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "One app, five revenue lines", "kicker": "What Groww sells"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Groww is a direct-to-customer investment platform: stocks, derivatives, mutual funds, bonds, IPOs, margin funding and personal loans in one app (prospectus). Broking produced 84.5% of FY25 revenue, and management has been widening the mix since. By the March 2026 quarter, <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Stock_Exchange_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">equity derivatives</a> (futures and options on stocks and indices) were 54.6% of total income, with stocks, commodities, margin trading, credit and treasury income making up the rest (shareholders' letter)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Product attach is the engine: 72% of active users trade stocks, 60% use mutual funds, 10% trade equity derivatives (letter, Q4 FY26). Commodity derivatives reached 393,000 active traders, up 53.8% in the quarter, still only a 2.4% attach rate, which is why management calls it early. The credit business, loans distributed for partner banks plus some on Groww's own balance sheet, contributed 4.1% of consolidated profit in Q4 FY26."}, {"t": "story", "title": "The organic engine", "kicker": "Growth without ads, mostly"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Across FY23, FY24, FY25 and the June 2025 quarter, between 81% and 84% of new customers arrived organically, without paid marketing (prospectus). Of users who completed three years on the platform, 77.7% remained. Transacting users opened the app often: a daily-to-monthly active ratio of about 56% in FY25. Cohorts deepen with age: users acquired in early FY22 held 5.36 times more assets per user by FY25, generated 1.86 times more revenue per user, and 53% of them used two or more products (prospectus)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The listed-company wrinkle: acquisition cost is rising as Groww spends on brand. Customer acquisition cost reached ₹1,374 in H1 FY26, against ₹796 a year earlier, after branding campaigns (Economic Times on Q2 FY26). The company's own framing splits spending into \"cost to serve\" (technology and transaction charges, 14.7% of Q4 FY26 revenue), \"cost to grow\" (marketing, 8.5%) and \"cost to operate\" (mostly employees, 18.8%), leaving an EBITDA margin of 66.1% on the platform before the Fisdom and GrowwMF losses."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Regulation hits the core product", "kicker": "The squeeze"}, {"t": "p", "html": "<a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Securities_and_Exchange_Board_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SEBI</a> (the securities market regulator) tightened derivatives rules in October 2024, and the impact was direct: Groww's active broking users fell from 7.24 million in Q1 FY25 to 6.12 million in Q1 FY26 (prospectus). A \"true-to-label\" circular then cut revenue per derivatives order (Economic Times). The September 2025 quarter captured the combined effect: revenue fell 9.5% year on year while profit rose 12%, and the company disclosed that adjusting for a one-time incentive reversal, profit too would have declined 12-13%. Industry-wide, NSE active users shrank from 50.2 million in January 2025 to 45.3 million by September 2025; Groww's fell less, from 13.2 to 11.9 million, so share kept rising through the shrink."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The response is the diversification visible in the numbers: margin trading book up 22% in a quarter when indices fell 16-17% and the industry book contracted 7%; commodity derivatives scaling; credit contributing to profit. The two loss-makers are deliberate: Fisdom lost ₹10.2 crore and the GrowwMF fund house lost ₹21.4 crore in Q4 FY26, with Fisdom expected to be profitable in FY28 and GrowwMF needing five to six times its current assets under management to break even (management's statements in the letter)."}]}, {"heading": "The numbers", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Total income and profit", "kicker": "Five quarters, from IPO quarter to record quarter"}, {"t": "p", "html": "850309Q4 FY25949378Q1 FY261,071471Q2 FY261,261547Q3 FY261,536686Q4 FY26Total incomeProfit after taxUnit: ₹ crore, consolidated. Source: Q4 FY26 shareholders' letter, April 2026."}, {"t": "p", "html": "FY26 closed with revenue from operations of ₹4,645 crore and profit of ₹2,083 crore, audited and consolidated (Q1 FY27 filing). The newest quarter, Q1 FY27 ended June 2026, brought revenue of ₹1,501 crore, up 66% year on year, and profit of ₹735 crore, up 94%. Platform scale at March 2026: 21.6 million total transacting users, 16.7 million active users, ₹3.0 trillion in customer assets, up 36% year on year, and ₹25,000 crore of net inflows in the March quarter alone. Customer assets dipped 1.1% in that quarter despite inflows, because falling markets marked down existing holdings; the company publishes the split."}, {"t": "story", "title": "Market share, Q4 FY25 to Q4 FY26", "kicker": "Share gains across every product"}, {"t": "p", "html": "MF SIP inflows12.1%15.7%Retail cash turnover12.3%14.0%Retail deriv. premium6.8%10.6%MTF book0.9%2.7%Q4 FY25Q4 FY26Market share, %. Turnover shares now computed on two-sided turnover. Source: Q4 FY26 letter."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The cleanest read on the Groww thesis: in a year when the industry's active base shrank, Groww's share of <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Systematic_investment_plan\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SIP</a> (systematic investment plan, a recurring mutual fund investment) inflows rose from 12.1% to 15.7%, of retail cash-market turnover from 12.3% to 14.0%, of retail derivatives premium turnover from 6.8% to 10.6%, and of the margin-funding book from 0.9% to 2.7% (letter). Gaining share in a shrinking market is how a platform grows through a regulatory squeeze; it is also why the squeeze's cost lands on competitors first."}]}, {"heading": "The contrast", "blocks": [{"t": "story", "title": "Groww versus Zerodha", "kicker": "Yesterday's teardown meets today's"}, {"t": "p", "html": "Pair this with <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/debmcmlq4ejy-how-zerodha-built-a-moat\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">yesterday's Zerodha teardown</a> and the two strategies stand in relief. Zerodha, private and bootstrapped, earned ₹4,237 crore on ₹8,847 crore of revenue in FY25, but both shrank, revenue down 11.5% and profit down 22.9%, and it holds ₹22,679 crore in cash (RoC filings via Entrackr). Groww, venture-raised and now listed, earned ₹2,083 crore on ₹4,645 crore in FY26, growing 81% in the final quarter. The incumbent is bigger, more profitable and contracting; the challenger is half the profit, growing fast, and deploying IPO money into lending and wealth. Different periods, labelled: Zerodha's figures are FY25, Groww's are FY26. Same regulator squeezing both core products. Source: <a href=\"https://entrackr.com/fintrackr/zerodha-cash-reserve-stands-at-rs-22679-cr-in-fy25-10799921\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Entrackr on Zerodha's FY25 RoC filing</a>."}]}, {"heading": "What to watch", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["Q2 FY27 results, due around November by last year's pattern: does the 66% revenue growth of Q1 hold, and does acquisition cost keep climbing?", "The derivatives dependency: 54.6% of total income in Q4 FY26, with SEBI still tightening. Every new rule has so far shrunk the market faster than Groww's share gains could fully offset.", "Margin-funding risk: the MTF book grew 22% in a falling market. Leveraged books grow fastest into volatility; the March quarter's risk-cost increase, which management flagged, is the line to watch.", "Fisdom integration: management says profitability in FY28. Wealth management is the natural next attach product after stocks and funds.", "GrowwMF: the fund house needs five to six times current assets to break even, by management's own estimate. Watch new fund launches and whether the platform pushes its own funds to its 18.5% SIP share."]}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange tower on Dalal Street", "caption": "Dalal Street, Mumbai. Groww's parent, Billionbrains Garage Ventures, listed on the BSE and NSE on 12 November 2025. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}}, "7": {"title": "PhonePe's road back to IPO", "fact": "A business teardown: how 45% of UPI became a ₹12,000 crore filing, a March pause, and a February-March 2027 target at a lower price.", "intro": "PhonePe carries nearly half of India's instant-payment traffic, grew revenue 40% last year, and still reported a ₹1,727 crore loss. SEBI cleared its IPO in January, the company paused in March, and the listing is now aimed at early 2027. This is how the machine works, where the money is made and lost, and what the offering math says.", "groups": [{"heading": "The company", "blocks": [{"t": "facts", "items": [{"k": "Founded", "v": "December 2015, Bengaluru, by Sameer Nigam (CEO), Rahul Chari (CTO) and Burzin Engineer, all former Flipkart executives."}, {"k": "Ownership", "v": "Majority-owned by Walmart, which acquired it through Flipkart; General Atlantic, Ribbit Capital, Tiger Global and TVS Capital hold stakes from the 2023 round at a $12 billion pre-money valuation."}, {"k": "Scale", "v": "45.4% of UPI transaction volume and 48.7% of value in December 2025 (NPCI data). Over 4 crore merchants on the network (Inc42)."}, {"k": "IPO status", "v": "SEBI approval received 20 January 2026; paused in March 2026; revived in September 2026, targeting February-March 2027 (Inc42, IBJ)."}]}, {"t": "p", "html": "The chronology in one pass. PhonePe was incorporated in December 2015, acquired by Flipkart in April 2016, and its app went live on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Unified_Payments_Interface\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">UPI</a> (Unified Payments Interface, India's instant bank-to-bank payment rail) in August 2016 in partnership with Yes Bank. Walmart took control through its Flipkart acquisition, the two companies separated fully in 2022 with PhonePe shifting its domicile from Singapore back to India, and in 2023 the company raised $850 million at a $12 billion pre-money valuation: $350 million from General Atlantic, $200 million from Walmart, and the rest from Ribbit, Tiger Global, TVS and a further General Atlantic cheque. That domicile move matters to the IPO story: listing in India requires being Indian."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PhonePe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia chronology</a> / <a href=\"https://inc42.com/features/phonepe-profit-puzzle-upi-dominance-diversification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42 feature, 24 Sep 2025</a>"}]}, {"heading": "The engine: UPI dominance, and its speed limits", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "PhonePe's business starts with a simple fact: it is the front end for nearly half of India's instant payments, 45.4% of UPI volume and 48.7% of value in December 2025, ahead of Google Pay and Paytm, per <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/National_Payments_Corporation_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NPCI</a> (National Payments Corporation of India, the retail-body that runs UPI) data. The merchant side is the strategic asset: over 4 crore merchants, many acquired through the soundbox, the speaker that announces each payment, a device on which industry sources tell Inc42 the company earns 80% to 90% margins."}, {"t": "p", "html": "But the engine has three speed limits, and each explains a piece of the IPO story. First, UPI carried no merchant fee for a decade, so the highest-volume product earns almost nothing directly; payments still produced 88.5% of revenue in FY25, through adjacent charges. Second, volumes are exposed to policy: the August 2025 ban on real-money gaming knocked UPI transaction volumes down 23% and values down 26% from July levels, and GST enforcement pushed some vendors back to cash (Inc42). Third, share is drifting, 47.22% in June 2025 to 45.4% by December, still dominant, no longer growing."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The fee regime that could fix the economics is the new <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/upi-mdr-framework-rollout-deferred-january-2027-festive-season-rbi-10912304/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">merchant discount rate</a> (the fee merchants pay to accept a payment) framework: 0.4% on person-to-merchant UPI payments above ₹2,000, capped at ₹300. Its rollout, originally due 15 October, is now deferred to 1 January 2027 (Indian Express). For PhonePe the deferral cuts both ways: the revenue unlock slips past the planned listing window, but the company avoids a merchant-friction experiment in the middle of its IPO roadshow."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/features/phonepe-profit-puzzle-upi-dominance-diversification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42 feature, 24 Sep 2025</a> / <a href=\"https://indiabusinessjournal.com/full-story/2443/phonepe-gets-sebis-approval-for-ipo-may-mop-up-rs-12000-cr-in-public-offer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">India Business Journal, Jan 2026</a> / <a href=\"https://indianexpress.com/article/business/upi-mdr-framework-rollout-deferred-january-2027-festive-season-rbi-10912304/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Indian Express on the MDR deferral</a>"}, {"t": "p", "html": "June 202547.22%July 202546.64%December 202545.40%Share of UPI transaction volume; axis starts at 44.5%. December 2025 value share: 48.7%. Source: NPCI data via Inc42 and India Business Journal."}]}, {"heading": "The money: reading FY25 two ways", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "FY25 revenue rose 40.4% to ₹7,114.9 crore. The mix is the story inside the number: payments grew 23% to ₹6,299 crore, a sharp deceleration from the 77% growth of FY24, while everything else more than tripled, financial services (lending distribution, insurance broking, the payment gateway) to ₹557 crore from ₹207 crore, and the new bets (Share.Market, Pincode, the Indus Appstore) to ₹57 crore. Non-payments is now 11.5% of revenue; it was 5% a year earlier (Inc42)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Payments₹6,299 cr, +23% YoY (was +77% in FY24)Financial services₹557 cr, from ₹207 cr in FY24Other new bets₹57 cr, Share.Market, Pincode, app storeFY25 revenue from operations, ₹7,115 crore total (+40% YoY). Unit: ₹ crore. Source: Inc42, Entrackr analysis of the annual report, Sep 2025."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The bottom line needs care, because PhonePe reports it two ways and both are true. The reported net loss narrowed 13.4% to ₹1,727.4 crore, and the <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Earnings_before_interest,_taxes,_depreciation,_and_amortization\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">EBITDA</a> (earnings before interest, tax, depreciation and amortization, a proxy for operating cash profit) loss narrowed 54% to ₹413.6 crore, a margin of -6% against -18% in FY24 (Inc42). Strip out employee stock-option costs, a non-cash charge, and the picture flips positive: adjusted EBITDA of ₹1,477 crore (from ₹652 crore), adjusted profit after tax of ₹630 crore (from ₹197 crore), the first positive adjusted EBIT at ₹117 crore, and free cash flow positive with ₹1,202 crore of operating cash flow (CNBC-TV18, Entrackr, IBJ). The roughly ₹1,900 crore gap between the two versions is mostly stock compensation, which is real dilution for shareholders even though it is not cash. One more number frames the whole journey: accumulated losses stood at ₹14,860 crore at the end of FY25 (Entrackr)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Adjusted PAT (ex-ESOP)+197 FY24+630 FY25Reported net result-1,996 FY24-1,727 FY25Unit: ₹ crore. Adjusted figures exclude employee stock-option costs. Source: CNBC-TV18, Entrackr, Sep 2025."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/features/phonepe-profit-puzzle-upi-dominance-diversification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42 feature</a> / <a href=\"https://www.cnbctv18.com/business/phonepe-ipo-bound-company-revenue-topline-growth-surges-in-fy25-net-loss-narrows-cash-flow-19683170.htm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CNBC-TV18, 22 Sep 2025</a> / <a href=\"https://entrackr.com/fintrackr/phonepe-posts-rs-7115-cr-revenue-and-rs-630-cr-adjusted-pat-in-fy25-10487742\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Entrackr, 22 Sep 2025</a>"}]}, {"heading": "The diversification bets", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "Lending is the intended profit engine. PhonePe acquired an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Non-bank_financial_company\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">NBFC</a> (non-banking financial company, a lender that cannot take deposits) licence in 2023, lends against its merchant transaction data, and has added secured loans against mutual funds. The pitch: 4 crore merchants generate the underwriting data, so credit can be priced better than a bank's guess. The reality check is relative: Inc42 cites PhonePe disbursing about ₹300 crore a month in mid-2024, against Paytm's roughly ₹5,000 crore a quarter, in a field that also includes BharatPe, Jio Financial and every bank app."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Around lending sit three smaller bets, each a use for the payments user base. Pincode (April 2023) is a hyperlocal commerce app built on <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Open_Network_for_Digital_Commerce\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">ONDC</a> (Open Network for Digital Commerce, the government-backed open e-commerce protocol). Share.Market (August 2023) is a stockbroking and mutual-fund platform, competing with the Groww and Zerodha machines this series has already torn down. The Indus Appstore (February 2024) is a homegrown alternative to Google Play. Together with financial services they are the 11.5% of revenue that is supposed to become the IPO's growth story (Inc42, Wikipedia)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "The contrast that prices all of this is Paytm. Paytm is less dependent on payments (58% of a ₹6,900 crore revenue), far ahead in lending, and back to net profit, helped by selling Paytm Insider to Eternal for ₹2,400 crore and its PayPay stake for $279 million. PhonePe has the bigger engine and the weaker second act; the IPO asks public investors to fund the catch-up (Inc42)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/features/phonepe-profit-puzzle-upi-dominance-diversification/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42 feature</a> / <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/PhonePe\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Wikipedia chronology</a>"}]}, {"heading": "The IPO math", "blocks": [{"t": "p", "html": "The timeline tells the story. PhonePe filed its draft papers confidentially in September 2025. <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Securities_and_Exchange_Board_of_India\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">SEBI</a> (the Securities and Exchange Board of India, the markets regulator) cleared the offer on 20 January 2026, and the public <a href=\"https://www.phonepe.com/apollo/investor-relations/phonepe-limited-draft-red-herring-prospectus.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">draft red herring prospectus</a>, the offer document with everything except price and dates, followed. The plan: about ₹12,000 crore, mostly an <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Offer_for_sale\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">offer for sale</a> (existing investors selling their shares rather than the company raising new money) by Walmart, General Atlantic, Ribbit, Tiger Global and TVS Capital. Then, in March 2026, the company paused, citing global market volatility. In September 2026, with the merchant-fee framework settled, the IPO was revived: a refile by the end of this year and a listing targeted for February-March 2027 (Inc42, Mint via Inc42, IBJ)."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Three numbers in that paragraph deserve a second look. First, mostly-OFS means the money goes to exiting investors, not into the business; buyers of the IPO are funding the 2023 round's exit more than PhonePe's next chapter. Second, the valuation talk has compressed: the 2023 private round priced PhonePe at $12 billion pre-money, Inc42's September 2025 feature floated $12-15 billion IPO hopes, and the September 2026 revival reports a target near $10 billion. Third, the March pause and the revival bracket exactly one variable, market conditions, which is the one thing the company does not control and the thing the February-March 2027 window is betting on."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Source: <a href=\"https://inc42.com/buzz/with-upi-mdr-clarity-phonepe-eyes-ipo-by-march-2027/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Inc42, 16 Sep 2026</a> / <a href=\"https://indiabusinessjournal.com/full-story/2443/phonepe-gets-sebis-approval-for-ipo-may-mop-up-rs-12000-cr-in-public-offer\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">India Business Journal, Jan 2026</a> / <a href=\"https://www.phonepe.com/apollo/investor-relations/phonepe-limited-draft-red-herring-prospectus.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">PhonePe DRHP, Jan 2026 (primary)</a>"}]}, {"heading": "Risks, and what to watch", "blocks": [{"t": "bullets", "items": ["UPI concentration: 88.5% of revenue touches payments, so every UPI policy decision, fees, caps, gaming rules, is a PhonePe earnings event. The gaming ban's 23% volume hit is the worked example.", "The fee deferral: the 0.4% merchant fee now starts 1 January 2027, weeks before the targeted listing. The first full quarter of fee revenue lands after the roadshow, so the IPO prices the promise, not the proof.", "Lending is late and crowded: Paytm disburses roughly five times PhonePe's monthly volume, and banks are in the same merchant-credit market.", "Valuation drift: $12 billion private in 2023, $10 billion targeted for 2027. A further market wobble between refile and listing reopens the March question.", "What to watch: the refiled DRHP (due by year-end per Inc42), monthly NPCI share data, the 1 January 2027 fee go-live, and any change to the February-March 2027 window."]}, {"t": "callout", "html": "How this teardown was sourcedFinancials are from Inc42's feature and Entrackr's and CNBC-TV18's analyses of PhonePe's FY25 annual report; the IPO timeline from India Business Journal and Inc42's September 2026 report; the chronology cross-checked against Wikipedia and PhonePe's own draft prospectus. Adjusted figures exclude stock-option costs and are labelled as such. Vendor-adjacent claims (soundbox margins, disbursement run-rates) are attributed to the sources that reported them."}, {"t": "p", "html": "Today's paper pairs with this read: <a href=\"https://files.instinct.com/cqdjrulv2lbk-cpi-day-and-the-rupees-first\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">CPI day and the rupee's first test</a>."}]}], "hero": {"alt": "The Bombay Stock Exchange building in Mumbai", "caption": "The Bombay Stock Exchange, Mumbai, where PhonePe intends to list by February-March 2027. Photo: Niyantha Shekhar (Wikimedia Commons, CC BY 2.0).", "img": "photos/d2-g1.jpg"}}}, "concept": {"label": "One concept a day", "name": "Anchoring", "line": "The first number you hear quietly sets every number after it. Yesterday's CPI poll said 5.6% - watch how today's coverage treats anything near it as “expected”."}, "audio": {"1": null, "2": null, "3": null, "4": null, "5": null, "6": null, "7": null}};
